(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.9a3dfe0def.js"]={"11":[{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Step-by-step O2/CO2 transport and exchange — atmosphere to body tissues","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning about gas transport from atmosphere to tissues. Which sequence correctly describes the complete pathway of oxygen from atmospheric air to mitochondria?","choices":{"A":"Atmospheric air → humidified air → expired air → alveolar air → pulmonary capillary","B":"Atmospheric air → bronchial air → humidified air → alveolar air → pulmonary capillary","C":"Atmospheric air → humidified air → alveolar air → pulmonary capillary → tissue capillary","D":"Atmospheric air → alveolar air → humidified air → pulmonary capillary → arterial blood","E":"Atmospheric air → humidified air → bronchial air → expired air → tissue capillary"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Oxygen follows a specific pathway from atmosphere to tissues: (1) atmospheric air enters the respiratory passages, (2) becomes humidified in the airways (reducing PO2 from 159 to 149 mm Hg due to water vapor pressure of 47 mm Hg), (3) reaches the alveoli where PO2 is ~104 mm Hg, (4) diffuses across the respiratory membrane into pulmonary capillary blood, (5) is transported in arterial blood, and (6) diffuses from tissue capillaries into cells where it reaches mitochondria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Along O2's path the PO2 falls in steps: about 159 mm Hg in dry air, 149 after humidification, 104 in the alveoli, about 95 in systemic arterial blood after venous admixture, and about 40 in the tissue interstitium. COMLEX: First Aid's hypoxemia causes map onto these steps: low inspired PO2 (altitude), hypoventilation, diffusion limitation, V/Q mismatch and shunt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — classification and causes of hypoxia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Step-by-step O2/CO2 transport and exchange — atmosphere to body tissues","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains to a patient how carbon dioxide is eliminated from the body. Starting from tissue cells, what is the correct sequence for CO2 removal?","choices":{"A":"Tissue cells → interstitial fluid → arterial blood → alveoli → pulmonary capillary → expired","B":"Tissue cells → arterial blood → pulmonary capillary → alveoli → humidified air → expired","C":"Tissue cells → venous blood → tissue capillary → humidified air → alveoli → expired","D":"Tissue cells → tissue capillary → arterial blood → alveoli → pulmonary capillary → expired","E":"Tissue cells → tissue capillary → venous blood → pulmonary capillary → alveoli → expired"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carbon dioxide follows the reverse pathway of oxygen: (1) CO2 is produced in tissue cell mitochondria as a metabolic byproduct, (2) diffuses into tissue capillaries where PCO2 is ~45 mm Hg, (3) travels in venous blood to the lungs, (4) diffuses from pulmonary capillaries into alveoli where alveolar PCO2 is ~40 mm Hg, and (5) is expelled in expired air. The concentration gradient drives CO2 from tissues (high PCO2) to alveoli (lower PCO2) to atmosphere (essentially 0 mm Hg).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 travels the reverse path: produced in cells (intracellular PCO2 about 46 mm Hg), it diffuses to the interstitium (45) and venous blood, then from pulmonary capillary blood into the alveoli (40) and out with expiration. COMLEX: First Aid notes about 90% of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate formed in red cells by carbonic anhydrase, about 5% as carbamino compounds and about 5% dissolved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — CO2 transport via carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate and carbaminohemoglobin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Step-by-step O2/CO2 transport and exchange — atmosphere to body tissues","batch":1,"stem":"During exercise, oxygen consumption increases from 250 mL/min to 1000 mL/min. To maintain normal alveolar PO2 of 104 mm Hg, alveolar ventilation must change. How must ventilation adjust?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar ventilation must remain constant at resting values throughout exercise","B":"Alveolar ventilation must decrease by half to conserve oxygen","C":"Alveolar ventilation must increase 4-fold to maintain normal alveolar PO2","D":"Alveolar ventilation must triple to prevent hypoxia during exercise","E":"Alveolar ventilation must increase by exactly 250 mL/min only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The alveolar PO2 is controlled by two factors: rate of O2 absorption into blood and rate of new O2 entry via ventilation. When O2 absorption increases 4-fold (from 250 to 1000 mL/min) during exercise, alveolar ventilation must also increase approximately 4-fold to maintain normal alveolar PO2 at 104 mm Hg. This prevents alveolar PO2 from falling as O2 is absorbed more rapidly. The increased ventilation brings fresh O2 into alveoli at a rate matching the increased removal rate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar PO2 is set by the balance between O2 absorption into the blood and O2 delivery by ventilation; at 4.2 L/min and 250 mL/min uptake it is 104 mm Hg, and a 4-fold rise in uptake needs a 4-fold rise in alveolar ventilation to hold it there. COMLEX: However hard a person breathes room air at sea level, alveolar PO2 can never exceed 149 mm Hg, the PO2 of humidified air.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 11 — chemical and nervous factors that raise ventilation during exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, N2; direction of gas movement","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory physiologist measures gas partial pressures across the alveolar-capillary interface. For oxygen, what is the normal partial pressure gradient driving O2 diffusion into pulmonary capillary blood?","choices":{"A":"From alveolar PO2 of 149 mm Hg to venous PO2 of 104 mm Hg","B":"From alveolar PO2 of 149 mm Hg to arterial PO2 of 40 mm Hg","C":"From alveolar PO2 of 160 mm Hg to venous PO2 of 100 mm Hg","D":"From alveolar PO2 of 40 mm Hg to venous PO2 of 104 mm Hg","E":"From alveolar PO2 of 104 mm Hg to venous PO2 of 40 mm Hg"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The partial pressure gradient for O2 across the respiratory membrane drives diffusion from alveoli (high PO2) into blood (low PO2). Normal alveolar PO2 is approximately 104 mm Hg, while mixed venous blood entering pulmonary capillaries has PO2 of approximately 40 mm Hg. This 64 mm Hg gradient (104 - 40) provides the driving force for O2 diffusion. Blood leaving pulmonary capillaries equilibrates to approximately 104 mm Hg, matching alveolar PO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 diffuses from alveolar air (PO2 about 104 mm Hg) into pulmonary capillary blood arriving from the veins (PO2 about 40 mm Hg); net diffusion runs from high to low partial pressure. COMLEX: First Aid's alveolar gas equation, PAO2 = PIO2 − PaCO2/RQ (about 150 − PaCO2/0.8 at sea level), predicts alveolar PO2 at the bedside, and the A–a gradient compares it with arterial PO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — O2 vs. CO2 diffusion: solubility vs. pressure gradient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, N2; direction of gas movement","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student studies CO2 exchange at the pulmonary capillaries. What partial pressure gradient drives CO2 diffusion from blood into alveoli under normal resting conditions?","choices":{"A":"From venous PCO2 of 40 mm Hg to alveolar PCO2 of 45 mm Hg","B":"From venous PCO2 of 45 mm Hg to alveolar PCO2 of 40 mm Hg","C":"From arterial PCO2 of 35 mm Hg to alveolar PCO2 of 45 mm Hg","D":"From arterial PCO2 of 45 mm Hg to alveolar PCO2 of 35 mm Hg","E":"From venous PCO2 of 50 mm Hg to alveolar PCO2 of 35 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The partial pressure gradient for CO2 is reversed compared to O2, driving diffusion from blood (high PCO2) into alveoli (lower PCO2). Normal mixed venous blood has PCO2 of approximately 45 mm Hg, while alveolar PCO2 is approximately 40 mm Hg. This small 5 mm Hg gradient is sufficient because CO2 diffuses about 20 times more rapidly than O2 due to its much higher solubility (diffusion coefficient 20.3 vs 1.0).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 moves from mixed venous blood (PCO2 about 45 mm Hg) into alveoli (about 40 mm Hg); the gradient is only about 5 mm Hg, but CO2's diffusion coefficient is about 20 times that of O2. COMLEX: Guyton notes the blood–alveolar PCO2 difference at the end of the capillary is less than 1 mm Hg, which is why a CO2 diffusing capacity has never been measured.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest and in exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, N2; direction of gas movement","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher compares the direction of net diffusion for different respiratory gases. Which statement correctly describes the normal direction of net gas movement across the alveolar membrane?","choices":{"A":"O2 and CO2 both diffuse from alveoli into blood normally","B":"O2 and N2 both diffuse from blood into alveoli normally","C":"CO2 and N2 both diffuse from alveoli into blood normally","D":"O2 diffuses from alveoli to blood; CO2 diffuses from blood to alveoli","E":"CO2 diffuses from alveoli to blood; O2 diffuses from blood to alveoli"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under normal conditions, net diffusion direction is determined by partial pressure gradients. O2 has higher partial pressure in alveoli (104 mm Hg) than in venous blood (40 mm Hg), so O2 diffuses INTO the blood. CO2 has higher partial pressure in venous blood (45 mm Hg) than in alveoli (40 mm Hg), so CO2 diffuses OUT OF the blood into alveoli. N2 is essentially inert and equilibrates without significant net movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Net diffusion follows partial pressure: alveolar PO2 (104) exceeds venous PO2 (40), so O2 enters blood, while venous PCO2 (45) exceeds alveolar PCO2 (40), so CO2 leaves it; nitrogen, neither used nor produced, shows no net exchange. COMLEX: First Aid notes CO2 and N2O are perfusion-limited, equilibrating early along the capillary, so their uptake rises only if blood flow rises.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — partial pressure gradients between alveoli, blood and tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henry's law; surface area and membrane thickness factors on gas diffusion rate","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns that CO2 is much more soluble than O2 in body fluids. According to Henry's Law, how does CO2's solubility coefficient compare to that of O2?","choices":{"A":"CO2 solubility coefficient (0.024) equals O2 solubility coefficient (0.024) exactly","B":"CO2 solubility coefficient (0.012) is half that of O2 solubility coefficient (0.024)","C":"CO2 solubility coefficient (0.57) is approximately 24 times that of O2 (0.024)","D":"CO2 solubility coefficient (20.3) is 20 times that of O2 solubility coefficient (1.0)","E":"CO2 solubility coefficient (1.0) equals O2 solubility coefficient (1.0) at body temperature"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Henry's Law states that partial pressure = concentration/solubility coefficient. At body temperature, the solubility coefficient for CO2 is 0.57, while for O2 it is 0.024. This means CO2 is approximately 24 times more soluble than O2 in body fluids (0.57/0.024 ≈ 24). This high solubility means CO2 exerts lower partial pressure for a given concentration, and CO2 diffuses much more readily through aqueous solutions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Henry's law: partial pressure = dissolved concentration / solubility coefficient; at body temperature the coefficients are O2 0.024, CO2 0.57, CO 0.018, N2 0.012 and He 0.008, so CO2 is more than 20 times as soluble as O2. COMLEX: Because CO2 is so soluble, Guyton notes its partial pressure for a given concentration is less than one-twentieth that of O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — solubility vs. gradient in O2 and CO2 diffusion rates","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henry's law; surface area and membrane thickness factors on gas diffusion rate","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist evaluates a patient with pulmonary edema. Which factor affecting gas diffusion rate is MOST directly compromised by fluid accumulation in the interstitial space?","choices":{"A":"Membrane thickness increases, and diffusion rate falls in inverse proportion","B":"Molecular weight of gases increases, slowing kinetic movement significantly","C":"Solubility coefficients decrease, preventing gas dissolution in fluid","D":"Cross-sectional area increases, enhancing overall diffusion rate substantially","E":"Partial pressure gradients reverse, causing backward gas movement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The rate of gas diffusion is expressed as: D ∝ (ΔP × A × S)/(d × √MW). When edema fluid accumulates, the thickness (d) of the respiratory membrane increases because gases must diffuse through both the membrane AND the edema fluid. Since diffusion rate is inversely proportional to distance, doubling the thickness halves the diffusion rate. When thickness increases 2-3 times normal, gas exchange becomes significantly impaired. The gradients do not reverse in edema: alveolar PO2 stays above capillary PO2, so O2 still moves into the blood, only more slowly across the thicker barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusion rate ∝ ΔP × area × solubility / (distance × √MW); edema fluid in the interstitium and alveoli adds to the distance, and thickening to more than 2–3 times normal significantly interferes with gas exchange. COMLEX: First Aid lists pulmonary edema among diseases that lower compliance, and Robbins notes edema makes the lungs heavy and wet.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 13 — interstitial fluid dynamics and pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henry's law; surface area and membrane thickness factors on gas diffusion rate","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher calculates the diffusion coefficient for various respiratory gases. The diffusion coefficient is proportional to S/√MW. How does CO2's diffusion coefficient compare to O2's?","choices":{"A":"CO2 diffusion coefficient (0.81) is slightly less than O2 diffusion coefficient (1.0)","B":"CO2 diffusion coefficient (0.53) is approximately half of O2 diffusion coefficient (1.0)","C":"CO2 diffusion coefficient (0.95) is nearly equal to O2 diffusion coefficient (1.0)","D":"CO2 diffusion coefficient (20.3) is approximately 20 times that of O2 (1.0)","E":"CO2 diffusion coefficient (24) is 24 times greater than O2 diffusion coefficient (1.0)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"If O2 diffusion coefficient is assigned a value of 1.0, the CO2 diffusion coefficient is 20.3. This is calculated from S/√MW where S is solubility and MW is molecular weight. CO2's much higher solubility (0.57 vs 0.024) more than compensates for its slightly higher molecular weight (44 vs 32). This 20-fold greater diffusion coefficient means CO2 diffuses through the respiratory membrane about 20 times faster than O2 for the same partial pressure gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A gas's diffusion coefficient is proportional to solubility / √molecular weight; taking O2 as 1.0, CO2 is 20.3, CO 0.81, N2 0.53 and He 0.95. COMLEX: CO2's much higher coefficient is why First Aid notes diffusion-limiting diseases such as fibrosis cause hypoxemia earlier than hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 11 — the carbon monoxide method and the O2/CO conversion factor","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Composition and partial pressures of O2/CO2 — atmospheric air vs. alveolar air","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student compares gas compositions. What is the primary reason alveolar PO2 (104 mm Hg) is significantly lower than humidified atmospheric air PO2 (149 mm Hg)?","choices":{"A":"Nitrogen displaces oxygen in alveoli, reducing PO2 to 104 mm Hg","B":"Water vapor dilutes all gases, reducing PO2 from 159 to 104 mm Hg","C":"CO2 excretion into alveoli increases total pressure above 760 mm Hg","D":"Oxygen is continuously absorbed into blood, lowering alveolar PO2","E":"Alveolar ventilation rate exceeds oxygen absorption rate into blood"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Alveolar PO2 (104 mm Hg) is lower than humidified inspired air PO2 (149 mm Hg) primarily because oxygen is continuously absorbed from alveoli into pulmonary blood. The alveolar PO2 represents an equilibrium between two processes: (1) O2 removal by blood absorption, and (2) O2 replenishment by ventilation. At normal ventilation (4.2 L/min) and normal O2 consumption (250 mL/min), steady-state alveolar PO2 settles at 104 mm Hg. Water vapor accounts only for the fall from 159 mm Hg in dry air to 149 mm Hg in humidified air, not for the further drop to 104 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar air differs from atmospheric air because only part is replaced each breath, O2 is constantly absorbed, CO2 constantly added and inspired air humidified; so alveolar PO2 (104) sits below humidified-air PO2 (149). COMLEX: First Aid's alveolar gas equation captures the CO2 term: PAO2 = PIO2 − PaCO2/RQ, so retained CO2 (hypoventilation) lowers alveolar PO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — when hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Composition and partial pressures of O2/CO2 — atmospheric air vs. alveolar air","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains why alveolar air contains significant CO2 while atmospheric air has essentially none. What is the normal alveolar PCO2 compared to atmospheric PCO2?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PCO2 (0 mm Hg) equals atmospheric PCO2 (0 mm Hg)","B":"Alveolar PCO2 (47 mm Hg) matches water vapor pressure in airways","C":"Alveolar PCO2 (40 mm Hg) while atmospheric PCO2 is 0.3 mm Hg","D":"Alveolar PCO2 (45 mm Hg) equals venous blood PCO2 (45 mm Hg)","E":"Alveolar PCO2 (104 mm Hg) is very high due to CO2 production"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atmospheric air contains only approximately 0.04% CO2 with a partial pressure of about 0.3 mm Hg. In contrast, alveolar air has a PCO2 of approximately 40 mm Hg because CO2 is continuously diffusing from pulmonary capillary blood into the alveoli. This equilibrium is determined by the rate of CO2 excretion from blood (200 mL/min at rest) and the rate of alveolar ventilation removing CO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atmospheric air contains about 0.04% CO2 (PCO2 about 0.3 mm Hg), whereas alveolar air has about 40 mm Hg (5.3%) because CO2 constantly diffuses in from the blood and is only partly washed out. COMLEX: Alveolar PCO2 varies inversely with alveolar ventilation, so in First Aid's hypoventilation examples (opiate overdose, obesity hypoventilation, neuromuscular weakness) PaCO2 rises.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 8 — how arterial CO2 acts on central chemoreceptors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Composition and partial pressures of O2/CO2 — atmospheric air vs. alveolar air","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory physiologist measures the percentage composition of alveolar air. Compared to atmospheric air, what percentage of alveolar air is oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar air contains 21% O2, identical to atmospheric air composition","B":"Alveolar air contains 20% O2, slightly less than atmospheric air","C":"Alveolar air contains 19.67% O2, due to humidification effects only","D":"Alveolar air contains 6.2% O2, very low due to absorption","E":"Alveolar air contains 13.6% O2, much lower than atmospheric 20.84%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Atmospheric air contains 20.84% O2 (PO2 = 159 mm Hg), while alveolar air contains only approximately 13.6% O2 (PO2 = 104 mm Hg). This reduction occurs because: (1) humidification dilutes all gases (water vapor = 6.2%), (2) O2 is continuously absorbed into blood, and (3) CO2 is continuously added to alveolar air (5.3%). The combined effect substantially reduces the percentage of O2 in alveolar air compared to atmospheric air.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's table gives O2 as 20.84% of atmospheric air (159 mm Hg), 19.67% of humidified air (149) and 13.6% of alveolar air (104); water vapor makes up 6.2% (47 mm Hg) of alveolar gas. COMLEX: First Aid writes PIO2 = FIO2 × (PB − PH2O), which is why low barometric pressure at altitude lowers inspired and alveolar PO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — respiratory and renal acclimatization to high altitude","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlation between alveolar CO2 concentration/partial pressure and physiological significance","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns that alveolar PCO2 increases directly with CO2 production rate. If tissue CO2 production quadruples from 200 to 800 mL/min while ventilation stays constant, how does alveolar PCO2 change?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PCO2 quadruples from normal 40 mm Hg to 160 mm Hg","B":"Alveolar PCO2 remains constant due to buffering capacity","C":"Alveolar PCO2 doubles from normal 40 mm Hg to 80 mm Hg","D":"Alveolar PCO2 triples from normal 40 mm Hg to 120 mm Hg","E":"Alveolar PCO2 decreases inversely with production rate changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alveolar PCO2 is directly proportional to the rate of CO2 excretion (which equals CO2 production in steady state). When CO2 production/excretion increases 4-fold from 200 to 800 mL/min while alveolar ventilation remains constant at 4.2 L/min, the alveolar PCO2 must also increase 4-fold. Normal alveolar PCO2 is 40 mm Hg, so it would rise to approximately 160 mm Hg. This represents severe hypercapnia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At a fixed alveolar ventilation, alveolar PCO2 rises in direct proportion to CO2 production and excretion; Guyton's 800 mL/min curve sits 4 times higher than the normal 200 mL/min curve. COMLEX: At steady state CO2 excretion equals production, so a hypermetabolic state with no ventilatory reserve produces hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — how O2, CO2 and H+ control respiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlation between alveolar CO2 concentration/partial pressure and physiological significance","batch":1,"stem":"A patient hyperventilates, increasing alveolar ventilation from 4.2 L/min to 8.4 L/min while CO2 production remains constant at 200 mL/min. What happens to alveolar PCO2?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PCO2 increases proportionally to the ventilation increase","B":"Alveolar PCO2 remains constant despite ventilation changes completely","C":"Alveolar PCO2 decreases by approximately half from 40 to 20 mm Hg","D":"Alveolar PCO2 increases slightly due to respiratory alkalosis development","E":"Alveolar PCO2 triples due to enhanced CO2 excretion rates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Alveolar PCO2 is inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation when CO2 production is constant. Doubling alveolar ventilation from 4.2 to 8.4 L/min while maintaining constant CO2 production (200 mL/min) causes alveolar PCO2 to decrease by half, from normal 40 mm Hg to approximately 20 mm Hg. This represents hyperventilation-induced hypocapnia, which causes respiratory alkalosis. This relationship is why alveolar ventilation is the primary controller of blood PCO2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With CO2 production constant, alveolar PCO2 falls in inverse proportion to alveolar ventilation, so doubling ventilation roughly halves alveolar PCO2. COMLEX: First Aid's high-altitude response shows the clinical version: hypoxic hyperventilation lowers PaCO2 and causes respiratory alkalosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 9 — acclimatization and the ventilatory response to hypoxia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlation between alveolar CO2 concentration/partial pressure and physiological significance","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains to a student why measuring alveolar PCO2 directly reflects CO2 concentration in alveolar air. What principle explains this direct correlation?","choices":{"A":"The partial pressure equals concentration divided by solubility (Henry's law)","B":"The partial pressure equals concentration multiplied by molecular weight value","C":"The partial pressure equals concentration minus water vapor pressure (47 mm Hg)","D":"The partial pressure equals concentration plus atmospheric pressure (760 mm Hg)","E":"The partial pressure inversely relates to concentration per Boyle's Law"},"correct":"A","explanation":"According to Henry's Law, partial pressure of a dissolved or gaseous substance equals the concentration divided by the solubility coefficient (Partial pressure = Concentration/Solubility coefficient). For gases in the alveoli, this means PCO2 is directly proportional to CO2 concentration. The solubility coefficient for CO2 at body temperature (0.57) is constant, so any change in CO2 concentration produces a proportional change in PCO2. This direct relationship makes PCO2 measurement a reliable indicator of alveolar CO2 concentration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Partial pressure is directly proportional to the concentration of gas molecules, and for a dissolved gas Henry's law divides concentration by the solubility coefficient; in the alveolar gas phase, PCO2 therefore tracks CO2 percentage (40 mm Hg = 5.3%). COMLEX: The last portion of a forceful expiration is pure alveolar air, which is why end-expiratory gas is used to sample alveolar PCO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 8 — arterial CO2 and central chemoreceptors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Structure (layers, cells) and function of the respiratory membrane","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student examines the ultrastructure of the respiratory membrane. How many distinct layers must O2 traverse to diffuse from alveolar air into red blood cell hemoglobin?","choices":{"A":"Six layers: fluid, epithelium, epithelial BM, interstitium, capillary BM, endothelium","B":"Three layers: alveolar epithelium, interstitial space, capillary endothelium only","C":"Four layers: surfactant fluid, alveolar epithelium, interstitium, capillary endothelium","D":"Two layers: alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium fused together","E":"Five layers: epithelium, two basement membranes, interstitium, endothelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The respiratory membrane consists of six distinct layers that gases must traverse: (1) surfactant-containing fluid layer lining the alveolus, (2) alveolar epithelium (thin epithelial cells), (3) epithelial basement membrane, (4) thin interstitial space, (5) capillary basement membrane (often fused with epithelial basement membrane), and (6) capillary endothelial membrane. Despite these multiple layers, total thickness averages only 0.6 micrometers, enabling rapid gas diffusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The respiratory membrane has six layers: surfactant-containing fluid, alveolar epithelium, epithelial basement membrane, thin interstitial space, capillary basement membrane (often fused with the epithelial one) and capillary endothelium. COMLEX: First Aid's diffusion equation puts membrane thickness in the denominator, so fibrosis that thickens the interstitium lowers DLCO.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type I and type II alveolar cells of the alveolar wall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Structure (layers, cells) and function of the respiratory membrane","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory physiologist measures the total surface area of the respiratory membrane in a healthy adult man. What is the approximate total surface area available for gas exchange?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 10 square meters, equivalent to a small bedroom floor","B":"Approximately 25 square meters, equivalent to a large living room","C":"Approximately 50 square meters, equivalent to a small apartment floor","D":"Approximately 70 square meters, equivalent to a 25×30 foot room floor","E":"Approximately 100 square meters, equivalent to a tennis court surface"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The total surface area of the respiratory membrane is approximately 70 square meters in healthy men, which is equivalent to the floor area of a 25 × 30-foot room. This enormous surface area, combined with the extremely thin membrane (averaging 0.6 micrometers), allows for rapid gas exchange. The total blood volume in pulmonary capillaries at any instant is only 60-140 mL, spread over this vast surface area like a thin sheet. Pawlina (Ch 19, the course histology text) gives a slightly larger figure, about 75 m², roughly the size of a tennis court.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The respiratory membrane totals about 70 m² in healthy men (a 25 × 30-ft floor), across roughly 300 million alveoli, yet only 60–140 mL of capillary blood is spread over it at any instant. COMLEX: Guyton notes that removing a whole lung halves this area, and that exchange is substantially impeded at rest once the area falls to about one-third to one-fourth normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — postnatal formation of most of the adult alveoli","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Structure (layers, cells) and function of the respiratory membrane","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains why the respiratory membrane is so effective for gas exchange. What is the average thickness of the respiratory membrane where gas exchange occurs?","choices":{"A":"The membrane averages 10 micrometers thick, allowing moderate gas exchange","B":"The membrane averages 0.6 micrometers thick, enabling extremely rapid diffusion","C":"The membrane averages 2 micrometers thick, providing adequate gas exchange","D":"The membrane averages 5 micrometers thick, permitting sufficient diffusion","E":"The membrane averages 20 micrometers thick, maintaining structural integrity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The respiratory membrane averages approximately 0.6 micrometers in thickness and can be as thin as 0.2 micrometers in some areas (except where cell nuclei are located). This extremely thin barrier, combined with the large surface area (70 m²), allows rapid gas diffusion. Since diffusion rate is inversely proportional to membrane thickness, this minimal distance is critical for efficient O2 and CO2 exchange, particularly during exercise when capillary transit time shortens to 0.3 seconds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The respiratory membrane averages about 0.6 µm thick and is as thin as 0.2 µm in places, and pulmonary capillaries (about 5 µm) are so narrow that red cells touch the wall, so O2 and CO2 cross little plasma. COMLEX: Guyton warns that any process thickening the membrane to more than 2–3 times normal, such as edema or fibrosis, significantly interferes with gas exchange.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — interstitial fibrosis and other restrictive diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q19_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Disease states (emphysema, pulmonary edema, fibrosis) effect on gas diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old patient with emphysema has reduced lung diffusing capacity. Which pathophysiological change in emphysema MOST directly decreases the surface area available for gas exchange?","choices":{"A":"Bronchial smooth muscle constriction narrows airways, reducing airflow","B":"Mucus hypersecretion blocks airways, preventing ventilation completely","C":"Alveolar walls break down and merge into larger, lower-area spaces","D":"Pulmonary capillaries proliferate, increasing blood flow excessively","E":"Surfactant production increases, thickening the alveolar fluid layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In emphysema, proteolytic destruction of alveolar walls causes many alveoli to coalesce into larger chambers. Although the new chambers are bigger than original alveoli, the total surface area of the respiratory membrane decreases dramatically (often to 1/5 normal or less) because many alveolar walls are lost. Since gas exchange rate is directly proportional to surface area, this reduction can decrease diffusing capacity to only 20% of normal, severely impairing gas exchange even at rest and especially during exercise. Bronchoconstriction and mucus plugging narrow or block the airways and affect ventilation, not membrane area, and capillaries are lost along with the alveolar walls rather than proliferating.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In emphysema many alveoli coalesce as walls dissolve, so the new chambers are larger but total membrane area often falls as much as 5-fold. COMLEX: First Aid lists the resulting findings: increased compliance, reduced recoil, low DLCO, and on x-ray a barrel chest with flattened diaphragms and increased lucency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four main types of emphysema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q20_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Disease states (emphysema, pulmonary edema, fibrosis) effect on gas diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with pulmonary fibrosis develops thickened respiratory membranes. According to diffusion principles, how does doubling the membrane thickness affect the rate of O2 diffusion?","choices":{"A":"Doubling thickness increases diffusion rate by exactly 2-fold proportionally","B":"Doubling thickness has no effect on diffusion rate through membranes","C":"Doubling thickness increases diffusion rate by approximately 4-fold","D":"Doubling thickness decreases diffusion rate by approximately half","E":"Doubling thickness increases diffusion resistance but not diffusion rate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"According to Fick's law of diffusion, the rate of gas diffusion is inversely proportional to membrane thickness (D ∝ 1/d). Therefore, doubling the thickness (d) of the respiratory membrane halves the diffusion rate. In pulmonary fibrosis, scar tissue increases membrane thickness by 2-3 times normal or more. When membrane thickness increases to more than 2-3 times normal, it significantly interferes with normal gas exchange, particularly during exercise when less time is available for equilibration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusion rate is inversely proportional to membrane thickness, so doubling the thickness halves the rate of O2 diffusion at the same gradient; fibrosis can thicken parts of the membrane. COMLEX: First Aid classes interstitial lung diseases as restrictive with a reduced DLCO and an increased A–a gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other interstitial lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q21_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Disease states (emphysema, pulmonary edema, fibrosis) effect on gas diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops pulmonary edema with fluid accumulation in alveoli. How does alveolar edema fluid affect O2 diffusion compared to normal conditions?","choices":{"A":"Edema fluid has no effect because O2 diffuses equally through all fluids","B":"Edema fluid enhances diffusion by providing additional surface area contact","C":"Edema fluid impairs diffusion by increasing the effective distance gases must traverse","D":"Edema fluid accelerates diffusion by increasing the partial pressure gradient","E":"Edema fluid prevents diffusion by blocking all alveolar-capillary contact completely"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When edema fluid accumulates in the interstitial space and alveoli, gases must diffuse not only through the normal respiratory membrane but also through the edema fluid. This increases the effective thickness of the diffusion barrier. Since diffusion rate is inversely proportional to distance (D ∝ 1/d), increasing the diffusion distance by adding fluid significantly slows gas exchange. Severe pulmonary edema can increase the effective membrane thickness several-fold, critically impairing oxygenation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With edema in the interstitium and alveoli, O2 must diffuse through the membrane and the fluid, increasing the effective distance and lowering the diffusion rate. COMLEX: First Aid's hypoxemia scheme places edema under V/Q mismatch (normal perfusion of poorly ventilated areas) with an increased A–a gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — cardiogenic vs. noncardiogenic pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q22_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Step-by-step O2/CO2 transport and exchange — atmosphere to body tissues","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old patient asks their physician to explain how breathing provides oxygen to their heart muscle. Which statement correctly describes the complete oxygen delivery pathway to cardiac myocytes?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar air → pulmonary artery → right atrium → aorta → coronary arteries → myocytes","B":"Atmospheric air → bronchi → pulmonary capillary → coronary sinus → cardiac myocytes","C":"Alveoli → pulmonary capillary → left heart → coronary artery → cardiac myocytes","D":"Humidified air → pulmonary artery → left ventricle → coronary veins → cardiac myocytes","E":"Atmospheric air → trachea → pulmonary vein → coronary arteries → cardiac myocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Oxygen delivery to cardiac myocytes follows this pathway: (1) atmospheric air is inspired and humidified in airways, (2) reaches alveoli where PO2 is ~104 mm Hg, (3) diffuses across respiratory membrane into pulmonary capillary blood, (4) oxygenated blood travels through pulmonary veins to left atrium and ventricle, (5) is ejected into aorta and coronary arteries, and (6) diffuses from cardiac capillaries into cardiac myocytes. The coronary circulation is the first branch off the aorta. The other sequences send blood through the wrong vessels or chambers (pulmonary artery, right atrium, coronary veins or sinus) or skip the pulmonary capillary, where O2 actually enters the blood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 reaches the heart muscle by the systemic route: alveoli, pulmonary capillaries, pulmonary veins, left heart and aorta, then the coronary arteries; arterial PO2 is about 95 mm Hg after venous admixture. COMLEX: At PO2 95 only about 0.29 mL O2 is dissolved per 100 mL of blood water, so nearly all the O2 delivered to the myocardium rides on hemoglobin, about 97% saturated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — O2 transport from alveoli to tissue capillaries","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q23_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Step-by-step O2/CO2 transport and exchange — atmosphere to body tissues","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns that alveolar air is only partially replaced with each breath. With a functional residual capacity of 2300 mL and about 350 mL of new air reaching the alveoli with each normal breath, what fraction of alveolar air is replaced per breath?","choices":{"A":"Approximately one-seventh of total alveolar air is replaced with each normal breath","B":"Approximately one-half of total alveolar air is replaced with each normal breath","C":"Approximately one-third of total alveolar air is replaced with each normal breath","D":"Approximately one-quarter of total alveolar air is replaced with each normal breath","E":"Approximately two-thirds of total alveolar air is replaced with each normal breath"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At the end of normal expiration, the functional residual capacity (FRC) is approximately 2300 mL. With each normal inspiration, only 350 mL of new air enters the alveoli, and the same amount is expired. Therefore, only 350/2300 or approximately 1/7 of the alveolar air is replaced with each breath. This slow replacement rate helps stabilize blood gas concentrations and prevents sudden changes in O2 and CO2 levels, making the respiratory control system more stable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With an FRC of about 2300 mL and only about 350 mL of new air reaching the alveoli each breath, roughly one-seventh of alveolar air is replaced per breath, so many breaths are needed to exchange it. COMLEX: Guyton notes this slow renewal stabilizes respiratory control and prevents sudden swings in tissue O2, CO2 and pH when breathing is briefly interrupted.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 10 — functional residual capacity and other lung capacities","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q24_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Step-by-step O2/CO2 transport and exchange — atmosphere to body tissues","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with normal alveolar ventilation (4.2 L/min) at rest has half the gas removed from their alveoli in approximately 17 seconds. If alveolar ventilation doubles to 8.4 L/min, how long does it take to remove half the gas?","choices":{"A":"Half the gas is removed in 34 seconds with doubled ventilation","B":"Half the gas is removed in 17 seconds, unchanged from the baseline rate","C":"Half the gas is removed in about 8 seconds with doubled ventilation","D":"Half the gas is removed in 25 seconds with doubled ventilation","E":"Half the gas is removed in 4 seconds with doubled ventilation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The time to remove half of excess gas from alveoli is inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation rate. At normal ventilation (4.2 L/min), half-time is approximately 17 seconds. When ventilation doubles to 8.4 L/min, the half-time is cut in half to approximately 8 seconds. Conversely, if ventilation were halved (2.1 L/min), the half-time would double to 34 seconds. This relationship demonstrates how ventilation rate directly affects the speed of alveolar gas equilibration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With normal alveolar ventilation about half of an excess gas leaves the alveoli in 17 seconds; at half-normal ventilation it takes 34 seconds, and at twice-normal ventilation about 8 seconds. COMLEX: The same slow washout buffers blood gases during a short apnea, which Guyton cites as a stabilizer of respiratory control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — alveolar ventilation and systemic homeostasis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q25_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, N2; direction of gas movement","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies gas exchange at tissue capillaries. What is the normal partial pressure gradient that drives oxygen diffusion from systemic capillaries into tissue cells?","choices":{"A":"From capillary PO2 of 104 mm Hg to tissue PO2 of 40 mm Hg","B":"From capillary PO2 of 95 mm Hg to tissue PO2 of 40 mm Hg or less","C":"From capillary PO2 of 40 mm Hg to tissue PO2 of 104 mm Hg","D":"From capillary PO2 of 159 mm Hg to tissue PO2 of 95 mm Hg","E":"From capillary PO2 of 149 mm Hg to tissue PO2 of 104 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At systemic tissue capillaries, arterial blood arrives with PO2 of approximately 95 mm Hg (slightly less than alveolar PO2 of 104 mm Hg due to venous admixture). Tissue cells continuously consume oxygen for metabolism, maintaining tissue PO2 at approximately 40 mm Hg or even lower during high metabolic activity. This creates a gradient of ~55 mm Hg (95 - 40) that drives O2 diffusion from capillaries into tissues. The steep gradient ensures adequate oxygen delivery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arterial blood reaches the tissue capillaries at PO2 about 95 mm Hg while interstitial PO2 averages about 40 mm Hg (intracellular about 23 mm Hg), so O2 diffuses out quickly; cells need only 1–3 mm Hg to run their oxidative reactions. COMLEX: Raising tissue blood flow raises interstitial PO2, but never above the 95 mm Hg of arterial blood.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — blood flow and tissue oxygenation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q26_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, N2; direction of gas movement","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks about carbon dioxide gradients at tissue capillaries. What partial pressure gradient drives CO2 diffusion from metabolically active tissues into capillary blood?","choices":{"A":"From capillary PCO2 of 45 mm Hg to tissue PCO2 of 40 mm Hg","B":"From capillary PCO2 of 35 mm Hg to tissue PCO2 of 45 mm Hg","C":"From tissue PCO2 of 46 mm Hg to capillary PCO2 of 40 mm Hg","D":"From capillary PCO2 of 40 mm Hg to tissue PCO2 of 35 mm Hg","E":"From tissue PCO2 of 40 mm Hg to capillary PCO2 of 45 mm Hg"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At tissue capillaries, arterial blood arrives with PCO2 of approximately 40 mm Hg. Tissue cells continuously produce CO2 through metabolism, creating tissue PCO2 of approximately 46 mm Hg or higher during intense metabolic activity. This 6 mm Hg gradient (46 - 40) drives CO2 diffusion from tissues into capillary blood. Although this gradient is small, it's sufficient because CO2 diffuses about 20 times faster than O2. Blood leaves tissue capillaries with PCO2 ~45 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intracellular PCO2 is about 46 mm Hg and interstitial PCO2 about 45 mm Hg, only a 1 mm Hg difference, yet enough because CO2 diffuses about 20 times as fast as O2; arterial blood enters tissue capillaries at about 40 mm Hg. COMLEX: First Aid's Haldane effect explains release in the lung: oxygenation of hemoglobin frees H+, shifting the equilibrium toward CO2 release from red cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q27_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2, N2; direction of gas movement","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains that nitrogen does not participate significantly in respiratory gas exchange despite being the most abundant gas in air. Why does nitrogen show minimal net diffusion across respiratory membranes?","choices":{"A":"Nitrogen is completely insoluble in blood and cannot dissolve","B":"Nitrogen has very high molecular weight, preventing any diffusion","C":"Nitrogen actively binds to hemoglobin, preventing free diffusion","D":"Nitrogen partial pressures equilibrate, eliminating any pressure gradient","E":"Nitrogen is consumed rapidly by tissues during cellular metabolism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nitrogen comprises approximately 79% of atmospheric air and remains at essentially the same concentration in alveolar air. Because nitrogen is metabolically inert (not consumed by tissues or produced by metabolism), its partial pressure in alveolar air (approximately 569 mm Hg) equilibrates with its partial pressure in blood. Without a partial pressure gradient, there is no driving force for net nitrogen diffusion. Nitrogen is somewhat soluble in blood (solubility coefficient 0.012), but the equilibrated pressures eliminate directional movement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nitrogen is about 75–79% of the gas mixture but is neither consumed nor produced, so its alveolar partial pressure (about 569 mm Hg) equilibrates with blood and there is no net exchange; it only changes when ambient pressure changes. COMLEX: First Aid notes that in ascending divers dissolved nitrogen comes out as bubbles (decompression sickness), treated with hyperbaric O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — barotrauma, decompression sickness and nitrogen narcosis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q28_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henry's law; surface area and membrane thickness factors on gas diffusion rate","batch":1,"stem":"A diver experiences decompression sickness due to nitrogen bubble formation. According to Henry's Law, what happens when a diver ascends rapidly from depth to surface?","choices":{"A":"Decreasing pressure increases nitrogen solubility, dissolving more nitrogen into tissues","B":"Decreasing pressure leaves tissues supersaturated with nitrogen, so bubbles form","C":"Increasing pressure forces nitrogen out of solution, creating gas bubbles","D":"Constant pressure maintains nitrogen equilibrium, preventing any bubble formation","E":"Decreasing temperature increases nitrogen solubility, preventing bubble formation completely"},"correct":"B","explanation":"According to Henry's Law (Partial pressure = Concentration/Solubility coefficient), the amount of gas dissolved in fluid is proportional to its partial pressure. At depth, high ambient pressure increases nitrogen partial pressure, dissolving more nitrogen in body tissues. During rapid ascent, ambient pressure drops quickly, reducing the partial pressure nitrogen can exert. The previously dissolved nitrogen becomes supersaturated and comes out of solution as bubbles, causing decompression sickness (the bends). Slow ascent allows gradual nitrogen elimination through lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: By Henry's law, the amount of gas dissolved rises with its partial pressure, so tissues load nitrogen at depth; on rapid ascent the pressure falls faster than nitrogen can leave, and bubbles form. COMLEX: First Aid calls these nitrogen-bubble air emboli caisson disease and lists hyperbaric O2 as treatment; the bends also appear among causes of avascular necrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric oxygen therapy: rationale and mechanism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q29_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henry's law; surface area and membrane thickness factors on gas diffusion rate","batch":1,"stem":"To diffuse from alveolar air into a red blood cell, an O₂ molecule must cross several cell membranes as well as thin layers of tissue fluid. Guyton states that diffusion of the respiratory gases through tissues, including the respiratory membrane, is almost equal to their diffusion in water. Which property of the respiratory gases explains why this is so?","choices":{"A":"They are highly lipid soluble, so membranes barely slow them","B":"They are actively carried across membranes by transporters","C":"They cross membranes through aquaporin water channels","D":"Their low molecular weight lets them bypass the lipid bilayer","E":"They diffuse exclusively through gaps between epithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton explains that the gases of respiratory importance are all highly soluble in lipids and therefore highly soluble in cell membranes, so the major limitation to their movement in tissues is the rate at which they diffuse through the tissue water, not through the membranes; diffusion through tissues, including the respiratory membrane, is therefore almost equal to diffusion in water, and the water diffusion coefficients (O₂ 1.0, CO₂ 20.3) apply. Gas exchange is simple diffusion down partial pressure differences, with no transporters. Aquaporins conduct water, and the gases do not need them because they dissolve directly in the lipid bilayer. Molecular weight affects velocity of kinetic movement (rate ∝ 1/√MW) but does not let a gas bypass the bilayer. Gas exchange occurs across the whole respiratory membrane, not only through intercellular gaps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Respiratory gases are highly lipid soluble, so cell membranes barely slow them; the main limit is diffusion through tissue water, which is why diffusion through the respiratory membrane nearly equals diffusion in water. COMLEX: That is why membrane thickening matters mainly by adding watery distance, as edema fluid and fibrous tissue do.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity and the factors that set it","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q30_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Henry's law; surface area and membrane thickness factors on gas diffusion rate","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher wants to measure how surface area affects gas diffusion through the respiratory membrane. If emphysema reduces total surface area from 70 m² to 14 m², how does this affect diffusion rate at constant pressure gradient?","choices":{"A":"Reducing surface area to one-fifth has no effect on diffusion","B":"Reducing surface area to one-fifth increases diffusion 5-fold","C":"Reducing surface area to one-fifth increases diffusion resistance only","D":"Reducing surface area to one-fifth decreases diffusion to one-fifth","E":"Reducing surface area to one-fifth decreases diffusion to one-tenth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffusion rate is directly proportional to cross-sectional area (D ∝ A). When emphysema destroys alveolar walls and reduces total respiratory membrane surface area from normal 70 m² to 14 m² (reducing to 1/5), the diffusion rate also decreases to 1/5 of normal at the same pressure gradient. This is why patients with severe emphysema become severely hypoxemic, especially during exercise when oxygen demand increases but the reduced surface area limits diffusion capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusion rate is directly proportional to membrane surface area, so a 5-fold loss of area (possible in emphysema) cuts diffusion 5-fold at the same gradient. COMLEX: Guyton notes exchange is substantially impeded even at rest once area falls to about one-third to one-fourth normal, and during strenuous exercise even a slight loss is a serious detriment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — emphysema pathophysiology leading to hypoxia and hypercapnia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q31_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Composition and partial pressures of O2/CO2 — atmospheric air vs. alveolar air","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student measures water vapor pressure in humidified inspired air at body temperature. What is the partial pressure of water vapor at 37°C, and how does it affect other gas pressures?","choices":{"A":"Water vapor pressure is 25 mm Hg, minimally affecting other gases","B":"Water vapor pressure is 10 mm Hg, slightly reducing other pressures","C":"Water vapor pressure is 60 mm Hg, greatly diluting all gases","D":"Water vapor pressure is 100 mm Hg, halving all other pressures","E":"Water vapor pressure is 47 mm Hg, diluting the other gas pressures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At normal body temperature of 37°C, water vapor pressure is 47 mm Hg. Since total pressure in airways cannot exceed atmospheric pressure (760 mm Hg at sea level), this water vapor dilutes all other gases proportionally. For example, atmospheric O2 partial pressure is 159 mm Hg (21% of 760), but in humidified air it drops to 149 mm Hg because water vapor occupies 47 mm Hg of the total 760 mm Hg, leaving only 713 mm Hg for other gases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Water vapor pressure depends only on temperature: 47 mm Hg at 37°C (5 mm Hg at 0°C, 760 at 100°C); because total alveolar pressure cannot exceed atmospheric, water vapor dilutes the other gases (O2 159 → 149 mm Hg). COMLEX: First Aid builds the 47 mm Hg into PIO2 = FIO2 × (PB − PH2O), the first term of the alveolar gas equation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — oxygen transport principles and hemoglobin saturation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q32_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Composition and partial pressures of O2/CO2 — atmospheric air vs. alveolar air","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains expired air composition to a patient. Expired air has intermediate composition between dead space air and alveolar air. What is the approximate PCO2 in mixed expired air?","choices":{"A":"Expired air PCO2 (0.3 mm Hg) matches the composition of atmospheric air","B":"Expired air PCO2 (47 mm Hg) equals water vapor pressure only","C":"Expired air PCO2 (27 mm Hg) lies between dead space and alveolar air","D":"Expired air PCO2 (45 mm Hg) equals venous blood PCO2 exactly","E":"Expired air PCO2 (104 mm Hg) matches alveolar oxygen pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Expired air is a mixture of dead space air (similar to humidified atmospheric air with PCO2 ~0.3 mm Hg) and alveolar air (PCO2 ~40 mm Hg). The exact composition depends on the ratio of dead space to alveolar air expired. Typically, expired air has PCO2 of approximately 27 mm Hg, representing a weighted average. Early in expiration, mostly dead space air with low PCO2 is expelled; late in expiration, pure alveolar air with 40 mm Hg PCO2 is expelled.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Expired air mixes dead-space air (essentially humidified air) with alveolar air, so its PCO2 (about 27 mm Hg) and PO2 (about 120 mm Hg) lie between those of humidified and alveolar air. COMLEX: First Aid's Bohr equation uses this mixed expired PCO2 to calculate physiologic dead space: VD = VT × (PaCO2 − PECO2)/PaCO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic dead space and its measurement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q33_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Composition and partial pressures of O2/CO2 — atmospheric air vs. alveolar air","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory therapist administers 100% oxygen to a patient. What is the maximum alveolar PO2 achievable at sea level with maximum alveolar ventilation while breathing 100% O2?","choices":{"A":"Maximum alveolar PO2 approaches 760 mm Hg with 100% oxygen","B":"Maximum alveolar PO2 approaches 600 mm Hg with 100% oxygen","C":"Maximum alveolar PO2 approaches 500 mm Hg with 100% oxygen","D":"Maximum alveolar PO2 approaches 713 − 40 (alveolar PCO2), about 673 mm Hg","E":"Maximum alveolar PO2 approaches 149 mm Hg, unchanged from breathing room air"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With 100% O2 breathing at sea level, inspired air after humidification has PO2 = 760 - 47 = 713 mm Hg (total pressure minus water vapor pressure). Even with maximum ventilation, alveolar air still contains CO2 at approximately 40 mm Hg. Therefore, maximum alveolar PO2 = 713 - 40 = approximately 673 mm Hg. This is much higher than the maximum of ~149 mm Hg achievable breathing room air, which is why supplemental oxygen is therapeutic in hypoxemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar PO2 can approach the PO2 of the inspired gas only at high ventilation; breathing air it can never exceed 149 mm Hg, but breathing gas with higher PO2 lets alveolar PO2 rise toward that level, minus the alveolar CO2. COMLEX: First Aid notes 100% O2 raises PaO2 in dead-space problems such as pulmonary embolism but not in a true shunt.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — value of oxygen therapy in different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q34_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlation between alveolar CO2 concentration/partial pressure and physiological significance","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with a fever has tissue CO₂ production that rises from 200 to 300 mL/min, and he holds his alveolar ventilation constant. After several minutes his alveolar PCO₂ has risen and then stabilized at a new, higher value. At this new steady state, how much CO₂ is he excreting through his alveoli each minute?","choices":{"A":"200 mL/min, the original excretion rate","B":"100 mL/min, just the added production","C":"450 mL/min, overshooting to clear the excess","D":"Less than 200 mL/min, as CO₂ is retained","E":"300 mL/min, equal to the new production"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton notes that CO₂ is continually formed in the body, carried to the alveoli, and removed by ventilation, so CO₂ production by the tissues and excretion from the alveoli are equal under steady-state conditions; the rise in alveolar PCO₂ (in direct proportion to production at a fixed ventilation) is what lets the same ventilation carry out the larger amount, and the system settles when excretion again equals production. Remaining at 200 mL/min, or falling below it, describes the transient before the new steady state, while CO₂ is still accumulating; a steady state cannot persist while 100 mL/min is retained. Excreting only the added 100 mL/min would ignore the baseline production. A sustained overshoot to 450 mL/min would progressively lower body CO₂ stores and is not a steady state.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 production by the tissues and excretion from the alveoli are equal at steady state, so when production rises at a fixed ventilation, alveolar PCO2 climbs until excretion again matches production. COMLEX: In fever or other hypermetabolic states, more ventilation is needed to keep PaCO2 normal; patients who cannot raise ventilation become hypercapnic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — chemical control of respiration by CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q35_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlation between alveolar CO2 concentration/partial pressure and physiological significance","batch":1,"stem":"A marathon runner increases metabolic rate during exercise, raising CO2 production from 200 mL/min to 800 mL/min. To maintain normal alveolar PCO2 of 40 mm Hg, how must alveolar ventilation change?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar ventilation must remain constant at 4.2 L/min baseline","B":"Alveolar ventilation must increase 4-fold from 4.2 to ~16.8 L/min","C":"Alveolar ventilation must double from 4.2 to 8.4 L/min only","D":"Alveolar ventilation must triple from 4.2 to 12.6 L/min only","E":"Alveolar ventilation must increase 10-fold to prevent CO2 retention"},"correct":"B","explanation":"To maintain constant alveolar PCO2 when CO2 production increases, alveolar ventilation must increase proportionally. When CO2 production quadruples from 200 to 800 mL/min, alveolar ventilation must also quadruple (from 4.2 to ~16.8 L/min) to maintain normal alveolar PCO2 at 40 mm Hg. If ventilation increased less than 4-fold, alveolar PCO2 would rise; if ventilation increased more, PCO2 would fall below normal. This demonstrates the tight coupling between metabolic CO2 production and ventilatory response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar PCO2 is proportional to CO2 output divided by alveolar ventilation, so a 4-fold rise in CO2 production needs a 4-fold rise in alveolar ventilation to keep PCO2 at 40 mm Hg. COMLEX: Guyton's Ch 39 shows the circulation keeps pace, with lung blood flow rising 4- to 7-fold in heavy exercise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 11 — chemical and nervous control of exercise ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q36_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlation between alveolar CO2 concentration/partial pressure and physiological significance","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher wants a gas sample that reflects true alveolar PCO₂ in a resting volunteer without placing a catheter in the lung. According to Guyton, which portion of the expired air should be collected?","choices":{"A":"The first portion, from the conducting airways","B":"The last portion, after a forceful expiration","C":"A mixed sample of the whole tidal breath","D":"The middle third of a normal tidal breath","E":"Air sampled at the nostrils during inspiration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton describes expired air as a combination of dead space air and alveolar air: the first portion, from the respiratory passageways, is typical humidified air, and progressively more alveolar air mixes in until all the dead space air has been washed out and nothing but alveolar air is expired at the end. The method of collecting alveolar air is therefore to collect the last portion of the expired air after forceful expiration has removed all the dead space air. The first portion is dead space air with a PCO₂ near that of humidified air. A mixed whole-breath sample has an intermediate composition (PCO₂ about 27 mm Hg versus 40 mm Hg alveolar). A mid-breath sample is still partly dead space air. Inspired air at the nostrils contains almost no CO₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The first expired air is dead-space air (humidified air), then alveolar air mixes in until only alveolar air remains, so a sample of the last portion after a forceful expiration reflects alveolar gas. COMLEX: First Aid's Bohr equation uses the PCO2 of the whole mixed expired air instead, to estimate physiologic dead space.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — dead space and alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q37_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Structure (layers, cells) and function of the respiratory membrane","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist explains that red blood cells must squeeze through pulmonary capillaries. What is the approximate diameter of pulmonary capillaries compared to red blood cell size?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary capillaries average 5 micrometers, requiring RBCs to squeeze through","B":"Pulmonary capillaries average 15 micrometers, allowing easy RBC passage","C":"Pulmonary capillaries average 25 micrometers, much larger than RBCs","D":"Pulmonary capillaries average 50 micrometers, providing ample RBC space","E":"Pulmonary capillaries average 2 micrometers, too small for RBCs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The average diameter of pulmonary capillaries is only about 5 micrometers. Red blood cells (RBCs), biconcave discs about 7.8 micrometers in diameter (Pawlina Ch 10), must squeeze through these narrow capillaries. This tight fit means the RBC membrane usually touches the capillary wall, which minimizes the distance O2 and CO2 must diffuse through plasma. This arrangement greatly increases the rapidity of gas diffusion by eliminating significant plasma barrier between alveolar air and RBC hemoglobin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary capillaries average only about 5 µm across, so red cells squeeze through with their membrane touching the capillary wall, and O2 and CO2 need not cross much plasma. COMLEX: This tight fit, with a membrane of about 0.6 µm, is part of why O2 normally equilibrates within the first third of the capillary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 12 — capillary transit time and gas exchange","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q38_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Structure (layers, cells) and function of the respiratory membrane","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies the blood volume in pulmonary capillaries. What is the total volume of blood in pulmonary capillaries at any given instant, and how does this relate to gas exchange efficiency?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 500-600 mL, providing large reservoir for gas exchange","B":"Approximately 250-300 mL, moderate volume for adequate gas exchange","C":"Approximately 1000-1200 mL, extensive volume ensuring complete oxygenation","D":"Approximately 10-20 mL, minimal volume requiring rapid gas exchange","E":"Approximately 60-140 mL, spread as thin sheet over 70 m² surface"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The total blood volume in pulmonary capillaries at any instant is only 60-140 mL. This small volume is spread over the enormous surface area of approximately 70 square meters (equivalent to a 25 × 30-foot room floor). Blood flows as a very thin sheet, described as being spread over this vast surface. This arrangement, with minimal blood volume distributed over maximum surface area, optimizes gas exchange by minimizing diffusion distance while maximizing contact area between blood and alveolar air.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At any instant only 60–140 mL of blood is in the lung capillaries, spread over about 70 m² of membrane, which Guyton compares to blood spread over a 25 × 30-ft floor. COMLEX: Guyton's Ch 39 gives about 70 mL of the lungs' 450 mL in the capillaries, flowing as a \"sheet of flow.\"","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — overall anatomy of the pulmonary circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q39_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Structure (layers, cells) and function of the respiratory membrane","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns about the basement membranes in the respiratory membrane structure. What unique characteristic do the epithelial and capillary basement membranes exhibit in many locations?","choices":{"A":"They remain separated by 10 micrometers of interstitial space","B":"They fuse together in many places, minimizing total membrane thickness","C":"They are absent, creating direct epithelial-endothelial contact","D":"They triple in thickness to provide structural support","E":"They contain smooth muscle cells that regulate capillary diameter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In many locations throughout the respiratory membrane, the epithelial basement membrane and the capillary basement membrane fuse together rather than remaining as separate layers. This fusion minimizes the total thickness of the respiratory membrane, reducing the distance gases must diffuse. Combined with the thin alveolar epithelium, minimal interstitial space, and thin capillary endothelium, this creates areas where total membrane thickness is as little as 0.2 micrometers, optimizing gas diffusion efficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In many places the capillary basement membrane fuses with the alveolar epithelial basement membrane, keeping the respiratory membrane as thin as 0.2 µm and about 0.6 µm on average. COMLEX: Interstitial fibrosis separates these layers with scar, which is why First Aid lists a low DLCO and an increased A–a gradient in interstitial lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — distinguishing features of restrictive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q40_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Disease states (emphysema, pulmonary edema, fibrosis) effect on gas diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old patient with severe emphysema has lung diffusing capacity for O2 reduced to 20% of normal. Which combination of pathophysiological changes BEST explains this severe reduction in diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"Increased alveolar surface area with enhanced capillary proliferation improves diffusion","B":"Bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy with enhanced airway tone increases resistance","C":"Pulmonary hypertension with increased capillary pressure enhances gas exchange","D":"Decreased surface area from alveolar destruction and V/Q mismatch reduce diffusion","E":"Mucus hypersecretion blocks airways but preserves normal diffusion capacity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Severe emphysema reduces lung diffusing capacity through multiple mechanisms: (1) destruction of alveolar walls decreases respiratory membrane surface area by 80% or more (from 70 m² to <14 m²), (2) loss of pulmonary capillaries further reduces surface area and blood volume for gas exchange, and (3) ventilation-perfusion mismatch occurs when some areas are ventilated but poorly perfused or vice versa. The dramatic surface area loss is the primary factor reducing diffusing capacity to 20% of normal. Bronchial smooth muscle and airway tone raise airway resistance and the work of breathing, but they do not change the membrane's diffusing capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In COPD, obstructed bronchioles leave alveoli unventilated (V/Q near zero, shunt) while destroyed walls leave ventilated areas with little blood flow (dead space), and membrane area can fall 5-fold; together these can cut gas-exchange effectiveness to one-tenth. COMLEX: Guyton calls this the most prevalent cause of pulmonary disability today.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — clinical features of COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q41_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Disease states (emphysema, pulmonary edema, fibrosis) effect on gas diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) with protein-rich edema fluid filling alveoli and interstitial spaces. How does this affect the rate of oxygen diffusion into the blood compared to normal conditions?","choices":{"A":"Diffusion rate increases due to enhanced protein-oxygen binding","B":"Diffusion rate is unchanged because O2 solubility is unaffected","C":"Diffusion rate increases as fluid provides additional surface area","D":"Diffusion rate decreases because diffusion distance increases","E":"Diffusion rate doubles because edema fluid enhances O2 transport"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffusion rate is proportional to the pressure difference, surface area and diffusion coefficient, and inversely proportional to diffusion distance (Guyton Ch 40). The diffusion coefficient for O2 (proportional to solubility/√molecular weight) is a property of the gas and is essentially unchanged, but edema fluid in the interstitium and alveoli greatly increases the distance O2 must travel and removes flooded alveoli from exchange, so the rate of O2 diffusion falls. Unchanged solubility does not keep the rate normal, protein does not bind O2 to speed its transfer, and fluid adds distance rather than surface area.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The diffusion coefficient depends only on the gas's solubility and molecular weight, so in ARDS O2 diffusion falls because protein-rich fluid and hyaline membranes lengthen the diffusion distance, not because the coefficient changes. COMLEX: First Aid's ARDS sequence: alveolar insult, cytokines, neutrophil recruitment and toxic mediators, endothelial damage and leaky vessels, protein-rich alveolar fluid and hyaline membranes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — pathogenesis of ARDS","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_ex1_q42_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Disease states (emphysema, pulmonary edema, fibrosis) effect on gas diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist measures diffusing capacity during exercise in a healthy person versus a patient with interstitial lung disease. In healthy individuals, diffusing capacity increases 3-fold during exercise. Why does it increase less in interstitial disease patients?","choices":{"A":"Decreased cardiac output limits blood flow to pulmonary capillaries","B":"Excessive capillary recruitment creates too much surface area","C":"Normal alveolar ventilation matches increased blood flow perfectly","D":"Enhanced diffusion coefficient compensates for membrane changes completely","E":"Thick membranes and poor capillary recruitment cap the diffusing capacity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During exercise, normal individuals increase lung diffusing capacity approximately 3-fold through: (1) recruitment of previously closed capillaries, and (2) distension of already-open capillaries, both increasing surface area. In interstitial lung disease, the respiratory membrane is thickened by fibrosis and inflammation, which impairs diffusion (D ∝ 1/d). Additionally, fibrosis may limit capillary recruitment and distension. Therefore, these patients cannot increase diffusing capacity normally during exercise, leading to exercise-induced hypoxemia despite increased ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In healthy people O2 diffusing capacity rises about 3-fold in strenuous exercise because dormant capillaries open and open ones dilate (more area) and V/Q matching improves. COMLEX: First Aid notes interstitial lung diseases lower DLCO, and Guyton warns that in strenuous exercise even a slight loss of surface area is a serious detriment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 10 — O2 diffusing capacity at rest vs. exercise","isHighYield":false}],"12":[{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q01_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2 — solubility vs. partial pressure gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Helium (molecular weight 4) is far lighter than oxygen (molecular weight 32), so its molecules move much faster. Yet Guyton lists helium's relative diffusion coefficient in body fluids as 0.95, slightly lower than oxygen's 1.0. Which property of helium explains why it diffuses no faster than oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Its higher partial pressure in the alveolar gas","B":"Its binding to hemoglobin in red cells","C":"Its low solubility in water, about 1/3 of O₂'s","D":"Its tendency to stay in the dead space air","E":"Its high vapor pressure at body temperature"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton's diffusion coefficient is proportional to S/√MW, so a gas's rate depends on both its solubility and its molecular weight. Helium's tiny molecular weight raises its molecular velocity (about √(32/4) ≈ 2.8 times that of O₂), but its solubility coefficient is only 0.008 versus 0.024 for O₂, about one-third, and the two effects nearly cancel, giving 0.95. Partial pressure is not part of the diffusion coefficient, which compares gases at equal pressure differences. Helium is inert and does not bind hemoglobin. Distribution between dead space and alveoli affects where a gas is, not its diffusion coefficient through tissue water. Vapor pressure describes water molecules escaping into the gas phase and plays no part in a gas's diffusion coefficient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A gas's diffusion coefficient is proportional to solubility / √molecular weight, so helium's very low solubility (0.008 vs. 0.024 for O2) offsets its light weight, giving it a relative coefficient of 0.95 vs. O2's 1.0. COMLEX: Helium's low solubility is also why Guyton uses it, an inert gas that stays in the lung, to measure FRC by dilution.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 11 — the helium dilution method for FRC","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q02_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2 — solubility vs. partial pressure gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Guyton compares how rapidly the respiratory gases cross the respiratory membrane for the same partial pressure difference. Which ranking, from fastest to slowest, matches his statement about CO₂, O₂, and nitrogen?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ about 20× O₂; O₂ about 2× N₂","B":"O₂ about 20× CO₂; CO₂ about 2× N₂","C":"N₂ about 2× O₂; O₂ about 20× CO₂","D":"CO₂ about 2× O₂; O₂ about 20× N₂","E":"O₂ about 2× N₂; N₂ about 20× CO₂"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that, because the rate of diffusion through the respiratory membrane is almost exactly the same as in water, for a given pressure difference CO₂ diffuses about 20 times as rapidly as O₂, and O₂ diffuses about twice as rapidly as nitrogen; the relative diffusion coefficients are CO₂ 20.3, O₂ 1.0, and N₂ 0.53. Ranking O₂ above CO₂ reverses the effect of CO₂'s high solubility (0.57 versus 0.024). Ranking N₂ above O₂ ignores nitrogen's lower solubility (0.012). Assigning the 20-fold step to O₂ versus N₂, or the 2-fold step to CO₂ versus O₂, swaps the two ratios.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusion through the respiratory membrane is almost the same as in water, so for a given pressure difference CO2 diffuses about 20 times as fast as O2 and O2 about twice as fast as nitrogen (relative coefficients 20.3, 1.0 and 0.53). COMLEX: First Aid notes diffusion-limiting diseases such as fibrosis therefore cause early hypoxemia before any hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — disease states that impair gas diffusion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q03_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2 — solubility vs. partial pressure gradient","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies factors affecting gas diffusion through the respiratory membrane. For gases with identical partial pressure gradients, which factor MOST determines the relative diffusion rates?","choices":{"A":"The surface area of the respiratory membrane available for exchange","B":"The thickness of the respiratory membrane at various locations","C":"The temperature of the blood flowing through pulmonary capillaries","D":"The cardiac output determining blood flow through pulmonary circulation","E":"The diffusion coefficient determined by solubility and molecular weight"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When partial pressure gradients are equal, the diffusion coefficient (S/√MW) is the primary determinant of relative diffusion rates between different gases. This coefficient incorporates both solubility (S) and molecular weight (MW). For example, CO2 diffuses 20 times faster than O2 primarily because of its much higher solubility (0.57 vs 0.024), despite slightly higher molecular weight. Surface area and thickness affect all gases equally and don't explain differences between gases. Temperature is a diffusion factor in Guyton's list, but body temperature stays nearly constant and affects all gases alike, so it does not explain differences between gases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When pressure gradients are equal, relative diffusion rates are set by each gas's diffusion coefficient, which combines solubility and molecular weight (∝ S/√MW). COMLEX: The other terms (area, thickness, gradient) are what disease changes: emphysema reduces area and fibrosis increases thickness (First Aid).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — Henry's law and the factors that set diffusion rate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q04_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Diffusing capacity — definition, calculation, quantifies gas exchange efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns that lung diffusing capacity quantifies gas exchange efficiency. What is the correct definition of diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"Total volume of gas exchanged per minute at any pressure","B":"Volume of gas diffusing per minute per mm Hg pressure difference","C":"Surface area available for gas exchange per square meter","D":"Pressure gradient required for normal resting oxygen consumption","E":"Time required for complete gas equilibration across membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diffusing capacity is defined as the volume of a gas that will diffuse through the respiratory membrane each minute for a partial pressure difference of 1 mm Hg. Units are mL/min per mm Hg. For example, normal O2 diffusing capacity at rest is 21 mL/min per mm Hg, meaning for each mm Hg of pressure gradient, 21 mL of O2 diffuses per minute. This quantifies membrane efficiency independent of the actual pressure gradient. Surface area is one determinant of diffusing capacity, not its definition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusing capacity is the volume of a gas that crosses the respiratory membrane per minute for a 1 mm Hg partial pressure difference (mL/min/mm Hg); for O2 in young men at rest it averages 21 mL/min/mm Hg. COMLEX: First Aid defines DLCO as the extent to which CO passes from the air sacs into blood; it is reduced in emphysema and interstitial lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC and the FEV1/FVC ratio in obstruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q05_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Diffusing capacity — definition, calculation, quantifies gas exchange efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist measures a patient's O2 diffusing capacity as 21 mL/min per mm Hg with a mean pressure gradient of 11 mm Hg across the respiratory membrane. How much O2 diffuses through the membrane per minute?","choices":{"A":"11 mL/min because diffusing capacity equals the pressure gradient","B":"231 mL/min by multiplying diffusing capacity by the gradient (21 × 11)","C":"21 mL/min regardless of the actual pressure gradient magnitude","D":"1.9 mL/min by dividing diffusing capacity by pressure gradient (21/11)","E":"32 mL/min by adding diffusing capacity and pressure gradient (21 + 11)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The total volume of gas diffusing per minute equals diffusing capacity × pressure gradient. With diffusing capacity of 21 mL/min per mm Hg and pressure gradient of 11 mm Hg: 21 × 11 = 231 mL/min of O2 diffusion. This approximately equals normal resting O2 consumption of 230-250 mL/min, demonstrating that normal diffusing capacity precisely matches metabolic needs at rest. The calculation shows how diffusing capacity and pressure gradient together determine actual gas transfer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 uptake = diffusing capacity × mean pressure difference: 21 mL/min/mm Hg × about 11 mm Hg ≈ 230 mL/min, equal to the resting body's O2 use. COMLEX: First Aid's diffusion equation (rate ∝ area × gradient / thickness) shows that losing area in emphysema or adding thickness in fibrosis lowers uptake at the same gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — O2 transport from alveoli to tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q06_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Diffusing capacity — definition, calculation, quantifies gas exchange efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains that diffusing capacity depends on multiple membrane factors. Which combination of factors would MOST increase lung diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"Decreased surface area with increased membrane thickness and lower solubility","B":"Increased membrane thickness with decreased surface area and lower gradient","C":"Normal surface area with normal thickness but decreased capillary blood volume","D":"Increased surface area with decreased membrane thickness and capillary recruitment","E":"Decreased alveolar ventilation with increased dead space and normal perfusion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffusing capacity is proportional to surface area, diffusion coefficient, and inversely proportional to membrane thickness (D ∝ A × S / d × √MW). Increasing surface area (through capillary recruitment and distension) and decreasing membrane thickness both directly increase diffusing capacity. This occurs during exercise when previously dormant capillaries open (increasing A) and blood distends thin membrane areas. Decreased area with thicker membrane, thicker membrane with less area, and decreased capillary blood volume describe factors that would decrease diffusing capacity. Less alveolar ventilation and more dead space impair ventilation without raising the membrane's diffusing capacity, which depends on area, thickness, and diffusion coefficient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Any factor that changes membrane thickness, surface area, the gas's diffusion coefficient or the pressure difference changes diffusing capacity; greater area and thinner membrane raise it. COMLEX: First Aid separates restrictive diseases by DLCO: extrapulmonary causes (muscle weakness, chest wall disease) keep a normal DLCO, while interstitial lung disease lowers it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — distinguishing features of restrictive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q07_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. during exercise","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns that normal resting O2 diffusing capacity is 21 mL/min per mm Hg. During strenuous exercise, how does O2 diffusing capacity change?","choices":{"A":"Decreases to approximately 7 mL/min per mm Hg during exercise","B":"Increases to approximately 63-65 mL/min per mm Hg during exercise","C":"Remains constant at 21 mL/min per mm Hg during exercise","D":"Decreases to approximately 15 mL/min per mm Hg during exercise","E":"Increases to approximately 42 mL/min per mm Hg during exercise"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During strenuous exercise, O2 diffusing capacity increases to approximately three times the resting value, from 21 mL/min per mm Hg to approximately 63-65 mL/min per mm Hg. This increase results from: (1) opening of previously dormant pulmonary capillaries, (2) distension of already-open capillaries, both increasing surface area, and (3) better ventilation-perfusion matching. This tripling of diffusing capacity is essential to meet the increased O2 demands during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In strenuous exercise the O2 diffusing capacity rises to about three times resting (about 65 vs. 21 mL/min/mm Hg) as dormant capillaries open, open ones dilate, and V/Q matching improves. COMLEX: First Aid notes the exercise rise in cardiac output dilates apical capillaries, bringing apical V/Q toward 1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 9 — pulmonary blood flow during heavy exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q08_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. during exercise","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher calculates CO2 diffusing capacity, which cannot be directly measured because the pressure gradient is too small (<1 mm Hg). What is the estimated CO2 diffusing capacity at rest?","choices":{"A":"About 400-450 mL/min per mm Hg, from CO2's diffusion coefficient","B":"Approximately 21 mL/min per mm Hg, equal to O2 diffusing capacity","C":"Approximately 100 mL/min per mm Hg, half of O2 capacity","D":"Approximately 50 mL/min per mm Hg, one-fourth of O2 capacity","E":"Approximately 10 mL/min per mm Hg, very low due to high solubility"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CO2 diffusing capacity cannot be directly measured because CO2 diffuses so rapidly that the pressure difference between alveolar air and pulmonary capillary blood is less than 1 mm Hg (too small to measure accurately). However, since diffusing capacity varies directly with the diffusion coefficient, and CO2's diffusion coefficient is ~20 times that of O2, CO2 diffusing capacity is estimated at 20 × 21 = 400-450 mL/min per mm Hg at rest. High solubility raises the diffusion coefficient, so it makes CO2 diffusing capacity very large, not very low.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 diffusing capacity has never been measured because the blood–alveolar PCO2 difference is under 1 mm Hg; since its diffusion coefficient is slightly over 20 times O2's, it is estimated at about 400–450 mL/min/mm Hg at rest. COMLEX: First Aid lists CO2 as perfusion-limited, equilibrating early in the capillary, so its removal depends on blood flow and ventilation rather than diffusion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — CO2 transport mechanisms in blood","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q09_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. during exercise","batch":1,"stem":"During maximal exercise, a healthy athlete's O2 diffusing capacity increases from 21 to 65 mL/min per mm Hg. What is the estimated CO2 diffusing capacity during this same exercise?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 200 mL/min per mm Hg, less than resting values","B":"Approximately 450 mL/min per mm Hg, unchanged from rest","C":"Approximately 800 mL/min per mm Hg, doubled from rest","D":"Approximately 1200-1300 mL/min per mm Hg, tripled from rest","E":"Approximately 2000 mL/min per mm Hg, greatly elevated"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During exercise, CO2 diffusing capacity increases proportionally to O2 diffusing capacity (both increase ~3-fold). Since CO2 diffusion coefficient is ~20 times that of O2, the CO2 diffusing capacity during exercise is approximately 20 × 65 = 1200-1300 mL/min per mm Hg (compared to 400-450 at rest). Despite this massive diffusing capacity, the actual CO2 pressure gradient remains less than 1 mm Hg even during maximal exercise, demonstrating CO2's remarkable diffusion efficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Because diffusing capacity varies with the diffusion coefficient, CO2's is estimated at about 400–450 mL/min/mm Hg at rest and about 1200–1300 during exercise, tripling as O2's does. COMLEX: With such a large reserve, hypercapnia in lung disease reflects inadequate ventilation or V/Q mismatch, not a CO2 diffusion limit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — alveolar PCO2 and its relation to ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q10_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Carbon monoxide method for diffusing capacity; conversion factor (1.23) to O2 DLco","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonary function laboratory uses carbon monoxide to measure diffusing capacity. Why is CO used instead of directly measuring O2 diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"CO is cheaper and more readily available than pure oxygen","B":"CO has higher diffusion coefficient, making measurements more accurate","C":"Blood PCO stays near zero, so the gradient equals alveolar PCO","D":"CO does not bind to hemoglobin, allowing free diffusion measurements","E":"CO requires smaller volumes, making the test safer for patients"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Measuring O2 diffusing capacity directly would require the PO2 of pulmonary capillary blood, which cannot be measured accurately. With CO, hemoglobin binds the gas so rapidly that its pressure in the blood never has time to build up and stays essentially zero, so the pressure gradient equals the alveolar PCO, which is easy to measure (Guyton Ch 40). Diffusing capacity for CO is then CO uptake divided by alveolar PCO, and O2 diffusing capacity is about 1.23 times that value. Cost and test volume are not the reason, CO binds hemoglobin avidly rather than not at all, and its diffusion coefficient is not what makes the method work.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the CO method a small amount of CO is inhaled; hemoglobin binds it so fast that blood PCO stays essentially zero, so the gradient equals alveolar PCO, and DLCO = CO uptake / alveolar PCO. COMLEX: First Aid lists CO as a diffusion-limited gas, which is what makes it suitable for testing the membrane.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide and its effects on hemoglobin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q11_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Carbon monoxide method for diffusing capacity; conversion factor (1.23) to O2 DLco","batch":1,"stem":"A patient's CO diffusing capacity is measured at 17 mL/min per mm Hg. To convert this to O2 diffusing capacity, which calculation is correct?","choices":{"A":"Divide by 1.23: 17 / 1.23 = 13.8 mL/min per mm Hg","B":"Multiply by 1.23: 17 × 1.23 = 21 mL/min per mm Hg","C":"Add 1.23: 17 + 1.23 = 18.23 mL/min per mm Hg","D":"Subtract 1.23: 17 - 1.23 = 15.77 mL/min per mm Hg","E":"Multiply by 20: 17 × 20 = 340 mL/min per mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"To convert CO diffusing capacity to O2 diffusing capacity, multiply by the factor 1.23 because the diffusion coefficient for O2 is 1.23 times that for CO. The average CO diffusing capacity in healthy young men at rest is about 17 mL/min per mm Hg, and multiplying by 1.23 gives O2 diffusing capacity of approximately 21 mL/min per mm Hg (17 × 1.23 = 20.91 ≈ 21). This conversion allows indirect measurement of O2 diffusing capacity. Dividing by 1.23 or subtracting 1.23 makes the O2 value smaller than the CO value, but O2 diffuses 1.23 times as readily as CO, so the O2 capacity must be larger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 diffusing capacity = CO diffusing capacity × 1.23; in healthy young men DLCO averages about 17 mL/min/mm Hg at rest, giving about 21 for O2. COMLEX: First Aid reports a low DLCO in emphysema (destroyed alveolar–capillary membrane) and in interstitial lung disease, but a normal DLCO in extrapulmonary restriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — emphysema types and their alveolar destruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q12_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Carbon monoxide method for diffusing capacity; conversion factor (1.23) to O2 DLco","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks why the conversion factor from CO to O2 diffusing capacity is 1.23. What does this factor represent?","choices":{"A":"The ratio of O2 molecular weight to CO molecular weight","B":"The ratio of CO solubility to O2 solubility coefficients","C":"The ratio of hemoglobin binding affinity for CO versus O2","D":"The ratio of alveolar PO2 to alveolar PCO at rest","E":"The ratio of the O2 and CO diffusion coefficients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The conversion factor 1.23 represents the ratio of O2 diffusion coefficient to CO diffusion coefficient. Since diffusion coefficient = S/√MW (solubility divided by square root of molecular weight), and O2's diffusion coefficient is 1.0 while CO's is 0.81, the ratio is 1.0/0.81 ≈ 1.23. This means O2 diffuses 1.23 times faster than CO for the same pressure gradient. Therefore, measured CO diffusing capacity × 1.23 = O2 diffusing capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The 1.23 factor is the ratio of the diffusion coefficients: O2 diffuses 1.23 times as fast as CO (relative coefficients 1.0 and 0.81). COMLEX: Because the conversion is a fixed constant, a falling DLCO tracks a falling O2 diffusing capacity, the change First Aid lists for emphysema and interstitial lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — Henry's law and factors in diffusion rate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q13_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Ventilation-perfusion (VA/Q) ratio — effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns about ventilation-perfusion (V̇A/Q̇) ratio. When V̇A/Q̇ equals zero (no ventilation but normal perfusion), what are the alveolar gas partial pressures?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PO2 = 149 mm Hg and PCO2 = 0 mm Hg, matching inspired air","B":"Alveolar PO2 = 104 mm Hg and PCO2 = 40 mm Hg, matching normal alveoli","C":"Alveolar PO2 = 95 mm Hg and PCO2 = 35 mm Hg, matching arterial blood","D":"Alveolar PO2 = 40 mm Hg and PCO2 = 45 mm Hg, matching venous blood","E":"Alveolar PO2 = 0 mm Hg and PCO2 = 760 mm Hg, complete hypoxia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When V̇A/Q̇ = 0 (no ventilation but continued perfusion), alveolar gases equilibrate with the blood perfusing the capillaries. Since this blood is mixed venous blood returning from systemic circulation (PO2 = 40 mm Hg, PCO2 = 45 mm Hg), the alveolar gases assume these same values. No fresh O2 enters to raise PO2, and no ventilation removes CO2, so the alveolus becomes a \"shunt\" with venous-like gas pressures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When V/Q is zero, an alveolus is perfused but not ventilated, so its gas equilibrates with mixed venous blood (PO2 40 mm Hg, PCO2 45 mm Hg) and no exchange occurs. COMLEX: First Aid calls V/Q = 0 an airway obstruction (shunt), as with a foreign body, in which 100% O2 does not improve PaO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — resorption atelectasis behind an airway obstruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q14_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Ventilation-perfusion (VA/Q) ratio — effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains V̇A/Q̇ mismatch to a patient. When V̇A/Q̇ equals infinity (normal ventilation but zero perfusion), what are the alveolar gas partial pressures?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PO2 = 40 mm Hg and PCO2 = 45 mm Hg, matching venous blood","B":"Alveolar PO2 = 104 mm Hg and PCO2 = 40 mm Hg, normal alveolar values","C":"Alveolar PO2 = 95 mm Hg and PCO2 = 35 mm Hg, matching arterial blood","D":"Alveolar PO2 = 760 mm Hg and PCO2 = 760 mm Hg, atmospheric pressure","E":"Alveolar PO2 = 149 and PCO2 = 0 mm Hg, matching humidified air"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When V̇A/Q̇ = infinity (normal ventilation but no blood flow), there is no capillary blood to remove O2 or add CO2. The alveolar air therefore becomes equal to humidified inspired air: PO2 = 149 mm Hg (atmospheric 21% O2 minus water vapor dilution) and PCO2 = 0 mm Hg. This represents complete \"dead space\" where ventilation is wasted because no gas exchange occurs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When V/Q is infinite, an alveolus is ventilated but not perfused, so nothing removes O2 or adds CO2 and alveolar gas equals humidified inspired air (PO2 149 mm Hg, PCO2 0). COMLEX: First Aid's example is pulmonary embolism, a dead-space problem in which 100% O2 still improves PaO2 (unless dead space is 100%).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — pathophysiology of pulmonary embolism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q15_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Ventilation-perfusion (VA/Q) ratio — effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies regional V̇A/Q̇ variation in normal upright lungs. How does V̇A/Q̇ ratio differ between lung apex and base?","choices":{"A":"Apex V̇A/Q̇ is lower than base due to reduced ventilation","B":"Apex and base have identical V̇A/Q̇ ratios in healthy lungs","C":"Apex V̇A/Q̇ (about 2.5) is higher than base (about 0.6)","D":"Base V̇A/Q̇ is much higher than apex due to gravity","E":"Apex V̇A/Q̇ is zero while base V̇A/Q̇ is infinity normally"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In upright healthy individuals, both ventilation and perfusion decrease from lung base to apex, but perfusion decreases more than ventilation. Therefore, apex V̇A/Q̇ is ~2.5 (creating physiological dead space), while base V̇A/Q̇ is ~0.6 (creating mild physiological shunt). During exercise, increased blood flow to upper lung improves matching, reducing dead space. This regional variation slightly decreases overall lung gas exchange efficiency at rest. Sources give the apex value differently: Guyton Ch 40 has apical V̇A/Q̇ as much as 2.5 times the ideal value and basal as low as 0.6 times; First Aid 2025 gives about 3 at the apex and 0.6 at the base.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upright, both ventilation and perfusion fall toward the apex, but perfusion falls more, so apical V/Q is as much as 2.5 times ideal (Guyton; First Aid gives about 3) and basal V/Q as low as 0.6; the apex behaves as dead space and the base as shunt. COMLEX: First Aid notes organisms that thrive on high O2, such as TB, flourish in the apex.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — physiologic differences among lung zones 1, 2 and 3","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q16_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological shunt vs. physiological dead space; implications in COPD and other diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist explains physiological shunt to a medical student. Which statement correctly defines physiological shunt?","choices":{"A":"Blood flow through anatomical dead space areas of respiratory passages","B":"Ventilation of alveoli that have no pulmonary capillary blood flow","C":"Total amount of blood flow per minute that fails to become fully oxygenated","D":"Air remaining in alveoli at end of normal expiration (functional residual capacity)","E":"Difference between alveolar ventilation and anatomical dead space ventilation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Physiological shunt is the total amount of blood per minute that fails to become fully oxygenated as it passes through the lungs. It includes: (1) blood flowing through alveolar capillaries with low V̇A/Q̇ ratios (inadequate ventilation), and (2) anatomical shunt blood bypassing alveoli entirely (bronchial circulation, ~2% cardiac output). Physiological shunt is measured using the shunt equation: Q̇PS/Q̇T = (CiO2 - CaO2)/(CiO2 - Cv̄O2). Greater shunt means more poorly oxygenated blood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physiologic shunt is the total blood per minute that fails to be oxygenated: blood through low-V/Q alveoli plus about 2% of cardiac output through bronchial vessels; it is calculated from arterial, mixed venous and ideal O2 contents. COMLEX: First Aid groups right-to-left shunt (anatomic or physiologic, as in ARDS) with V/Q mismatch and diffusion limitation as causes of an increased A–a gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — bronchial vessels and their drainage into pulmonary veins","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q17_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological shunt vs. physiological dead space; implications in COPD and other diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old patient with COPD has significant physiological dead space. Which clinical scenario BEST represents physiological dead space?","choices":{"A":"Well-ventilated alveoli with adequate perfusion, creating normal gas exchange","B":"Poorly ventilated alveoli with excessive perfusion, causing venous admixture","C":"Bronchial obstruction with complete atelectasis, eliminating all ventilation","D":"Well-ventilated alveoli with inadequate or absent perfusion, wasting ventilation","E":"Normal anatomical dead space in trachea and bronchi only"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Physiological dead space represents wasted ventilation: air ventilating alveoli that have inadequate blood flow to transport the gases away. It includes anatomical dead space (airways) plus alveolar dead space (well-ventilated but poorly perfused alveoli). In COPD with emphysema, many alveolar walls and capillaries are destroyed, creating areas with ventilation but minimal perfusion (high V̇A/Q̇). This wasted ventilation increases work of breathing without improving gas exchange.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physiologic dead space is wasted ventilation: anatomic dead space plus well-ventilated alveoli with too little blood flow; it is measured with the Bohr equation from arterial and mixed expired PCO2. COMLEX: In COPD, destroyed alveolar walls with remaining ventilation add dead space while obstructed bronchioles add shunt, sometimes cutting lung effectiveness to one-tenth (Guyton).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — dead space, alveolar ventilation and homeostasis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q18_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological shunt vs. physiological dead space; implications in COPD and other diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A physician calculates physiological dead space using the Bohr equation: V̇D/V̇T = (PaCO2 - P̄eCO2)/PaCO2. A patient has arterial PCO2 of 40 mm Hg and mixed expired PCO2 of 30 mm Hg. What is the physiological dead space fraction?","choices":{"A":"Dead space is 10% of tidal volume: (40-30)/40 = 0.10","B":"Dead space is 25% of tidal volume: (40-30)/40 = 0.25","C":"Dead space is 50% of tidal volume: (40-30)/40 = 0.50","D":"Dead space is 75% of tidal volume: (40-30)/40 = 0.75","E":"Dead space cannot be calculated from these values alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using the Bohr equation: V̇D/V̇T = (PaCO2 - P̄eCO2)/PaCO2 = (40 - 30)/40 = 10/40 = 0.25 or 25%. This means 25% of each breath ventilates dead space and doesn't participate in gas exchange. The remaining 75% ventilates functional alveoli. Guyton Ch 38 gives a normal dead space of about 150 mL with a normal tidal volume of 500 mL (about 30%), and First Aid 2025 notes that in normal lungs physiological dead space approximately equals anatomic dead space, so a fraction of 25% shows no increase in wasted ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bohr equation — VD/VT = (PaCO2 − PECO2)/PaCO2; expired air carries less CO2 than arterial blood because part of each breath never meets perfused alveoli. FA: physiologic dead space = anatomic + alveolar dead space, roughly equal to anatomic in normal lungs and larger with V/Q mismatch; the apex of a healthy lung is the largest contributor of alveolar dead space. COMLEX: A rising VD/VT means wasted ventilation — the patient must raise minute ventilation just to hold PaCO2 steady.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — pulmonary embolism, which leaves ventilated alveoli unperfused and so enlarges alveolar dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q19_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2 — solubility vs. partial pressure gradient","batch":1,"stem":"Guyton lists five factors besides the pressure difference that affect the rate of gas diffusion in a fluid. He notes that one of them can usually be left out when analyzing gas exchange in the body. Which factor is it?","choices":{"A":"Solubility of the gas in the fluid","B":"Cross-sectional area of the fluid","C":"Temperature of the fluid medium","D":"Distance the gas must diffuse","E":"Molecular weight of the gas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton's factors are the solubility of the gas, the cross-sectional area of the fluid, the distance through which the gas must diffuse, the molecular weight of the gas, and the temperature of the fluid, and he notes that in the body the temperature remains reasonably constant and usually need not be considered. Solubility and molecular weight together make up the diffusion coefficient (S/√MW), which differs greatly between gases. Cross-sectional area and diffusion distance correspond to the surface area and thickness of the respiratory membrane, both of which change with disease, such as loss of alveolar walls in emphysema or thickening in fibrosis and edema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's five factors for gas diffusion in a fluid are solubility, cross-sectional area, diffusion distance, molecular weight and temperature; temperature drops out because body temperature stays reasonably constant. Solubility and molecular weight together set the diffusion coefficient (∝ S/√MW). COMLEX: FA's Fick form, diffusion = A × Dk × (P1 − P2)/Δx, maps the rest onto disease — area falls in emphysema, thickness rises in fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — Henry's law and how surface area and membrane thickness set the diffusion rate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q20_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2 — solubility vs. partial pressure gradient","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks why CO2 elimination is rarely a problem even in severe lung disease, while O2 uptake often fails. Which factor explains this clinical observation?","choices":{"A":"CO2's 20-fold faster diffusion offsets its small gradient","B":"CO2 has larger pressure gradients than O2 across membranes","C":"CO2 is actively transported, requiring no diffusion gradient","D":"CO2 molecular weight is lower, increasing diffusion velocity","E":"CO2 partial pressures equilibrate faster due to lower solubility"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CO2 elimination rarely fails because CO2 diffuses approximately 20 times faster than O2 due to its much higher diffusion coefficient (20.3 vs 1.0). Even though CO2's pressure gradient (~5 mm Hg) is much smaller than O2's (~64 mm Hg), the 20-fold diffusion advantage more than compensates. Therefore, even when membrane thickness increases or surface area decreases in lung disease, CO2 can still diffuse adequately while O2 transfer becomes impaired. This is why hypoxemia appears before hypercapnia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: For a given pressure difference CO2 diffuses about 20 times as rapidly as O2, so its tiny gradient still clears it; FA: diffusion-limiting disease such as pulmonary fibrosis causes early hypoxia rather than hypercapnia. COMLEX: Hypoxemia with a normal or low PaCO2 in interstitial disease is expected — a rising PaCO2 points to hypoventilation or advanced disease rather than a diffusion defect alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — when hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist and when one occurs without the other","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q21_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Diffusion rates of O2 vs. CO2 — solubility vs. partial pressure gradient","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist explains that doubling membrane thickness affects O2 and CO2 diffusion equally in percentage terms but differently in clinical significance. If membrane thickness doubles, why does hypoxemia develop before hypercapnia?","choices":{"A":"O2 has lower molecular weight, making it more sensitive to thickness","B":"O2 is actively transported, making diffusion less efficient","C":"O2 requires larger surface area due to hemoglobin binding","D":"O2 diffuses ~20× slower, so it has little reserve when the barrier thickens","E":"O2 has smaller pressure gradient, providing less driving force initially"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Doubling membrane thickness halves the diffusion rate of both gases (rate ∝ 1/distance). CO2's diffusion coefficient is about 20 times that of O2, so even with its small gradient (~5 mm Hg) it equilibrates with a large reserve; O2 diffuses about 20 times more slowly, so a thickened barrier erodes its margin first and hypoxemia appears while CO2 is still eliminated (Guyton Ch 40). O2 actually has the larger pressure gradient (about 64 mm Hg vs 5 mm Hg for CO2), so a small gradient cannot explain it. O2 is not actively transported, its molecular weight (32) is lower than CO2's (44), and hemoglobin binding does not change the membrane surface area it needs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusion rate is inversely proportional to membrane thickness for every gas, but O2 has a diffusion coefficient about one-twentieth that of CO2, so a thickened barrier erodes O2 transfer first. FA: O2 becomes diffusion limited in fibrosis and emphysema, while CO2 stays perfusion limited. COMLEX: Exertional desaturation on a walk test is often the earliest objective sign of a thickened membrane, before resting PaO2 falls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — fibrosis, edema and emphysema as disease states that slow diffusion across the membrane","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q22_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Diffusing capacity — definition, calculation, quantifies gas exchange efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has his right lung removed for cancer, halving the surface area of his respiratory membrane. His physician explains how far surface area can fall before gas exchange is substantially impeded even at rest. According to Guyton, at about what fraction of normal surface area does this occur?","choices":{"A":"About four-fifths to nine-tenths","B":"About one-third to one-fourth","C":"About two-thirds to three-fourths","D":"About one-half to two-thirds","E":"About one-tenth to one-twentieth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton notes that removal of an entire lung decreases the total respiratory membrane surface area to half normal, and emphysema can reduce it as much as 5-fold through loss of alveolar walls; when the total surface area is decreased to about one-third to one-fourth normal, exchange of gases is substantially impeded even under resting conditions, and during strenuous exercise even the slightest decrease can be a serious detriment. Four-fifths to nine-tenths and two-thirds to three-fourths of normal are smaller losses that matter mainly during exercise. One-half to two-thirds spans the pneumonectomy value itself, which Guyton does not describe as impeding resting exchange. One-tenth to one-twentieth of normal lies far below the threshold he gives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Removing a whole lung halves membrane area, and emphysema can cut it as much as 5-fold; exchange is substantially impeded at rest only when area falls to about one-third to one-fourth of normal, though in strenuous exercise even a slight loss matters. COMLEX: Preoperative lung-resection planning rests on this reserve — a patient who tolerates rest may still desaturate with exertion after pneumonectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — emphysema types, whose alveolar wall destruction is the other way membrane area is lost","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q23_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Diffusing capacity — definition, calculation, quantifies gas exchange efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher measures diffusing capacity in healthy subjects. One subject has diffusing capacity of 21 mL/min per mm Hg with mean pressure gradient of 8 mm Hg. Another has 18 mL/min per mm Hg with gradient of 14 mm Hg. Which subject has greater total O2 diffusion?","choices":{"A":"First subject: 21 × 8 = 168 mL/min exceeds the second","B":"Second subject: 18 + 14 = 32 mL/min is greater than first","C":"First subject: 21 - 8 = 13 mL/min is less than second","D":"Second subject: 18 × 14 = 252 mL/min is greater than first","E":"Both subjects have identical O2 diffusion rates at all times"},"correct":"D","explanation":"First subject: 21 × 8 = 168 mL/min. Second subject: 18 × 14 = 252 mL/min. The second subject has greater total O2 diffusion (252 vs 168 mL/min) despite lower diffusing capacity because the larger pressure gradient compensates. This illustrates that total diffusion depends on BOTH diffusing capacity (membrane efficiency) and pressure gradient (driving force). The second subject might have lower alveolar PO2 or higher venous PO2, creating the larger gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Net O2 diffusion per minute = diffusing capacity × mean pressure gradient; in Guyton's resting example a mean gradient of about 11 mm Hg moves roughly 230 mL O2/min, matching resting O2 use. Diffusing capacity is a property of the membrane; the gradient is a separate driver. COMLEX: A widened alveolar–capillary gradient can keep O2 uptake near normal at rest even when diffusing capacity is reduced.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — the O2 and CO2 partial pressure gradients that drive gas movement","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q24_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Diffusing capacity — definition, calculation, quantifies gas exchange efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks which factors are included in the diffusing capacity measurement. What does diffusing capacity directly incorporate?","choices":{"A":"Only the partial pressure gradient across the membrane","B":"Only the oxygen consumption rate of body tissues","C":"Only the cardiac output and blood flow rate","D":"Surface area, thickness, and diffusion coefficient combined","E":"Only the alveolar ventilation rate per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffusing capacity (D) incorporates: (1) surface area (A) of the respiratory membrane, (2) membrane thickness (d), and (3) diffusion coefficient (S/√MW) of the gas. The formula D ∝ (A × S)/(d × √MW) shows these factors are intrinsic to diffusing capacity. Pressure gradient is NOT part of diffusing capacity itself; it's multiplied by diffusing capacity to calculate total diffusion. This is why diffusing capacity is expressed per mm Hg, indicating it's independent of actual pressure gradient. Tissue oxygen consumption, cardiac output, and alveolar ventilation are not properties of the membrane and are not part of diffusing capacity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diffusing capacity is the volume of gas crossing the membrane each minute per 1 mm Hg of pressure difference, so it folds in area, thickness and the gas's diffusion coefficient but excludes the gradient itself; FA: DLCO falls when area falls (emphysema) or thickness rises (fibrosis). COMLEX: FA uses DLCO to split restrictive disease — extrapulmonary causes (neuromuscular weakness, scoliosis, obesity) keep a normal DLCO and A-a gradient, while interstitial lung disease lowers DLCO.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 6 — the layers of the respiratory membrane that make up its area and thickness","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q25_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. during exercise","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy athlete's O2 diffusing capacity at rest is 21 mL/min per mm Hg. During maximal exercise, it increases to 65 mL/min per mm Hg. What physiological changes account for this 3-fold increase?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar ventilation increases, raising alveolar PO2 to maximum levels","B":"Capillary recruitment and distension increase surface area available for diffusion","C":"Membrane thickness decreases as lung tissue stretches during deep breathing","D":"Blood hemoglobin concentration increases, enhancing oxygen binding capacity","E":"Cardiac output increases, raising pressure gradient across respiratory membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The 3-fold increase in O2 diffusing capacity during exercise results primarily from: (1) recruitment of previously closed pulmonary capillaries (up to 3-fold increase in number), and (2) distension of already-open capillaries. Both mechanisms dramatically increase surface area (A) for gas exchange. Since D ∝ A, tripling surface area triples diffusing capacity. Better ventilation-perfusion matching also contributes. Membrane thickness and hemoglobin concentration don't change acutely during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In exercise O2 diffusing capacity rises to about three times resting, chiefly by opening dormant pulmonary capillaries and dilating open ones (more surface area) and by better matching of ventilation to perfusion. COMLEX: Because the apices shift from zone 2 to zone 3 flow in exercise, capillary recruitment is the lung's built-in reserve — the reserve that ILD and emphysema patients lose first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 9 — pulmonary blood flow in heavy exercise, which recruits and distends the same capillaries","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q26_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. during exercise","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains that CO2 diffusing capacity at rest (~400-450 mL/min per mm Hg) far exceeds O2 capacity (21 mL/min per mm Hg). Despite this massive capacity, why can't CO2 pressure gradient be directly measured?","choices":{"A":"CO2 equilibrates so rapidly that pressure difference is <1 mm Hg, too small to measure","B":"CO2 actively binds to carbonic anhydrase, preventing partial pressure measurement","C":"CO2 is completely removed from blood, creating zero capillary PCO2","D":"CO2 measuring instruments lack sufficient sensitivity for any measurement","E":"CO2 production varies too rapidly for stable pressure measurements"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CO2's diffusing capacity is so high (~400-450 mL/min per mm Hg at rest, ~1200-1300 during exercise) that CO2 equilibrates almost instantly between blood and alveolar air. The pressure difference between pulmonary capillary blood PCO2 and alveolar PCO2 is less than 1 mm Hg, which is below the measurement threshold of available techniques. This tiny gradient is still adequate for complete CO2 elimination because of CO2's extremely high diffusion coefficient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2's diffusing capacity is many times O2's, so the capillary–alveolar PCO2 difference is under 1 mm Hg, too small to measure. FA: CO2 is perfusion limited — it equilibrates early along the capillary. COMLEX: Because CO2 clears so easily, a high PaCO2 almost always means inadequate alveolar ventilation, not a membrane problem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — the alveolar, capillary and tissue partial pressure gradients for O2 and CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q27_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest vs. during exercise","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with interstitial lung disease has O2 diffusing capacity of 10 mL/min per mm Hg at rest (normal 21). During exercise, it increases to only 18 mL/min per mm Hg. What limits the exercise-induced increase in diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"Excessive capillary recruitment creates too much surface area","B":"Decreased cardiac output limits pulmonary blood flow during exercise","C":"Normal alveolar ventilation prevents adequate gas exchange","D":"Fibrotic membrane prevents normal capillary recruitment and distension","E":"Enhanced diffusion coefficient compensates for all membrane abnormalities"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In interstitial lung disease, fibrosis thickens the membrane and restricts the pulmonary vasculature. During exercise, normal lungs recruit/distend capillaries to triple diffusing capacity (21→65). This patient's capacity only increases from 10→18 (1.8-fold) because fibrosis: (1) prevents capillary recruitment (stiff tissue limits opening of dormant capillaries), and (2) limits capillary distension. The thickened membrane also reduces baseline capacity. This patient will develop severe exercise-induced hypoxemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal O2 diffusing capacity triples in exercise through capillary recruitment and distension; a fibrotic, thickened interstitium both lowers the resting value and blunts this rise. FA: in pulmonary fibrosis O2 becomes diffusion limited — it fails to equilibrate by the end of the capillary. COMLEX: Exercise-induced desaturation is typical of ILD; ambulatory oximetry guides when to prescribe supplemental O2 for exertion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and other restrictive diseases that thicken the interstitium","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q28_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Carbon monoxide method for diffusing capacity; conversion factor (1.23) to O2 DLco","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student measures CO diffusing capacity as 14 mL/min per mm Hg in a patient with mild emphysema. What is the calculated O2 diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"14 / 1.23 = 11.4 mL/min per mm Hg (below normal)","B":"14 × 1.23 = 17.2 mL/min per mm Hg (below normal)","C":"14 + 1.23 = 15.23 mL/min per mm Hg (below normal)","D":"14 - 1.23 = 12.77 mL/min per mm Hg (below normal)","E":"14 × 20 = 280 mL/min per mm Hg (above normal)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"O2 diffusing capacity = CO diffusing capacity × 1.23 = 14 × 1.23 = 17.2 mL/min per mm Hg. This is below normal (21 mL/min per mm Hg), consistent with mild emphysema where surface area is reduced. The patient would likely have adequate oxygenation at rest, because the alveolar–capillary PO2 gradient widens enough to keep uptake near 250 mL/min, but would desaturate during exercise when O2 demands increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO diffusing capacity × 1.23 = O2 diffusing capacity, because O2's diffusion coefficient is 1.23 times that of CO. COMLEX: FA — emphysema destroys alveolar walls and the alveolar–capillary membrane, lowering DLCO, whereas chronic bronchitis is defined clinically by productive cough for at least 3 months in 2 consecutive years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — how chronic emphysema leads to hypoxia and hypercapnia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q29_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Carbon monoxide method for diffusing capacity; conversion factor (1.23) to O2 DLco","batch":1,"stem":"In a single-breath carbon monoxide test, a 25-year-old man absorbs CO at a rate of 8.5 mL/min while his alveolar PCO averages 0.5 mm Hg. What is his CO diffusing capacity?","choices":{"A":"4.25 mL/min per mm Hg","B":"8.5 mL/min per mm Hg","C":"17 mL/min per mm Hg","D":"21 mL/min per mm Hg","E":"34 mL/min per mm Hg"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton's CO method relies on the CO pressure in the blood being essentially zero, because hemoglobin binds CO so rapidly that its pressure never builds up; the pressure difference across the membrane therefore equals the alveolar PCO, and dividing the volume of CO absorbed per minute by the alveolar PCO gives the CO diffusing capacity: 8.5 ÷ 0.5 = 17 mL/min per mm Hg, the average for healthy young men. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 4.25. Reporting the uptake itself, 8.5, ignores the pressure term. A value of 21 is the O₂ diffusing capacity obtained after multiplying 17 by 1.23, not the CO value. A value of 34 doubles the correct answer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The CO method works because hemoglobin binds CO so avidly that capillary PCO stays essentially zero; the gradient therefore equals alveolar PCO, and CO diffusing capacity = CO absorbed per minute ÷ alveolar PCO. COMLEX: FA lists DLCO as the bedside measure of how readily gas crosses from air sacs into blood — normal in neuromuscular or chest-wall restriction, low in interstitial lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide's high-affinity binding to hemoglobin, the basis of the CO method","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q30_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Ventilation-perfusion (VA/Q) ratio — effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","batch":1,"stem":"In a healthy upright person at rest, the lung apices have a ventilation-perfusion ratio about 2.5 times the ideal value, creating a moderate physiological dead space there. What happens to this apical dead space during exercise, and why?","choices":{"A":"It grows, as apical ventilation rises more than flow","B":"It is unchanged, as gravity fixes regional flow","C":"It grows, as zone 1 flow spreads down the lung","D":"It shrinks, as alveolar ventilation falls at the apex","E":"It shrinks, as blood flow to the upper lung rises"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton explains that at rest both blood flow and ventilation are lower at the top of the upright lung, but flow falls more, so the apical V̇A/Q̇ is as much as 2.5 times ideal and produces moderate physiological dead space; during exercise, blood flow to the upper part of the lung increases markedly, so far less physiological dead space occurs and gas exchange approaches optimum. A rise in apical ventilation exceeding the rise in flow is the opposite of what Guyton describes. Regional flow is not fixed: rising pulmonary pressures in exercise convert the apex from zone 2 to zone 3. Zone 1 flow appears with low arterial or high alveolar pressure, not in exercise. Apical ventilation does not fall during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upright at rest both flow and ventilation are lowest at the apex, but flow falls more — apical V/Q is up to 2.5 times ideal per Guyton (FA gives about 3); in exercise apical blood flow rises markedly and the dead space largely disappears (FA: V/Q approaches 1). COMLEX: The high-O2 apex is where TB reactivates, a V/Q fact tested on both exams.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — pressure and flow differences among zones 1, 2 and 3 in the upright lung","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q31_b1","objective":12,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 12 — Ventilation-perfusion (VA/Q) ratio — effects on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with lung disease is in severe respiratory distress. Her total alveolar ventilation is normal, and her total pulmonary blood flow is normal. Which explanation best accounts for her impaired gas exchange?","choices":{"A":"Her respiratory membrane must be uniformly thickened","B":"Her hemoglobin must have lost its O₂ affinity","C":"Ventilation and flow reach different lung regions","D":"Her tidal volume is entirely dead space ventilation","E":"Her alveolar PO₂ exceeds that of inspired air"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton explains that in many lung diseases some areas are well ventilated but have almost no blood flow, while other areas have excellent blood flow but little or no ventilation; gas exchange is then seriously impaired and the person may suffer severe respiratory distress despite normal total ventilation and normal total pulmonary blood flow, because the ventilation and blood flow are going to different parts of the lungs. This is the reason the ventilation-perfusion ratio concept was developed. A uniformly thickened membrane impairs diffusion but is not implied by matched normal totals. Loss of hemoglobin's O₂ affinity is not a lung exchange problem. A tidal volume that was entirely dead space would leave no alveolar ventilation, contradicting the normal value. Alveolar PO₂ cannot exceed the 149 mm Hg of humidified inspired air while breathing room air.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal total ventilation and normal total blood flow can still give severe hypoxemia if they go to different lung regions — the reason the VA/Q concept was developed. FA: V/Q mismatch raises the A-a gradient, and a true shunt (V/Q = 0) does not correct with 100% O2. COMLEX: An elevated A-a gradient localizes the problem to the lung — mismatch, shunt or diffusion — rather than to hypoventilation or low inspired O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 7 — the lung abnormalities that lower O2 saturation in pneumonia, a common V/Q mismatch","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q32_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological shunt vs. physiological dead space; implications in COPD and other diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist uses the shunt equation to calculate physiological shunt: Q̇PS/Q̇T = (CiO2 - CaO2)/(CiO2 - Cv̄O2). In the equation, what does CiO2 represent?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen content in mixed venous blood returning to right atrium","B":"Oxygen content in arterial blood measured from radial artery","C":"O2 content if blood were ideally oxygenated (perfect V̇A/Q̇)","D":"Oxygen content in pulmonary capillary blood entering alveoli","E":"Oxygen content in alveolar air expressed as concentration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the shunt equation, CiO2 represents the \"ideal\" oxygen content—the O2 content blood would have if perfectly oxygenated with ideal V̇A/Q̇ matching. CaO2 is actual measured arterial O2 content, and Cv̄O2 is mixed venous O2 content. The numerator (CiO2 - CaO2) represents the \"oxygen deficit\" due to shunting. Dividing by the total arteriovenous difference (CiO2 - Cv̄O2) gives the fraction of cardiac output that's shunted. Normally about 2% of the cardiac output bypasses the alveoli as a physiologic shunt (Costanzo Ch 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shunt equation QPS/QT = (CiO2 − CaO2)/(CiO2 − Cv̄O2), where CiO2 is the O2 content blood would have with ideal V/Q; physiological shunt is measured from mixed venous and arterial O2 content plus cardiac output. COMLEX: FA — in a shunt (V/Q = 0) 100% O2 does not raise PaO2, the bedside clue that separates shunt from simple mismatch.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — O2 transport from alveoli to tissues, including the shunt flow that lowers arterial PO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q33_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological shunt vs. physiological dead space; implications in COPD and other diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old patient with COPD has significant emphysema and chronic bronchitis. Which combination of V̇A/Q̇ abnormalities would be expected?","choices":{"A":"All lung regions have normal V̇A/Q̇ ratios with perfect matching","B":"All regions have V̇A/Q̇ = infinity, creating only dead space","C":"All regions have V̇A/Q̇ = 0, creating only shunt","D":"Mix of shunt-like (V̇A/Q̇ ≈ 0) and dead-space-like (V̇A/Q̇ ≫ 1) regions","E":"All regions have mildly elevated V̇A/Q̇ = 1.5, which is minimally abnormal"},"correct":"D","explanation":"COPD creates heterogeneous V̇A/Q̇ abnormalities: (1) Areas with bronchial obstruction have V̇A/Q̇ ≈ 0 (ventilation blocked but perfusion continues, creating physiological shunt), and (2) Areas with destroyed alveolar walls from emphysema have high V̇A/Q̇ (ventilation continues but capillaries destroyed, creating physiological dead space). This dual pathology severely impairs gas exchange. Some areas produce poorly oxygenated blood (shunt), while other areas waste ventilation (dead space). This reduces lung effectiveness to as little as 10% of normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In chronic obstructive lung disease obstructed bronchioles leave alveoli unventilated (VA/Q near zero, shunt) while destroyed alveolar walls leave ventilation without flow (high VA/Q, dead space); together they can cut lung effectiveness to as little as one-tenth of normal. COMLEX: Supplemental O2 helps the low-V/Q units but cannot fix true shunt, which is why some COPD patients stay hypoxemic despite O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the pink puffer and blue bloater phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_ex1_q34_b1","objective":13,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 13 — Physiological shunt vs. physiological dead space; implications in COPD and other diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonary function laboratory measures both the physiological shunt and the physiological dead space in a 66-year-old man with COPD, whose lungs contain both low and high ventilation-perfusion regions. Which set of measurements does each calculation require?","choices":{"A":"Shunt: arterial and venous PCO₂; dead space: cardiac output and rate","B":"Shunt: O₂ contents, cardiac output; dead space: PaCO₂, expired PCO₂","C":"Shunt: alveolar PO₂ by itself; dead space: tidal volume by itself","D":"Shunt: DLCO and alveolar PCO; dead space: FEV₁ and the FEV₁/FVC","E":"Shunt: hemoglobin and SaO₂; dead space: respiratory rate and VE"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton states that the physiological shunt is measured by analyzing the O₂ concentration in both mixed venous and arterial blood along with simultaneous measurement of cardiac output (Q̇PS/Q̇T = (CiO₂ − CaO₂)/(CiO₂ − Cv̄O₂)), whereas the physiological dead space is measured with the Bohr equation from the arterial PCO₂ and the average PCO₂ of the entire expired air (V̇Dphys/V̇T = (PaCO₂ − P̄eCO₂)/PaCO₂). Arterial and venous PCO₂ are not the shunt inputs, and neither cardiac output nor respiratory rate is part of the Bohr equation. Alveolar PO₂ or tidal volume by itself cannot partition blood flow or ventilation. DLCO measures diffusing capacity and FEV₁/FVC measures airflow obstruction. Hemoglobin, SaO₂, respiratory rate, and minute ventilation (VE) describe oxygen carriage and total ventilation, not the shunt or dead space fraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Physiological shunt = O2 content of mixed venous and arterial blood plus cardiac output (shunt equation); physiological dead space = arterial PCO2 and mixed expired PCO2 (Bohr equation). COMLEX: Guyton notes O2 therapy is of great value in some hypoxias and almost none in others — knowing whether shunt or dead space dominates predicts the response.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — the value of oxygen therapy in the different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":false}],"13":[{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Oxygen transport from alveoli to tissue capillaries, shunt flow, and hemoglobin's role","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with normal hemoglobin concentration (15 g/100 mL blood) has arterial blood with a PO₂ of 95 mm Hg and hemoglobin that is 97% saturated. Her venous blood returning from resting skeletal muscle has a PO₂ of 40 mm Hg with 75% hemoglobin saturation. Approximately how much oxygen is delivered to her tissues per 100 mL of blood flow under these resting conditions?","choices":{"A":"2.5 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","B":"3.5 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","C":"5.0 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","D":"7.5 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","E":"10.0 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The oxygen-carrying capacity of blood with 15 g hemoglobin per 100 mL is approximately 20.1 mL O₂ (15 g × 1.34 mL O₂/g). At 97% saturation, arterial blood carries 19.4 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood. At 75% saturation, venous blood carries 14.4 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood. The difference (19.4 - 14.4 = 5.0 mL) represents oxygen delivered to tissues under normal resting conditions. This 5 mL per 100 mL of blood represents the normal oxygen extraction by tissues and corresponds to a utilization coefficient of approximately 25%. 2.5 mL and 3.5 mL underestimate normal oxygen delivery, while 7.5 mL and 10.0 mL overestimate it and would only occur during heavy exercise when tissue PO₂ drops significantly below 40 mm Hg.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Arterial blood (97% saturated) carries about 19.4 mL O2/100 mL and resting venous blood (PO2 40, 75%) about 14.4, so roughly 5 mL O2 is delivered per 100 mL — a utilization coefficient of about 25%. FA: O2 content = (1.34 × Hb × SaO2) + (0.003 × PaO2). COMLEX: The Fick principle (FA) turns this arteriovenous O2 difference into cardiac output — CO = O2 consumption ÷ (arterial − venous O2 content).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 8 — the factors that shift the oxyhemoglobin curve and change how much O2 tissues extract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Oxygen transport from alveoli to tissue capillaries, shunt flow, and hemoglobin's role","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoes right heart catheterization, and blood samples are obtained from the pulmonary artery and pulmonary vein. The pulmonary artery blood has a PO₂ of 40 mm Hg, while pulmonary vein blood has a PO₂ of 98 mm Hg. However, when arterial blood is sampled from his radial artery, the PO₂ is measured at 95 mm Hg rather than 98 mm Hg. Which physiological mechanism best explains this 3 mm Hg decrease in PO₂?","choices":{"A":"Venous admixture from bronchial circulation shunt flow that bypasses alveolar gas exchange areas","B":"Incomplete oxygen equilibration in the distal third of pulmonary capillaries during blood transit","C":"Oxygen consumption by pulmonary tissue cells as blood flows through the pulmonary veins","D":"Back-diffusion of oxygen from arterial blood into tissues of the left heart and aorta","E":"Methemoglobin formation during transit from pulmonary veins to systemic arteries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"About 2% of cardiac output flows through the bronchial circulation, which supplies the deep tissues of the lungs but does not participate in gas exchange. This blood, called shunt flow, has a PO₂ similar to systemic venous blood (approximately 40 mm Hg). When this deoxygenated shunt blood mixes with fully oxygenated blood from the alveolar capillaries in the pulmonary veins, this venous admixture causes the PO₂ to fall from the alveolar level of 104 mm Hg (and pulmonary capillary blood of ~98-100 mm Hg) to about 95 mm Hg in the systemic arterial blood. Incomplete equilibration is incorrect because oxygen equilibration is nearly complete within the first third of capillary transit time. Pulmonary tissue O₂ consumption is incorrect because oxygen consumption by pulmonary tissues is negligible. Back-diffusion into the left heart is incorrect because oxygen doesn't back-diffuse from arterial blood under normal conditions. Methemoglobin formation is incorrect because methemoglobin formation doesn't occur during normal blood transit and would not cause such a consistent, predictable decrease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 2% of the blood passes through the bronchial circulation, supplying the deep lung tissues without meeting alveolar air; this shunt flow (PO2 near venous) mixes into the pulmonary veins and drops PO2 from about 104 in the pulmonary capillaries to about 95 mm Hg in the aorta. COMLEX: A normal arterial PO2 below alveolar PO2 is therefore expected — the normal A-a gradient FA estimates as age/4 + 4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 6 — bronchial vessels, their systemic origin and their drainage into the pulmonary veins","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Oxygen transport from alveoli to tissue capillaries, shunt flow, and hemoglobin's role","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student studies oxygen transport in the blood and learns that hemoglobin can carry 30 to 100 times as much oxygen as could be transported in dissolved form alone. If a patient has 15 grams of hemoglobin per 100 mL blood and the hemoglobin is 100% saturated, what is the maximum oxygen-carrying capacity of this blood?","choices":{"A":"10.1 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood","B":"15.0 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood","C":"17.5 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood","D":"19.0 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood","E":"20.1 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Each gram of hemoglobin can bind with a maximum of 1.34 mL of oxygen when 100% saturated (the theoretical value for pure hemoglobin is 1.39 mL, but impurities such as methemoglobin reduce this to 1.34 mL in practice). With 15 grams of hemoglobin per 100 mL blood, the maximum oxygen-carrying capacity is 15 × 1.34 = 20.1 mL O₂ per 100 mL blood, usually expressed as 20 volume percent. This demonstrates the critical importance of hemoglobin in oxygen transport—without hemoglobin, only about 0.3 mL of oxygen would be dissolved in 100 mL of blood at normal arterial PO₂ of 95 mm Hg. The other options all underestimate the true oxygen-carrying capacity of blood with normal hemoglobin concentration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Each gram of hemoglobin binds at most 1.34 mL O2, so 15 g/100 mL × 1.34 ≈ 20 mL O2 per 100 mL — the O2-carrying capacity, or about 20 volumes percent. FA: anemia lowers O2 content while SaO2 and PaO2 stay normal. COMLEX: A normal pulse oximetry reading does not exclude poor O2 delivery in a severely anemic patient — content, not saturation, has fallen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide, which lowers O2-carrying capacity while PaO2 stays normal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — CO₂ transport mechanisms in blood and its diffusion from tissues to lungs","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old marathon runner is being studied during exercise physiology testing. Blood samples from his active muscle capillaries show that CO₂ is being transported back to the lungs through multiple chemical mechanisms. Approximately what percentage of total CO₂ is transported in the form of bicarbonate ion after conversion by carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells?","choices":{"A":"20% of total CO₂ transport","B":"70% of total CO₂ transport","C":"50% of total CO₂ transport","D":"30% of total CO₂ transport","E":"10% of total CO₂ transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The bicarbonate mechanism accounts for approximately 70% of CO₂ transport from tissues to lungs, making it the most important CO₂ transport mechanism. Inside red blood cells, carbonic anhydrase catalyzes the reaction of CO₂ with water to form carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which rapidly dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ ions. The H⁺ ions are buffered by hemoglobin, while HCO₃⁻ ions diffuse into plasma (with Cl⁻ moving into RBCs via the chloride shift). This reaction would be too slow to be physiologically important without carbonic anhydrase, which accelerates the reaction rate about 5000-fold. Guyton Ch 41 puts carbamino transport at about 30% (probably no more than 20% in practice, because that reaction is slower) and dissolved CO₂ at about 7%. First Aid 2025 and Costanzo Ch 5 count total blood CO₂ content rather than the amount exchanged and give different shares: about 90% as bicarbonate, about 5% dissolved, and 5% (First Aid) or 3% (Costanzo) as carbamino compounds. The other options significantly underestimate the contribution of the bicarbonate mechanism to CO₂ transport.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton: CO2 converted to bicarbonate inside red cells by carbonic anhydrase carries about 70% of the CO2 moved from tissues to lungs; FA 2025 gives about 90% as HCO3− carried in plasma, with chloride entering red cells in exchange (chloride shift). COMLEX: Because bicarbonate is the main CO2 carrier, ventilation changes reset plasma HCO3− through this same reaction — the basis of acute respiratory acid-base shifts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate and carbaminohemoglobin in CO2 transport","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — CO₂ transport mechanisms in blood and its diffusion from tissues to lungs","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is prescribed acetazolamide as part of a research study. This drug is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor that blocks the enzyme's action in red blood cells. What would be the expected effect on her tissue PCO₂ if carbonic anhydrase were completely blocked?","choices":{"A":"Tissue PCO₂ would remain unchanged at 45 mm Hg due to compensatory mechanisms","B":"Tissue PCO₂ would decrease to approximately 30 mm Hg from enhanced CO₂ diffusion","C":"Tissue PCO₂ would increase to approximately 80 mm Hg from impaired CO₂ transport","D":"Tissue PCO₂ would fluctuate between 40 and 50 mm Hg with respiratory variations","E":"Tissue PCO₂ would decrease to 40 mm Hg matching alveolar levels more closely"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase is essential for efficient CO₂ transport because it accelerates the reaction between CO₂ and water in red blood cells by approximately 5000-fold. Without this enzyme, the formation of carbonic acid (and subsequently bicarbonate) becomes so slow that CO₂ transport from tissues becomes severely impaired. Studies show that when carbonic anhydrase is blocked, tissue PCO₂ can rise from the normal 45 mm Hg to approximately 80 mm Hg or higher. This occurs because CO₂ accumulates in tissues when it cannot be efficiently loaded into the blood for transport to the lungs. The bicarbonate mechanism normally accounts for 70% of CO₂ transport, so its impairment has profound effects. The other options do not reflect the severe impairment of CO₂ transport that occurs when carbonic anhydrase is inhibited.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Carbonic anhydrase speeds the CO2 + H2O reaction about 5000-fold; when acetazolamide blocks it, CO2 transport becomes so poor that tissue PCO2 may rise to 80 mm Hg instead of the normal 45. COMLEX: FA lists acetazolamide as an add-on at altitude, hastening renal HCO3− excretion to compensate for respiratory alkalosis; it is a sulfa drug, so check for sulfa allergy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and their use in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — CO₂ transport mechanisms in blood and its diffusion from tissues to lungs","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns that CO₂ combines with hemoglobin to form carbaminohemoglobin. This reaction involves CO₂ binding directly with amine radicals on the hemoglobin molecule. Using Guyton's accounting of the CO₂ exchanged between tissues and lungs, approximately what percentage of the CO₂ transported is carried as carbaminohemoglobin under normal resting conditions?","choices":{"A":"60% to 70% of total CO₂ transport","B":"50% of total CO₂ transport","C":"Less than 1% of total CO₂ transport","D":"20% to 30% of total CO₂ transport","E":"45% of total CO₂ transport"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton Ch 41 puts carbamino transport (with hemoglobin and plasma proteins) at about 30% of the CO₂ carried from tissues to lungs, but because this reaction is slower than the bicarbonate reaction it probably carries no more than 20% in practice. CO₂ reacts directly with amine radicals (-NH₂ groups) on hemoglobin to form carbaminohemoglobin (CO₂Hgb). This is a reversible reaction with a loose bond, allowing CO₂ to be easily released in the lungs where PCO₂ is lower. A small amount of CO₂ also forms carbamino compounds with plasma proteins, but this is less significant because plasma protein concentration is only one-fourth that of hemoglobin. The remaining CO₂ is transported as bicarbonate (about 70%) and dissolved in plasma (about 7%). First Aid 2025 and Costanzo Ch 5 count total blood CO₂ content rather than the amount exchanged and give different shares: about 90% as bicarbonate, about 5% dissolved, and 5% (First Aid) or 3% (Costanzo) as carbamino compounds. The other options either overestimate or underestimate the contribution of the carbaminohemoglobin mechanism; less than 1% falls below every source's figure, including the 3% (Costanzo) and 5% (First Aid) that count total blood CO₂ content.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 binds amine radicals of hemoglobin to form carbaminohemoglobin; Guyton puts carbamino transport (hemoglobin plus plasma proteins) at about 30% in theory but probably no more than 20% in practice because the reaction is slow; FA 2025 gives about 5% as carbamino compounds. COMLEX: Oxygenation in the lungs displaces CO2 from hemoglobin (Haldane effect), so CO2 unloading depends on adequate alveolar O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 7 — the Haldane effect, in which O2 binding displaces carbamino-bound CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Partial pressure gradients driving O₂ and CO₂ movement","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is reviewing oxygen partial pressure gradients throughout the cardiopulmonary system. When blood enters the pulmonary capillary at the arterial end, the PO₂ of venous blood is 40 mm Hg, while alveolar PO₂ is 104 mm Hg. What is the initial pressure difference that drives oxygen diffusion into the pulmonary capillary blood?","choices":{"A":"40 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into blood","B":"64 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into blood","C":"95 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into blood","D":"104 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into blood","E":"54 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into blood"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The initial driving pressure for oxygen diffusion from alveoli into pulmonary capillary blood is 104 - 40 = 64 mm Hg. This large pressure gradient causes rapid oxygen uptake, and the blood PO₂ rises almost to equal alveolar PO₂ (104 mm Hg) by the time blood has moved only one-third of the distance through the pulmonary capillary. This provides a large safety factor for oxygenation—the blood normally stays in the lung capillaries about three times as long as needed for full oxygenation. During exercise, even though capillary transit time is reduced to less than half normal, the blood still becomes almost fully saturated because of this safety margin and the increase in diffusing capacity that occurs with exercise. The other options represent incorrect calculations or misconceptions about the pressure gradient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alveolar PO2 about 104 minus entering venous PO2 about 40 gives an initial 64 mm Hg gradient; blood PO2 rises almost to alveolar PO2 within the first third of the capillary. COMLEX: FA — in healthy lungs O2 is perfusion limited, so uptake rises only with more blood flow; it becomes diffusion limited in emphysema and fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — the partial pressure gradients for O2, CO2 and N2 that set the direction of diffusion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Partial pressure gradients driving O₂ and CO₂ movement","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with peripheral vascular disease has reduced blood flow to his lower extremities. In a region where blood flow has decreased to one-quarter of normal, his interstitial fluid PO₂ drops significantly. Assuming normal oxygen consumption by the tissues, what would be the approximate interstitial fluid PO₂ with blood flow at 25% of normal?","choices":{"A":"80 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","B":"40 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","C":"60 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","D":"12 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","E":"95 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tissue PO₂ is determined by the balance between oxygen delivery (dependent on blood flow) and oxygen consumption. With normal blood flow, interstitial fluid PO₂ is approximately 40 mm Hg. When blood flow decreases to one-quarter of normal (25%) while oxygen consumption remains constant, the tissue PO₂ drops significantly because less oxygen is being delivered per unit time. Based on the relationship between blood flow and tissue PO₂, a decrease to 25% of normal flow reduces interstitial PO₂ to roughly 12 mm Hg (Guyton Fig 41-4, point C on the normal-O₂-consumption curve at 25% of normal flow). This reduced PO₂ can still support cellular metabolism since only 1-3 mm Hg is required for full support of oxidative processes, but it represents a critically low level with minimal safety margin. 40 mm Hg represents normal tissue PO₂ with normal blood flow. 80 mm Hg, 60 mm Hg, and 95 mm Hg would require increased blood flow above normal, which contradicts the clinical scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial PO2 is set by the balance of O2 delivery (blood flow) and O2 use; at constant metabolism, falling flow lowers tissue PO2 below the normal 40 mm Hg. COMLEX: Guyton classes localized circulatory deficiency (peripheral, cerebral, coronary) as a cause of hypoxia from inadequate O2 transport — and O2 therapy is of much less value there because alveolar O2 is already normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — the classification of hypoxia, including localized circulatory deficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Partial pressure gradients driving O₂ and CO₂ movement","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher measures CO₂ partial pressures at various points in the circulation. In a tissue cell that is actively metabolizing oxygen, the intracellular PCO₂ is measured at 46 mm Hg. What is the approximate PCO₂ in the surrounding interstitial fluid?","choices":{"A":"40 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","B":"50 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","C":"35 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","D":"45 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","E":"46 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The PCO₂ in interstitial fluid is approximately 45 mm Hg, only 1 mm Hg less than the intracellular PCO₂ of 46 mm Hg. This small pressure gradient is sufficient to drive CO₂ diffusion from cells into the interstitial fluid because CO₂ diffuses approximately 20 times more rapidly than oxygen through biological membranes. The high diffusion rate of CO₂ means that only minimal pressure differences are required for adequate CO₂ transport at each step of the gas exchange process. From the interstitial fluid (45 mm Hg), CO₂ diffuses into tissue capillary blood, raising the PCO₂ from the arterial value of 40 mm Hg to the venous value of 45 mm Hg. 40 mm Hg and 35 mm Hg represent arterial or alveolar PCO₂ levels. 50 mm Hg overestimates the interstitial PCO₂. 46 mm Hg incorrectly suggests no gradient exists between intracellular and interstitial spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intracellular PCO2 is about 46 and interstitial about 45 mm Hg — only a 1 mm Hg difference, enough because CO2 diffuses about 20 times as rapidly as O2. COMLEX: FA — CO2 is perfusion limited; tissue CO2 removal therefore depends on blood flow far more than on diffusion distance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — why CO2 outdiffuses O2 (solubility versus pressure gradient)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Exercise effects on oxygen demand and diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old competitive cyclist is exercising at maximal effort. During heavy exercise, the interstitial fluid PO₂ in his active leg muscles drops from the normal resting value of 40 mm Hg to 15 mm Hg. How much oxygen is delivered to the tissues per 100 mL of blood flow under these conditions compared to the normal 5 mL delivered at rest?","choices":{"A":"7.5 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","B":"10.0 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","C":"12.5 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","D":"13.0 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood","E":"15.0 mL of oxygen per 100 mL blood"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During heavy exercise when tissue PO₂ falls to 15 mm Hg, the hemoglobin saturation drops to approximately 22%, leaving only 4.4 mL of oxygen bound to hemoglobin per 100 mL blood (compared to 14.4 mL in resting venous blood). Starting with arterial blood containing 19.4 mL oxygen per 100 mL, the amount delivered to tissues is 19.4 - 4.4 = 15 mL per 100 mL of blood flow. This represents a three-fold increase compared to the normal 5 mL delivered at rest. When combined with the six- to seven-fold increase in cardiac output that can occur in well-trained athletes, total oxygen delivery to tissues can increase approximately 20-fold during maximal exercise (3-fold increase per volume of blood × 6-7 fold increase in cardiac output = ~20-fold total increase). The other options underestimate the dramatic increase in oxygen extraction that occurs during heavy exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In heavy exercise muscle interstitial PO2 can fall from 40 to as low as 15 mm Hg, leaving only 4.4 mL O2 bound per 100 mL, so about 15 mL is delivered — three times resting; the whole-body utilization coefficient can rise to 75–85%. COMLEX: FA — exercise widens the arteriovenous O2 difference while PaO2 and PaCO2 stay normal, so a falling PaO2 during exercise testing is abnormal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 9 — exercise adaptations that enhance O2 transport","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Exercise effects on oxygen demand and diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman undergoes exercise stress testing. During strenuous exercise, her cardiac output increases to six times normal, and her pulmonary blood spends less than one-half the normal time in the pulmonary capillaries. Despite this reduced capillary transit time, her arterial blood PO₂ remains at 95 mm Hg. Which physiological adaptation best explains why blood oxygenation remains adequate during exercise?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary capillaries dilate to increase transit time back to normal resting values","B":"Increased diffusing capacity and the safety factor of three-fold excess capillary time at rest","C":"Decreased oxygen consumption by exercising muscles preserves arterial oxygen saturation","D":"Enhanced hemoglobin oxygen-binding affinity during exercise maintains saturation levels","E":"Bronchial shunt flow decreases during exercise to minimize venous admixture effects"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Two key factors explain adequate oxygenation during exercise despite reduced capillary transit time. First, the diffusing capacity for oxygen increases almost three-fold during exercise due to increased surface area of participating capillaries and more ideal V/Q ratios in the upper lungs. Second, there is normally a large safety factor—blood becomes almost saturated with oxygen by the time it has passed through only one-third of the pulmonary capillary length. This means blood stays in lung capillaries about three times longer than needed for full oxygenation at rest. During exercise, even with capillary transit time reduced to less than half normal, the blood can still become almost fully oxygenated. Capillary dilation is incorrect because capillary dilation doesn't significantly extend transit time when flow velocity increases dramatically. Decreased muscle O₂ consumption is incorrect because oxygen consumption increases, not decreases, during exercise. Increased hemoglobin affinity is incorrect because the Bohr effect actually shifts the curve right during exercise. Decreased bronchial shunt is incorrect because bronchial shunt flow doesn't significantly change during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two reserves keep exercising blood oxygenated — O2 diffusing capacity rises almost 3-fold (more capillary surface, better apical V/Q), and blood normally stays in the capillary about three times as long as needed, saturating in the first third. COMLEX: When diffusion is impaired (fibrosis, edema), this transit-time reserve is lost first, so desaturation appears with exertion before rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest versus exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Exercise effects on oxygen demand and diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old marathon runner completes a race. During peak exercise, his oxygen consumption increased approximately 20-fold above resting levels. This 20-fold increase resulted from the combination of increased oxygen extraction per volume of blood and increased cardiac output. If oxygen delivery per 100 mL of blood increased 3-fold and total oxygen consumption increased 20-fold, approximately how much did his cardiac output increase?","choices":{"A":"3-fold increase in cardiac output","B":"5-fold increase in cardiac output","C":"6 to 7-fold increase in cardiac output","D":"10-fold increase in cardiac output","E":"20-fold increase in cardiac output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The total increase in oxygen delivery to tissues is the product of: (1) increased oxygen extraction per volume of blood, and (2) increased blood flow (cardiac output). During heavy exercise, oxygen extraction can increase approximately 3-fold (from 5 mL to 15 mL per 100 mL blood) when tissue PO₂ drops to 15 mm Hg. If total oxygen consumption increases 20-fold and oxygen extraction per volume of blood increases 3-fold, then cardiac output must increase by 20 ÷ 3 ≈ 6.7-fold. Well-trained endurance athletes can increase cardiac output from a resting value of about 5 L/min to 30-35 L/min during maximal exercise, representing a 6- to 7-fold increase. This demonstrates the remarkable capacity of the cardiovascular system to meet increased metabolic demands during exercise. 3-fold and 5-fold underestimate the cardiac output increase, while 10-fold and 20-fold overestimate it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Total O2 delivery = O2 extracted per volume of blood × blood flow; cardiac output can rise six- to sevenfold in trained marathon runners and extraction about 3-fold, giving roughly 20-fold more O2 to tissues. COMLEX: FA — early in exercise cardiac output rises through heart rate and stroke volume, later through heart rate alone once stroke volume plateaus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 9 — how the pulmonary circulation carries a several-fold rise in flow during heavy exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Blood flow variations and their effects on tissue oxygenation and CO₂ removal","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with congestive heart failure has reduced cardiac output, resulting in decreased blood flow to peripheral tissues. In a tissue where blood flow has decreased to one-quarter of normal while metabolic rate remains constant, what happens to the interstitial fluid PCO₂?","choices":{"A":"PCO₂ decreases from 45 to 40 mm Hg","B":"PCO₂ increases from 45 to 60 mm Hg","C":"PCO₂ increases from 45 to 50 mm Hg","D":"PCO₂ remains stable at 45 mm Hg","E":"PCO₂ decreases from 45 to 35 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tissue PCO₂ is determined by the balance between CO₂ production (metabolic rate) and CO₂ removal (blood flow). When blood flow decreases to one-quarter (25%) of normal while metabolism remains constant, CO₂ accumulates in the tissues because it cannot be removed as efficiently. A decrease in blood flow from normal (point A) to one-quarter normal (point B) increases peripheral tissue PCO₂ from the normal value of 45 mm Hg to an elevated level of approximately 60 mm Hg. This represents a significant increase of 15 mm Hg. The relationship is inverse—as blood flow decreases, tissue PCO₂ increases, and vice versa. Conversely, increasing blood flow to six times normal can decrease interstitial PCO₂ to 41 mm Hg, almost equal to arterial blood (40 mm Hg). Falling to 40 mm Hg, stable at 45 mm Hg, and falling to 35 mm Hg incorrectly show PCO₂ decreasing or remaining stable when flow decreases. Rising to 50 mm Hg underestimates the magnitude of PCO₂ increase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tissue PCO2 reflects CO2 production versus removal by blood flow; cutting flow to one-quarter of normal raises interstitial PCO2 from 45 to about 60 mm Hg. COMLEX: FA — rising tissue CO2 and H+ shift the oxyhemoglobin curve right (Bohr effect), partly offsetting poor flow by unloading more O2 in low-output states.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 8 — factors such as CO2 and H+ that shift the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Blood flow variations and their effects on tissue oxygenation and CO₂ removal","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman's forearm blood flow is experimentally increased to 400% of normal (4-fold increase) while her arm remains at rest with normal metabolic rate. What would be the expected change in her forearm interstitial fluid PO₂?","choices":{"A":"Increases from 40 mm Hg to approximately 66 mm Hg in the interstitial fluid","B":"Remains stable at 40 mm Hg despite increased blood flow to the region","C":"Increases from 40 mm Hg to 95 mm Hg matching arterial blood values","D":"Decreases from 40 mm Hg to 25 mm Hg from enhanced oxygen extraction","E":"Increases from 40 mm Hg to 104 mm Hg matching alveolar oxygen levels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increasing blood flow delivers more oxygen to tissues per unit time, which raises tissue PO₂. When blood flow increases from normal (point A) to 400% of normal (4-fold, point B), the interstitial fluid PO₂ increases from the normal 40 mm Hg to approximately 66 mm Hg. However, there is an upper limit to this increase—even with maximal blood flow, tissue PO₂ cannot rise above 95 mm Hg because this is the PO₂ of arterial blood entering the tissue. The relationship between blood flow and tissue PO₂ follows a curve that plateaus as flow increases. The increased tissue PO₂ with increased flow represents a higher oxygen reservoir available for cellular metabolism if metabolic rate increases. Stable at 40 mm Hg is incorrect because tissue PO₂ does increase with increased flow. Rising to 95 mm Hg and rising to 104 mm Hg exceed the physiological maximum (arterial PO₂). Falling to 25 mm Hg incorrectly shows a decrease when flow increases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Raising flow to 400% of normal raises interstitial PO2 from 40 to about 66 mm Hg — more flow brings tissue PO2 closer to arterial, but flow alone has an upper limit. COMLEX: Guyton — O2 therapy is of much less value in circulatory hypoxia, yet the 7–30% extra O2 carried dissolved at maximal alveolar PO2 may be the difference between life and death.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — the value of oxygen therapy in the different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Blood flow variations and their effects on tissue oxygenation and CO₂ removal","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old man performs intense forearm exercise, increasing his forearm muscle metabolic rate 10-fold above normal. Assuming blood flow remains constant, what would be the expected effect on interstitial fluid PCO₂ in the exercising muscles?","choices":{"A":"PCO₂ would remain at 45 mm Hg due to buffering","B":"PCO₂ would decrease toward 40 mm Hg from hyperventilation","C":"PCO₂ would greatly elevate above 45 mm Hg at all blood flow rates","D":"PCO₂ would decrease to 35 mm Hg from enhanced diffusion","E":"PCO₂ would increase to 50 mm Hg from mild accumulation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A 10-fold increase in tissue metabolic rate dramatically elevates interstitial fluid PCO₂ at all rates of blood flow. When metabolism increases while blood flow remains constant, CO₂ production increases much faster than CO₂ can be removed by the blood. This causes significant CO₂ accumulation in the tissues. Even with increased blood flow, a 10-fold metabolic increase still results in markedly elevated tissue PCO₂. This represents a profound disturbance in the normal balance between CO₂ production and removal. In reality, the increased PCO₂ and decreased PO₂ in exercising muscle would trigger local vasodilation to increase blood flow, which would partially compensate for the increased metabolic rate. Staying at 45 mm Hg and falling toward 40 mm Hg incorrectly suggest no change or a decrease. Falling to 35 mm Hg and rising only to 50 mm Hg significantly underestimate the magnitude of PCO₂ elevation with such a dramatic increase in metabolism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A 10-fold rise in metabolic rate greatly elevates interstitial PCO2 at every blood flow, whereas cutting metabolism to one-quarter lets it fall toward the arterial value. COMLEX: FA — in exercise the extra tissue CO2 and acid shift the oxyhemoglobin curve right and ventilation rises to remove the excess CO2, keeping arterial PCO2 normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 11 — chemical and nervous factors that match ventilation to exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Effects of pressure gradients, metabolic rates, and capillary transit times on gas diffusion","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns that intracellular PO₂ must remain above a critical minimum level to support cellular oxidative metabolism. What is the approximate minimum intracellular PO₂ required for full support of the respiratory enzyme systems in cells?","choices":{"A":"1 to 3 mm Hg is the minimum required for full metabolic support","B":"10 to 15 mm Hg is the minimum required for full metabolic support","C":"20 to 25 mm Hg is the minimum required for full metabolic support","D":"35 to 40 mm Hg is the minimum required for full metabolic support","E":"45 to 50 mm Hg is the minimum required for full metabolic support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The respiratory enzyme systems of cells require only 1 to 3 mm Hg of oxygen pressure for full support of intracellular chemical reactions. Above this minimal level, oxygen availability is no longer a limiting factor—instead, the main limiting factor becomes the concentration of ADP in the cells. Normal intracellular PO₂ ranges from as low as 5 mm Hg to as high as 40 mm Hg, averaging about 23 mm Hg in peripheral tissues. This means that even the lowest normal intracellular PO₂ provides a large safety factor (5 mm Hg is well above the 1-3 mm Hg minimum requirement). The respiratory enzymes have been configured through evolution to function efficiently at very low oxygen tensions, which allows cells to extract oxygen effectively even when delivery is limited. The other options all overestimate the minimum PO₂ required for cellular metabolism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intracellular PO2 ranges from about 5 to 40 mm Hg (average about 23), yet only 1–3 mm Hg is needed to fully support the cell's oxidative enzymes, leaving a wide margin. COMLEX: Guyton's classic failure of O2 use despite adequate supply is cyanide blocking cytochrome oxidase — O2 therapy gives no measurable benefit in that type of hypoxia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — causes of hypoxia, including inadequate tissue capability to use O2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Effects of pressure gradients, metabolic rates, and capillary transit times on gas diffusion","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with severe peripheral arterial disease has compromised blood flow to her lower leg muscles. Under these conditions, tissue cells are located more than 50 micrometers from the nearest capillary, and intracellular PO₂ falls below 1 mm Hg in some cells. Under these circumstances, what becomes the rate-limiting factor for oxygen consumption by the affected cells?","choices":{"A":"The concentration of ADP within the cells limits oxygen consumption rate","B":"The activity of mitochondrial respiratory enzymes limits oxygen consumption","C":"The rate of hemoglobin oxygen release in nearby capillaries limits consumption","D":"The rate of oxygen diffusion from capillary to cell limits oxygen consumption","E":"The partial pressure of oxygen in arterial blood limits cellular consumption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normally, tissue cells are within 50 micrometers of a capillary, allowing adequate oxygen diffusion to maintain intracellular PO₂ above the critical 1-3 mm Hg needed for metabolism. When cells are located farther from capillaries or when blood flow is severely reduced, the rate of oxygen diffusion to the cells can become so low that intracellular PO₂ falls below this critical level. Under these conditions, oxygen usage becomes diffusion-limited rather than being determined by the amount of ADP formed in cells (which is the normal regulatory mechanism). This represents a pathological state that cannot continue for long because cells cannot survive without adequate oxygen. ADP concentration describes the normal regulatory mechanism when oxygen supply is adequate. Mitochondrial enzyme activity, hemoglobin O₂ release, and arterial PO₂ do not describe the actual limiting factor when diffusion distance is excessive.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cells are seldom more than 50 micrometers from a capillary; if they lie farther away, O2 diffusion can fall so low that intracellular PO2 drops below the critical level and diffusion, not cell demand, limits O2 use. COMLEX: Guyton lists tissue edema among causes of inadequate O2 transport — swelling widens the capillary-to-cell distance in the same way.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 13 — the physiological consequences of systemic hypoxia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Effects of pressure gradients, metabolic rates, and capillary transit times on gas diffusion","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old man undergoes assessment of tissue oxygen delivery. His physician explains that tissue oxygen availability depends on both the oxygen content of blood and the rate of blood flow. Under what circumstances does oxygen consumption by tissues become blood flow-limited rather than diffusion-limited?","choices":{"A":"When intracellular PO₂ rises above 40 mm Hg from enhanced diffusion capacity","B":"When blood flow rate decreases to the point that tissue PO₂ falls below 1 mm Hg","C":"When hemoglobin saturation exceeds 97% in arterial blood entering the tissues","D":"When tissue metabolic rate increases above normal resting levels during exercise","E":"When capillary density increases with angiogenesis in chronically hypoxic tissues"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tissue oxygen consumption becomes blood flow-limited when blood flow rate through a tissue becomes so low that tissue PO₂ falls below the critical 1 mm Hg required for maintaining intracellular metabolism. The total amount of oxygen available each minute for use in tissue is determined by: (1) the quantity of oxygen transported to the tissue in each 100 mL of blood, and (2) the rate of blood flow. If blood flow falls to zero, oxygen availability also falls to zero. When tissue PO₂ drops below 1 mm Hg, cells cannot maintain adequate ATP production, and metabolism becomes limited by oxygen delivery via blood flow rather than by diffusion or ADP concentration. This represents a severe pathological condition that cannot be sustained. Intracellular PO₂ above 40 mm Hg describes the opposite situation with excess oxygen. Saturation above 97% describes normal arterial saturation, which doesn't cause flow limitation. Exercise-level metabolism describes a situation where metabolism increases but doesn't necessarily become flow-limited. Angiogenesis describes a compensatory mechanism that would improve oxygen delivery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Above about 1 mm Hg intracellular PO2, O2 use is set by cell demand; when flow falls so far that tissue PO2 drops below that level, O2 use becomes blood-flow limited — total O2 available each minute is O2 per volume of blood × flow. COMLEX: Ischemic tissue is the clinical picture — restoring perfusion helps more than raising inspired O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — why O2 therapy is of much less value when circulatory deficiency limits delivery","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q19_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Oxygen transport from alveoli to tissue capillaries, shunt flow, and hemoglobin's role","batch":1,"stem":"A biochemistry student calculates that chemically pure hemoglobin can bind 1.39 mL of O₂ per gram, but her physiology text uses 1.34 mL/g when computing the O₂ capacity of a patient's blood. Why does Guyton use the lower value for blood?","choices":{"A":"Each hemoglobin molecule binds two O₂ in vivo","B":"Plasma proteins compete with hemoglobin for O₂","C":"Impurities like methemoglobin lower the O₂ bound","D":"Red cell 2,3-BPG lowers the maximum number of sites","E":"Arterial blood is normally just 75% saturated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton notes that each gram of hemoglobin can bind a maximum of 1.34 mL of O₂, or 1.39 mL when the hemoglobin is chemically pure, because impurities such as methemoglobin reduce the amount of O₂ bound; 15 g/100 mL × 1.34 gives about 20 mL, or 20 volume percent. Hemoglobin is a tetramer whose four subunits each bind one O₂ (First Aid 2025), so the site count does not fall to two in the body. Plasma proteins do not bind O₂; the small dissolved fraction rides in plasma water. 2,3-BPG shifts the dissociation curve to the right, lowering affinity, but it does not reduce the maximum O₂ each gram can hold at full saturation. Arterial blood is about 97% saturated; 75% is the value for venous blood, and saturation in any case does not change the per-gram maximum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pure hemoglobin binds 1.39 mL O2/g, but impurities such as methemoglobin lower blood's practical value to 1.34 mL/g, giving about 20 volumes percent at 15 g/100 mL. FA: methemoglobin's ferric iron (Fe3+) binds O2 poorly, lowering saturation and content while PaO2 stays normal; typical levels are 1–2%. COMLEX: Methemoglobinemia (dapsone, benzocaine, nitrites) gives cyanosis that does not improve with O2 and chocolate-colored blood; treat with methylene blue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 11 — carbon monoxide, another hemoglobin abnormality that lowers O2 content with a normal PaO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q20_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Oxygen transport from alveoli to tissue capillaries, shunt flow, and hemoglobin's role","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a large intracardiac right-to-left shunt (due to uncorrected congenital heart disease) has 15% of his cardiac output bypassing the lungs entirely. His pulmonary capillary blood is fully oxygenated with PO₂ of 104 mm Hg. Compared with the normal arterial PO₂ of about 95 mm Hg, what arterial PO₂ would you expect after mixing of shunted and oxygenated blood?","choices":{"A":"Below 95 mm Hg, as the larger admixture dilutes oxygenated blood","B":"104 mm Hg, as most of the blood is fully oxygenated in the lungs","C":"About 40 mm Hg, matching the mixed venous blood being shunted","D":"Above 104 mm Hg, as the lungs hyperoxygenate the non-shunted blood","E":"About 95 mm Hg, the value the normal 2% shunt already produces"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton Ch 41: normally about 2% of the blood is bronchial shunt flow with a PO₂ of about 40 mm Hg, and this venous admixture lowers PO₂ from the pulmonary capillary value of about 104 mm Hg to about 95 mm Hg in the aorta. Costanzo Ch 5: in a right-to-left shunt hypoxemia always occurs, because the oxygenated blood is diluted by the low-O₂ shunted blood. With 15% instead of 2% of the cardiac output bypassing the lungs, the admixture is much larger, so arterial PO₂ falls below 95 mm Hg; the exact value depends on the mixed venous O₂ and the dissociation curve, and the sources give no single number for it. Staying at 104 mm Hg ignores the shunt; 40 mm Hg would require all of the blood to bypass the lungs; pulmonary capillary blood equilibrates with alveolar gas at about 104 mm Hg and cannot be pushed above it; and 95 mm Hg is the value produced by the normal 2% shunt alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal 2% bronchial shunt flow already lowers PO2 from about 104 (pulmonary capillary) to about 95 mm Hg (aorta); any larger right-to-left admixture drops arterial PO2 further. FA: a right-to-left shunt is the extreme of V/Q mismatch, raises the A-a gradient and does not correct with 100% O2. COMLEX: Failure of PaO2 to rise on 100% O2 is the bedside clue to an anatomic shunt such as uncorrected congenital heart disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt versus physiological dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q21_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — CO₂ transport mechanisms in blood and its diffusion from tissues to lungs","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman's blood sample is analyzed while she is at rest. Under normal conditions, approximately 4 mL of CO₂ is transported from the tissues to the lungs in each 100 mL of blood. What percentage of this CO₂ is transported in the dissolved state in the plasma and intracellular fluid?","choices":{"A":"23% is transported in dissolved form","B":"7% is transported in dissolved form","C":"70% is transported in dissolved form","D":"50% is transported in dissolved form","E":"15% is transported in dissolved form"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Only about 7% of total CO₂ transport occurs in the dissolved molecular form. At the normal venous PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg, about 2.7 mL of CO₂ is dissolved per 100 mL of blood, and at the arterial PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg, about 2.4 mL is dissolved. The difference of 0.3 mL represents the dissolved CO₂ transported per 100 mL of blood flow. Compared to the total 4 mL of CO₂ transported, this 0.3 mL represents only about 7% of total CO₂ transport. The vast majority of CO₂ is transported as bicarbonate (about 70%) and carbamino compounds (about 20–30%) (Guyton Ch 41). First Aid 2025 and Costanzo Ch 5 count total blood CO₂ content rather than the amount exchanged and give different shares: about 90% as bicarbonate, about 5% dissolved, and 5% (First Aid) or 3% (Costanzo) as carbamino compounds. This is in stark contrast to oxygen, where the dissolved form contributes only about 3% of total transport. The other options overestimate the contribution of dissolved CO₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton: dissolved CO2 falls from about 2.7 mL/dL at venous PCO2 45 to 2.4 at arterial 40, so about 0.3 mL of the roughly 4 mL exchanged per 100 mL — about 7% — travels dissolved; FA 2025 gives about 5% dissolved in plasma. COMLEX: Because CO2 is about 20 times as soluble as O2, its dissolved share is far larger than O2's, which is only a few percent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — the bicarbonate and carbamino routes that carry the rest of the CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q22_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — CO₂ transport mechanisms in blood and its diffusion from tissues to lungs","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with normal lung function undergoes blood gas analysis. Blood samples show venous PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg with total CO₂ content of 52 volume percent, and arterial PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg with total CO₂ content of 48 volume percent. How much CO₂ is exchanged during normal transport from tissues to lungs per 100 mL of blood?","choices":{"A":"10 volume percent of CO₂ is exchanged","B":"8 volume percent of CO₂ is exchanged","C":"2 volume percent of CO₂ is exchanged","D":"4 volume percent of CO₂ is exchanged","E":"1 volume percent of CO₂ is exchanged"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The CO₂ dissociation curve shows that normal blood contains about 50 volume percent of CO₂ in all its different forms (dissolved, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin). As blood passes through the tissues, the CO₂ content rises to about 52 volume percent (at PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg in venous blood). As blood passes through the lungs, the CO₂ content falls to about 48 volume percent (at PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg in arterial blood). Therefore, 52 - 48 = 4 volume percent of CO₂ is exchanged during normal transport from tissues to lungs. This corresponds to approximately 4 mL of CO₂ per 100 mL of blood flow under normal resting conditions. The narrow range of normal blood PCO₂ (40-45 mm Hg) reflects the efficient CO₂ transport mechanisms and the body's tight regulation of acid-base balance. 10 volume percent and 8 volume percent overestimate the amount exchanged, while 2 volume percent and 1 volume percent underestimate it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal blood holds about 50 volumes percent CO2 in all forms; it rises to about 52 in the tissues and falls to about 48 in the lungs, so only about 4 volumes percent is exchanged per pass. COMLEX: Because only this small fraction turns over, a modest fall in alveolar ventilation quickly raises PaCO2 — FA's link between hypoventilation and hypercapnia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 7 — the Haldane effect, which shifts the CO2 dissociation curve as hemoglobin is oxygenated","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q23_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — CO₂ transport mechanisms in blood and its diffusion from tissues to lungs","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies the Haldane effect in blood samples. When oxygen binds to hemoglobin in the pulmonary capillaries, it causes hemoglobin to become a stronger acid, which displaces CO₂ from the blood. Approximately how much does the Haldane effect increase the amount of CO₂ released from blood in the lungs?","choices":{"A":"The Haldane effect increases CO₂ release by 25%","B":"The Haldane effect increases CO₂ release by 50%","C":"The Haldane effect increases CO₂ release by 75%","D":"The Haldane effect roughly doubles CO₂ release","E":"The Haldane effect increases CO₂ release by 10%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Haldane effect approximately doubles both the amount of CO₂ released from blood in the lungs and the amount of CO₂ picked up from the tissues. When O₂ binds to hemoglobin in the lungs, it makes hemoglobin more acidic through two mechanisms: (1) increased acidity reduces carbaminohemoglobin formation, displacing CO₂ that was bound to hemoglobin; (2) the excess H⁺ ions released by the more acidic hemoglobin bind with HCO₃⁻ to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into water and CO₂. If the Haldane effect didn't occur, only about 2 volume percent of CO₂ would be released as PCO₂ drops from 45 to 40 mm Hg. With the Haldane effect shifting the CO₂ dissociation curve, an additional 2 volume percent is released, bringing the total to 4 volume percent. This effect is quantitatively far more important for CO₂ transport than the Bohr effect is for O₂ transport. The other options underestimate the magnitude of the Haldane effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Haldane effect — O2 binding makes hemoglobin a stronger acid, displacing carbamino CO2 and releasing H+ that drives bicarbonate back to CO2; it roughly doubles CO2 released in the lungs and CO2 picked up in tissues. FA: in the lungs, Hb oxygenation promotes H+ dissociation and CO2 release from red cells. COMLEX: Pair it with the Bohr effect for exams — Bohr is CO2/H+ lowering O2 affinity in tissues, Haldane is O2 lowering CO2 binding in the lungs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 7 — the Bohr and Haldane effects in O2 and CO2 transport","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q24_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Partial pressure gradients driving O₂ and CO₂ movement","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old healthy woman has blood drawn from a peripheral vein in her arm while at rest. The venous blood shows a PO₂ of 40 mm Hg and a PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg. What are the corresponding values in the interstitial fluid surrounding her tissue cells?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial PO₂ of 23 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 46 mm Hg","B":"Interstitial PO₂ of 40 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg","C":"Interstitial PO₂ of 95 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg","D":"Interstitial PO₂ of 60 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 35 mm Hg","E":"Interstitial PO₂ of 104 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under normal resting conditions, the interstitial fluid PO₂ averages 40 mm Hg and the interstitial PCO₂ averages 45 mm Hg, which are the same values found in venous blood leaving the tissue capillaries. This equilibration occurs because the capillary blood comes almost exactly to equilibrium with the interstitial fluid as it passes through the tissues. For oxygen, there is initially a 55 mm Hg gradient (arterial blood at 95 mm Hg entering tissues with interstitial fluid at 40 mm Hg), which drives rapid O₂ diffusion into tissues. For CO₂, there is initially a 5 mm Hg gradient (arterial blood at 40 mm Hg entering tissues with interstitial fluid at 45 mm Hg), which drives CO₂ diffusion into blood. By the time blood exits as venous blood, both gases have equilibrated with interstitial values. PO₂ 23/PCO₂ 46 mm Hg shows intracellular values. PO₂ 95/PCO₂ 40, PO₂ 60/PCO₂ 35, and PO₂ 104/PCO₂ 40 show various incorrect arterial or alveolar values.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Capillary blood equilibrates almost exactly with interstitial fluid, so venous blood leaving resting tissue carries the interstitial values — PO2 about 40 and PCO2 about 45 mm Hg. COMLEX: A peripheral venous sample therefore reports the tissue it drains, not lung oxygenation; arterial PO2 (about 95) is what reflects gas exchange.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 2 — the O2 and CO2 partial pressure gradients between alveoli, blood and tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q25_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Partial pressure gradients driving O₂ and CO₂ movement","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns that CO₂ diffuses much more rapidly than O₂ through biological membranes and tissue. This allows CO₂ transport to occur with much smaller pressure gradients. Approximately what is the pressure difference required to drive CO₂ diffusion from tissue cells (PCO₂ = 46 mm Hg) into the surrounding interstitial fluid (PCO₂ = 45 mm Hg)?","choices":{"A":"Only a 1 mm Hg gradient is required for adequate CO₂ diffusion from cells","B":"A 5 mm Hg gradient is required for adequate CO₂ diffusion from cells","C":"A 10 mm Hg gradient is required for adequate CO₂ diffusion from cells","D":"A 20 mm Hg gradient is required for adequate CO₂ diffusion from cells","E":"A 40 mm Hg gradient is required for adequate CO₂ diffusion from cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CO₂ diffuses approximately 20 times more rapidly than O₂ through biological membranes, which means that only very small pressure gradients are required for adequate CO₂ transport. The pressure difference between intracellular PCO₂ (46 mm Hg) and interstitial fluid PCO₂ (45 mm Hg) is only 1 mm Hg, yet this tiny gradient is sufficient to drive all necessary CO₂ diffusion from cells into interstitial fluid. Similarly, the gradient from interstitial fluid (45 mm Hg) to arterial blood entering tissues (40 mm Hg) is only 5 mm Hg, and the gradient from venous blood (45 mm Hg) to alveolar air (40 mm Hg) is also only 5 mm Hg. In contrast, O₂ requires much larger gradients: 64 mm Hg from alveoli to pulmonary capillary blood, and 55 mm Hg from arterial blood to interstitial fluid. The other options overestimate the pressure gradient needed for CO₂ diffusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intracellular PCO2 about 46 and interstitial about 45 mm Hg — the 1 mm Hg difference suffices because CO2 diffuses about 20 times as rapidly as O2. Matching values: arterial PCO2 40 entering the tissue, venous 45 leaving it. COMLEX: FA — diffusion-limiting disease such as pulmonary fibrosis causes early hypoxia rather than hypercapnia, for the same reason.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — diffusion rates of O2 versus CO2, solubility versus gradient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q26_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Partial pressure gradients driving O₂ and CO₂ movement","batch":1,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with emphysema has impaired gas exchange in his lungs. His pulmonary capillary blood enters with a PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg (venous blood), and the alveolar PCO₂ is 40 mm Hg. What is the pressure gradient driving CO₂ diffusion from blood into alveoli?","choices":{"A":"5 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into alveoli","B":"10 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into alveoli","C":"25 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into alveoli","D":"1 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into alveoli","E":"45 mm Hg gradient favoring diffusion into alveoli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pressure gradient driving CO₂ diffusion from pulmonary capillary blood into alveoli is 45 - 40 = 5 mm Hg. This small gradient is sufficient because CO₂ diffuses about 20 times more rapidly than O₂. The PCO₂ of pulmonary capillary blood falls to almost exactly equal the alveolar PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg before the blood has passed more than about one-third of the distance through the capillary. This rapid equilibration provides a safety factor similar to that for O₂ diffusion. Even in patients with emphysema and impaired diffusion, the 5 mm Hg gradient and high diffusibility of CO₂ usually allow adequate CO₂ elimination. In fact, patients with severe emphysema typically develop hypoxemia (low PO₂) before they develop hypercapnia (elevated PCO₂) because O₂ diffusion is more easily impaired than CO₂ diffusion. The other options represent incorrect gradient calculations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Entering capillary PCO2 is about 45 and alveolar PCO2 about 40, a 5 mm Hg gradient; blood PCO2 reaches alveolar values within about the first third of the capillary. COMLEX: Hypercapnia in emphysema reflects inadequate alveolar ventilation of perfused units rather than a CO2 diffusion barrier, since CO2 equilibrates so quickly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — how chronic emphysema leads to hypoxia and hypercapnia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q27_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Exercise effects on oxygen demand and diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"During a hard 30-minute run, a 27-year-old woman's leg muscles need many times their resting O₂ delivery, yet their interstitial PO₂ falls only modestly and stays within roughly 15 to 40 mm Hg. According to Guyton, which features allow hemoglobin to release so much extra O₂ with so little further fall in tissue PO₂?","choices":{"A":"Higher arterial PO₂ and a larger dissolved O₂ load","B":"Flat upper curve and less capillary blood flow","C":"Higher hemoglobin and a leftward curve shift","D":"Faster carbonic anhydrase and less muscle CO₂","E":"Steep curve slope and more tissue blood flow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton describes hemoglobin as a tissue O₂ buffer: during heavy exercise extra O₂, up to about 20 times normal, is delivered with little further decrease in tissue PO₂ because of the steep slope of the dissociation curve, so a very small fall in PO₂ releases large amounts of O₂, and because the low PO₂ increases tissue blood flow; hemoglobin therefore delivers O₂ at a pressure held rather tightly between about 15 and 40 mm Hg. Arterial PO₂ stays near 95 mm Hg, and dissolved O₂ is a small and shrinking share of delivery in exercise. The flat upper part of the curve governs loading in the lungs, not unloading in muscle, and blood flow rises rather than falls. A leftward shift would hold O₂ on hemoglobin; exercise shifts the curve to the right. Carbonic anhydrase speed and muscle CO₂ concern CO₂ transport, and CO₂ output rises rather than falls in exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemoglobin is a tissue O2 buffer — the steep slope of the dissociation curve lets a small fall in PO2 release large amounts of O2, and low PO2 increases tissue blood flow, so tissue PO2 stays between about 15 and 40 mm Hg even when O2 delivery rises up to 20-fold. COMLEX: The same buffering keeps tissue PO2 nearly constant when alveolar PO2 falls to 60 mm Hg (Hb still 89% saturated), the basis of the SpO2 safety margin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — oxygen transport principles and the factors that set hemoglobin saturation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q28_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Exercise effects on oxygen demand and diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"At rest, each 100 mL of arterial blood delivers about 0.17 mL of dissolved O₂ to the tissues and almost 5 mL carried by hemoglobin. During strenuous exercise, hemoglobin's release of O₂ to the tissues increases about 3-fold. Approximately what share of total O₂ delivery does the dissolved form then provide?","choices":{"A":"About 3%","B":"About 1.5%","C":"About 7%","D":"About 10%","E":"About 0.1%"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Guyton calculates that at an arterial PO₂ of 95 mm Hg about 0.29 mL of O₂ is dissolved per 100 mL of blood water and 0.12 mL remains at the tissue PO₂ of 40 mm Hg, so 0.17 mL is delivered in dissolved form, about 3% of the total versus 97% by hemoglobin. During strenuous exercise, when hemoglobin release increases another 3-fold, the relative quantity transported in the dissolved state falls to as little as 1.5%. About 3% is the resting value. About 7% is the dissolved share of CO₂ transport, not O₂. About 10% overstates the dissolved contribution, and about 0.1% understates it. The dissolved fraction becomes large only at very high alveolar PO₂, as in hyperbaric O₂ breathing, where it can cause O₂ toxicity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normally only about 3% of O2 delivered travels dissolved (about 0.17 mL/100 mL) versus 97% on hemoglobin; when hemoglobin's release rises 3-fold in strenuous exercise, the dissolved share falls to about 1.5%. FA: dissolved O2 = 0.003 × PaO2. COMLEX: Dissolved O2 matters only at very high alveolar PO2 — the principle behind hyperbaric O2 therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric oxygen, which raises the dissolved O2 that is normally trivial","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q29_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Exercise effects on oxygen demand and diffusion efficiency","batch":1,"stem":"A 33-year-old man exercises vigorously, and his active muscles release large quantities of CO₂, H⁺ ions, and heat. The local muscle temperature rises 3°C above normal. How do these factors affect oxygen delivery to the exercising muscles?","choices":{"A":"These factors shift the oxygen-hemoglobin curve left, impairing oxygen delivery","B":"These factors have no effect on the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","C":"These factors cause hemoglobin to release all bound oxygen immediately","D":"These factors decrease hemoglobin's oxygen-carrying capacity by 50%","E":"These factors shift the oxygen-hemoglobin curve right, enhancing oxygen delivery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During exercise, several factors shift the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the right, which enhances oxygen delivery to active tissues. These rightward-shifting factors include: (1) increased CO₂ concentration from enhanced metabolism, (2) increased H⁺ concentration (decreased pH) from CO₂ and lactic acid production, (3) increased temperature (2-3°C rise in active muscles), and (4) increased 2,3-BPG levels during prolonged exercise. The rightward shift caused by increased CO₂ and H⁺ is called the Bohr effect. This rightward shift allows oxygen to be released at higher PO₂ levels, meaning more oxygen can be delivered even when tissue PO₂ doesn't fall as dramatically. Guyton Ch 41 notes that the shifted curve forces O₂ off hemoglobin at a PO₂ as high as 40 mm Hg even when 70% of the O₂ has already been removed. In the lungs, these factors reverse (lower CO₂, higher pH, cooler temperature), shifting the curve leftward and enhancing oxygen uptake. The other options are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exercising muscle releases CO2 and acid and warms 2–3°C — each shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve right, so O2 unloads at higher tissue PO2. FA: right shift = ↓ affinity, ↑ P50, with exercise, ↓ pH, ↑ temperature/fever and ↑ 2,3-BPG (Bohr effect at the cellular level). COMLEX: Fever shifts the curve the same way, aiding O2 unloading in the febrile patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 8 — the factors that shift the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q30_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Blood flow variations and their effects on tissue oxygenation and CO₂ removal","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old diabetic patient develops peripheral vascular disease affecting his lower legs. In one region, blood flow increases to six times normal due to collateral vessel formation. If his tissue metabolic rate remains normal, what would be the expected effect on interstitial fluid PCO₂ in that region?","choices":{"A":"PCO₂ would increase from 45 to 60 mm Hg","B":"PCO₂ would remain stable at 45 mm Hg","C":"PCO₂ would increase from 45 to 50 mm Hg","D":"PCO₂ would decrease from 45 to 41 mm Hg","E":"PCO₂ would decrease from 45 to 35 mm Hg"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When blood flow increases to six times normal while metabolic rate remains constant, CO₂ is removed from tissues much more rapidly, causing interstitial PCO₂ to fall. A six-fold increase in blood flow decreases interstitial fluid PCO₂ from the normal value of 45 mm Hg to approximately 41 mm Hg, which is almost equal to the PCO₂ in arterial blood (40 mm Hg) entering the tissue capillaries. This represents the lower limit that tissue PCO₂ can reach because arterial blood entering the tissues has a PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg. Even with infinite blood flow, the tissue PCO₂ cannot fall below 40 mm Hg as long as tissues are producing CO₂. Conversely, if blood flow decreased to one-quarter normal, the PCO₂ would rise to about 60 mm Hg. The relationship demonstrates how tissue gas tensions represent a balance between metabolic production/consumption and blood flow. Rising to 60 mm Hg, stable at 45 mm Hg, and rising to 50 mm Hg incorrectly show PCO₂ increasing or remaining stable. Falling to 35 mm Hg shows an impossible decrease below arterial levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At constant metabolism, raising flow sixfold lowers interstitial PCO2 from 45 to about 41 mm Hg — close to the arterial 40 that enters the capillaries, which is the floor flow can reach. COMLEX: Tissue PCO2, like alveolar PCO2, is a production-versus-removal balance, so CO2 retention in a limb or organ points to inadequate flow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — alveolar PCO2 as a balance of CO2 production and ventilatory removal","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q31_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Blood flow variations and their effects on tissue oxygenation and CO₂ removal","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman takes part in a tissue-oxygenation study. In a tissue where blood flow remains normal at 100 mL/min but tissue oxygen consumption doubles, what happens to the interstitial fluid PO₂?","choices":{"A":"PO₂ decreases significantly below the normal 40 mm Hg value","B":"PO₂ remains stable at 40 mm Hg due to autoregulation","C":"PO₂ increases to 60 mm Hg from enhanced extraction","D":"PO₂ increases to match arterial blood at 95 mm Hg","E":"PO₂ fluctuates between 20 and 60 mm Hg"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tissue PO₂ is determined by the balance between oxygen delivery (determined by blood flow) and oxygen consumption (metabolic rate). When oxygen consumption doubles while blood flow remains constant, oxygen is being used faster than it can be delivered, causing tissue PO₂ to fall below the normal 40 mm Hg. The magnitude of the decrease depends on the exact metabolic rate increase, but a doubling of metabolism would cause a significant reduction in tissue PO₂ (Guyton Fig. 41.4). This lower PO₂ would then increase the pressure gradient from capillary blood to tissues, partially compensating for the increased metabolic demand. In reality, the decreased PO₂ would trigger local vasodilation (through release of vasodilator metabolites), which would increase blood flow and help restore tissue PO₂ toward normal. Stable at 40 mm Hg, rising to 60 mm Hg, and rising to 95 mm Hg incorrectly show PO₂ remaining stable or increasing. Fluctuating between 20 and 60 mm Hg suggests inappropriate fluctuation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial PO2 is a balance — at fixed blood flow, a rise in tissue O2 consumption pulls PO2 below the normal 40 mm Hg, while a rise in flow raises it. COMLEX: FA — in exercise this falling tissue PO2 widens the arteriovenous O2 difference and, with local CO2 and acid, shifts the curve right to unload more O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 9 — exercise adaptations that raise O2 delivery to working muscle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q32_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Blood flow variations and their effects on tissue oxygenation and CO₂ removal","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher conducts an experiment where tissue blood flow is increased to 400% of normal in a resting tissue. What is the theoretical maximum PO₂ that could be achieved in the interstitial fluid, even with infinitely high blood flow?","choices":{"A":"40 mm Hg matching venous blood oxygen levels","B":"66 mm Hg representing a moderate increase above normal","C":"75 mm Hg approaching three-quarters of arterial levels","D":"85 mm Hg approaching near-arterial saturation levels","E":"95 mm Hg matching arterial blood entering the tissues"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The maximum interstitial fluid PO₂ that can be achieved, even with infinitely high blood flow, is 95 mm Hg—the PO₂ of arterial blood entering the tissue capillaries. No matter how high blood flow increases, tissue PO₂ cannot exceed the PO₂ of the blood delivering oxygen to the tissue. At 400% of normal blood flow, the interstitial PO₂ would increase from 40 mm Hg to approximately 66 mm Hg, still well below the arterial level. As flow continues to increase, tissue PO₂ asymptotically approaches but never exceeds 95 mm Hg. This upper limit exists because arterial blood is the source of oxygen, and blood cannot deliver oxygen at a higher pressure than it contains. In the experiment described, the tissue PO₂ would be approximately 66 mm Hg, not the maximum possible 95 mm Hg. The other options all represent PO₂ values below the theoretical maximum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Even with infinite blood flow, interstitial PO2 cannot exceed the PO2 of the arterial blood entering the capillaries, about 95 mm Hg; at 400% flow it reaches only about 66. COMLEX: Raising tissue PO2 beyond this ceiling requires raising arterial PO2 — the rationale for supplemental and hyperbaric O2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric O2 therapy, which raises arterial and therefore tissue PO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q33_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Effects of pressure gradients, metabolic rates, and capillary transit times on gas diffusion","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student learns that normal intracellular PO₂ in peripheral tissues averages about 23 mm Hg, with a range from as low as 5 mm Hg to as high as 40 mm Hg. Despite this low intracellular PO₂, cells function normally. What factor primarily determines the rate of cellular oxygen consumption when intracellular PO₂ is above 1 mm Hg?","choices":{"A":"The rate of ADP formation from ATP usage determines oxygen consumption rate","B":"The partial pressure of oxygen in nearby capillaries determines consumption","C":"The concentration of oxygen-dependent enzymes determines consumption rate","D":"The distance from the cell to the nearest capillary determines consumption","E":"The hemoglobin concentration in blood determines cellular consumption rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When intracellular PO₂ is above 1 mm Hg, oxygen availability is not the limiting factor for metabolic rate. Instead, the main limiting factor is the concentration of ADP in cells. When ATP is used by cells to provide energy for cellular processes, it is converted to ADP. The increasing concentration of ADP then drives increased metabolic oxygen usage as cells oxidize nutrients to reconvert ADP back to ATP. This relationship means that under normal operating conditions, the rate of oxygen usage by cells is controlled ultimately by the rate of energy expenditure within cells—that is, by the rate at which ADP is formed from ATP. The respiratory enzyme systems have been configured so that when cellular PO₂ is more than 1 mm Hg, oxygen availability no longer limits chemical reaction rates. Only when intracellular PO₂ falls below 1 mm Hg does oxygen itself become rate-limiting. The other options describe factors that might affect oxygen delivery but don't determine consumption rate under normal conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whenever intracellular PO2 is above about 1 mm Hg, O2 usage is set by ADP concentration — ATP use generates ADP, which drives oxidative metabolism and O2 consumption. COMLEX: This is why O2 consumption rises in proportion to work in exercise, while supplemental O2 does not raise metabolism in a resting patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — hypoxia-inducible factors, the cellular response once O2 supply does fall","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q34_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Effects of pressure gradients, metabolic rates, and capillary transit times on gas diffusion","batch":1,"stem":"Under normal resting conditions each 100 mL of blood delivers about 5 mL of O₂ to the tissues and carries about 4 mL of CO₂ back to the lungs. A volunteer is then studied after several days of metabolizing fat almost exclusively. Which pair gives his resting respiratory exchange ratio before and during the fat-burning period?","choices":{"A":"1.25, falling to 1.00","B":"0.70, rising to 0.82","C":"1.00, falling to 0.82","D":"0.82, falling to 0.70","E":"0.82, rising to 1.00"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton defines the respiratory exchange ratio (R) as the rate of CO₂ output divided by the rate of O₂ uptake; with 4 mL of CO₂ expired for every 5 mL of O₂ taken up, R is about 0.82 (0.825 on an average mixed diet). When fats are used exclusively, R falls to as low as 0.7, because much of the O₂ combines with hydrogen atoms from the fats to form water instead of CO₂; with carbohydrates exclusively, R rises to 1.00, because one CO₂ is formed for each O₂ consumed. 1.25 inverts the ratio (O₂ over CO₂). 0.70 rising to 0.82 starts from the fat value. 1.00 is the pure-carbohydrate value, not the resting mixed-diet value. A rise to 1.00 is the change expected with a carbohydrate-only diet, not with fat.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Respiratory exchange ratio R = CO2 output ÷ O2 uptake; 4 mL CO2 for 5 mL O2 gives about 0.82 (0.825 on a mixed diet), 1.00 on pure carbohydrate and as low as 0.7 on fat, because much of the O2 used with fat forms water instead of CO2. COMLEX: FA's alveolar gas equation, PAO2 = PIO2 − PaCO2/R, uses this ratio (taken as 0.8) to estimate the A-a gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 4 — atmospheric versus alveolar air, whose O2 and CO2 values depend on R","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_ex1_q35_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Effects of pressure gradients, metabolic rates, and capillary transit times on gas diffusion","batch":1,"stem":"A 27-year-old man undergoes physiological testing during progressive exercise. At rest, his blood spends approximately 0.8 seconds in the pulmonary capillaries. During maximal exercise, capillary transit time decreases to about 0.3 seconds. Despite this more than two-fold reduction in transit time, his arterial blood remains well-oxygenated. What physiological principle explains this maintained oxygenation?","choices":{"A":"Exercise causes pulmonary capillaries to lengthen, maintaining transit time","B":"Blood is normally saturated within the first third of the capillary","C":"Hemoglobin oxygen-binding affinity increases during exercise to compensate","D":"Alveolar PO₂ increases to 150 mm Hg during exercise to enhance diffusion","E":"Red blood cells slow their velocity through capillaries during exercise"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under resting conditions, blood becomes almost completely saturated with oxygen by the time it has passed through only about one-third of the distance through the pulmonary capillary. This means blood normally stays in lung capillaries about three times as long as needed for full oxygenation, providing a large safety factor. During exercise, even though capillary transit time falls to less than half of normal (from about 0.8 to about 0.3 seconds, Guyton Ch 39), this is still sufficient for complete oxygenation. Additionally, the diffusing capacity for oxygen increases almost 3-fold during exercise (from ~21 to ~65 mL/min/mm Hg) due to recruitment of additional capillary surface area and more ideal V/Q ratios. These two factors combined—the resting safety margin and increased diffusing capacity—ensure that blood can still become fully oxygenated even with dramatically reduced capillary transit times. The other options describe mechanisms that don't actually occur during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At rest blood is almost saturated after one-third of the pulmonary capillary, so it stays about three times as long as needed; even with transit shortened by exercise it still oxygenates fully. COMLEX: Thickened membranes (edema, fibrosis) consume this reserve, so exertional hypoxemia is an early clue to diffusion impairment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 12 — normal capillary transit time and its relation to gas exchange","isHighYield":false}],"14":[{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q01_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"Most descriptions of the Bohr effect focus on O₂ release in the tissues. Guyton points out that the same mechanism also acts in the pulmonary capillaries as CO₂ diffuses from the blood into the alveoli. What is the effect there?","choices":{"A":"The curve shifts right, unloading O₂ into the alveoli","B":"The curve is unchanged, as alveolar PO₂ is already high","C":"The curve shifts left, so more O₂ binds at alveolar PO₂","D":"The curve flattens, lowering arterial O₂ saturation","E":"The curve shifts right, freeing sites for CO₂ to bind"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton explains that in the tissues rising PCO₂ and H⁺ shift the O₂-hemoglobin curve to the right and downward, forcing O₂ off hemoglobin, and that exactly the opposite occurs in the lungs: CO₂ diffusing from the blood into the alveoli reduces blood PCO₂ and H⁺, shifting the curve to the left and upward, so the quantity of O₂ that binds at any given alveolar PO₂ is considerably increased, allowing greater O₂ transport to the tissues. A rightward shift in the lungs would oppose loading, and hemoglobin does not unload O₂ into alveoli. The curve does change, because the fall in PCO₂ and H⁺ happens as blood crosses the capillary. Flattening with lower saturation is not the effect described. Freeing sites for CO₂ binding describes the reciprocal Haldane effect in tissues, not the Bohr effect in the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The Bohr effect works in both directions — in tissues rising PCO2 and H+ shift the curve right and force O2 off hemoglobin; in the lungs CO2 leaves the blood, PCO2 and H+ fall, and the curve shifts left so more O2 binds at any alveolar PO2. COMLEX: FA frames the curve as matching local need — right shift where O2 must be unloaded, left shift where it must be loaded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — the O2 and CO2 gradients between alveoli, capillary blood and tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q02_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology instructor contrasts the Bohr effect, in which CO₂ and H⁺ displace O₂ from hemoglobin, with the Haldane effect, in which O₂ binding displaces CO₂ from the blood. According to Guyton, how do the two compare in quantitative importance?","choices":{"A":"Haldane matters far more for CO₂ transport than Bohr does for O₂","B":"Bohr matters far more for O₂ transport than Haldane does for CO₂","C":"Both are equal, being one reaction seen from two sides","D":"Both are negligible beside the steep dissociation curve","E":"Haldane acts solely in tissues; Bohr solely in lungs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton notes that binding of O₂ with hemoglobin tends to displace CO₂ from the blood, and that this Haldane effect is quantitatively far more important in promoting CO₂ transport than the Bohr effect is in promoting O₂ transport; the Haldane effect roughly doubles the CO₂ released in the lungs and picked up in the tissues. Ranking the Bohr effect higher reverses Guyton's statement. Although the two effects are reciprocal, they are not equal in size. The Haldane effect is far from negligible, since it accounts for about half of normal CO₂ exchange. Both effects act at both sites: each operates in one direction in the tissues and in the opposite direction in the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bohr effect = CO2/H+ lowering hemoglobin's O2 affinity; Haldane effect = O2 binding displacing CO2. Guyton: the Haldane effect is quantitatively far more important for CO2 transport than the Bohr effect is for O2 transport — it roughly doubles CO2 release in the lungs and uptake in the tissues. COMLEX: FA states the Haldane effect simply: Hb oxygenation in the lungs promotes H+ dissociation and CO2 release from red cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport as bicarbonate, carbaminohemoglobin and dissolved CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q03_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman's arterial blood is analyzed under different conditions. At a pH of 7.4, her hemoglobin is 97% saturated at a PO₂ of 95 mm Hg. When the pH is experimentally decreased to 7.2 (more acidic), what happens to the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve?","choices":{"A":"The curve shifts leftward, requiring lower PO₂ to achieve 97% saturation","B":"The curve remains unchanged because pH doesn't affect hemoglobin binding","C":"The curve becomes steeper without shifting left or right","D":"The curve shifts upward, increasing maximum saturation to 100%","E":"The curve shifts rightward, so a higher PO₂ is needed for 97% saturation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When blood pH decreases from the normal 7.4 to 7.2 (increased H⁺ concentration, more acidic), the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts, on average, about 15% to the right. This rightward shift means that at any given PO₂, hemoglobin saturation will be lower, or conversely, a higher PO₂ is required to achieve the same saturation. This rightward shift with acidosis is part of the Bohr effect and is physiologically beneficial in tissues where metabolism produces acids—the lower pH helps release oxygen where it's needed. Conversely, an increase in pH from 7.4 to 7.6 (more alkaline) shifts the curve about 15% to the left, increasing hemoglobin's oxygen affinity. A leftward shift shows the opposite shift. An unchanged curve is incorrect because pH profoundly affects hemoglobin. A steeper curve and an upward shift to 100% describe changes that don't occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A fall in pH from 7.4 to 7.2 shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve, on average, about 15% to the right; a rise to 7.6 shifts it a similar amount to the left. FA: right shift = ↓ affinity, ↑ P50, more unloading. COMLEX: In acidemia a given PaO2 corresponds to a lower saturation, so pulse oximetry and ABG values are read together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 1 — PO2, PCO2 and pH as the basic measures of pulmonary performance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q04_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man ascends to high altitude (10,000 feet) and remains there for several weeks. His body adapts by increasing 2,3-biphosphoglycerate (BPG) levels in red blood cells. How does increased BPG affect oxygen transport?","choices":{"A":"Increased BPG shifts the curve rightward, enhancing tissue oxygen delivery","B":"Increased BPG shifts the curve leftward, impairing oxygen delivery to tissues","C":"Increased BPG increases hemoglobin concentration, improving oxygen-carrying capacity","D":"Increased BPG has no effect on oxygen transport or the dissociation curve","E":"Increased BPG decreases hemoglobin's maximum oxygen-binding capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normal BPG (2,3-biphosphoglycerate) in blood always keeps the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifted slightly to the right. In hypoxic conditions that last longer than a few hours (such as prolonged high-altitude exposure), the quantity of BPG in blood increases considerably, shifting the curve even farther to the right. This rightward shift causes oxygen to be released to tissues at as much as 10 mm Hg higher tissue oxygen pressure than would occur without the increased BPG. This adaptation is particularly important at high altitude where arterial PO₂ is reduced—the rightward shift helps maintain adequate tissue oxygen delivery despite lower arterial oxygen content. The BPG mechanism represents an important adaptation to chronic hypoxia caused by poor tissue blood flow or reduced atmospheric oxygen. A leftward shift shows the wrong direction. Increased hemoglobin concentration confuses BPG with erythropoietin effects. No effect and decreased maximum binding capacity are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal BPG keeps the curve slightly right-shifted; in hypoxia lasting longer than a few hours BPG rises considerably and shifts it farther right, releasing O2 at as much as 10 mm Hg higher tissue PO2. FA: at altitude, ↑ 2,3-BPG (from increased glycolysis) is listed with ↑ erythropoietin and ↑ ventilation. COMLEX: FA lists chronic hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction at altitude as a route to pulmonary hypertension and RVH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 9 — the phenomenon of acclimatization to altitude","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q05_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-month-old infant is given a well-water formula high in nitrates and becomes cyanotic. Blood drawn from her looks chocolate-brown, and co-oximetry shows that 25% of her hemoglobin iron is in the ferric (Fe³⁺) state. How does this disorder affect her oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve, according to First Aid?","choices":{"A":"Right shift, raising P50 and easing tissue unloading","B":"No shift, as just the O₂ content is reduced","C":"Right shift from the rise in red cell 2,3-BPG","D":"Left shift, lowering P50 and impeding unloading","E":"Downward shift with an unchanged P50"},"correct":"D","explanation":"First Aid 2025 lists methemoglobinemia (increased MetHb) with carbon monoxide among the pathologic causes of a left shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve: hemoglobin's O₂ affinity rises, P50 falls, and O₂ unloading in the tissues decreases. First Aid's O₂ table and methemoglobin entry (p688) add that the ferric iron itself binds O₂ poorly, so SaO₂ and total O₂ content fall while PaO₂ and hemoglobin concentration stay normal; nitrites from dietary intake or polluted water oxidize Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺, and the blood is chocolate-colored. A right shift with a higher P50 occurs with exercise, acidosis, fever, and hypoxia-induced 2,3-BPG, not with methemoglobin. The reduced O₂ content is real, but the curve is not left unchanged. A downward shift with unchanged P50 is not how First Aid describes this disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FA lists ↑ methemoglobin with CO as pathologic causes of a left shift — affinity rises, P50 falls, unloading falls; MetHb's ferric iron (Fe3+) binds O2 poorly, lowering SaO2 and O2 content while PaO2 stays normal. COMLEX: Nitrites (including from well water), dapsone and benzocaine cause it; cyanosis does not improve with O2, blood is chocolate-colored, and methylene blue with vitamin C treats it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — causes of hypoxia, including anemia and abnormal hemoglobin","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q06_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher studies factors affecting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve. Which of the following combinations would shift the curve to the LEFT, thereby increasing hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen and potentially impairing oxygen release to tissues?","choices":{"A":"Increased temperature, increased CO₂, and increased H⁺ concentration","B":"Decreased temperature, increased CO₂, and increased 2,3-BPG levels","C":"Normal temperature, increased CO₂, and decreased 2,3-BPG levels","D":"Increased temperature, decreased CO₂, and increased 2,3-BPG levels","E":"Decreased temperature, decreased CO₂, and decreased H⁺ (increased pH)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the LEFT (increasing hemoglobin's oxygen affinity) when: (1) temperature decreases, (2) CO₂ concentration decreases, (3) H⁺ concentration decreases (pH increases/becomes more alkaline), and (4) 2,3-BPG levels decrease. A leftward shift means hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly, which is beneficial in the lungs for oxygen loading but can impair oxygen release to tissues. This combination might occur in hypothermic patients or during hyperventilation (which lowers CO₂ and raises pH). In contrast, a rightward shift occurs with increased temperature, increased CO₂, increased H⁺ (decreased pH), and increased 2,3-BPG—all of which decrease hemoglobin's oxygen affinity and enhance tissue oxygen delivery. All-increased factors, normal temperature with increased CO₂ and low BPG, and increased temperature with low CO₂ and high BPG contain factors that would shift the curve rightward. Decreased temperature with increased CO₂ and BPG is mixed, but the increased CO₂ and BPG would dominate, causing a rightward shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Right-shift factors are increased CO2, H+, temperature and BPG; the reverse of each — cooling, low CO2, alkalosis, low BPG — shifts the curve left, raising affinity and impairing unloading. FA adds CO, methemoglobin and fetal hemoglobin as left-shift causes ('left is lower' P50). COMLEX: Hypothermia and iatrogenic alkalosis can leave tissues hypoxic despite a reassuring saturation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — oxygen transport principles and factors that influence hemoglobin saturation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q07_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old competitive cyclist undergoes exercise testing. At rest, his pulmonary diffusing capacity for oxygen is 21 mL/min per mm Hg. During maximal exercise, his diffusing capacity increases substantially. What is the primary mechanism responsible for this increase in diffusing capacity during exercise?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar surface area decreases, concentrating gas exchange in fewer alveoli","B":"Respiratory membrane thickness increases to strengthen the barrier","C":"Minute ventilation decreases to allow more time for diffusion","D":"Hemoglobin concentration in blood doubles during exercise","E":"More pulmonary capillaries are recruited, increasing capillary surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The diffusing capacity for oxygen increases almost 3-fold during exercise, from about 21 mL/min per mm Hg at rest to about 65 mL/min per mm Hg during maximal exercise. This increase results mainly from two mechanisms: (1) increased surface area of capillaries participating in diffusion—as cardiac output increases, previously closed or poorly perfused alveolar capillaries become recruited and fully perfused, dramatically increasing the total surface area available for gas exchange; (2) more ideal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratios in the upper parts of the lungs as both ventilation and perfusion increase proportionally. This enhanced diffusing capacity, combined with the safety factor that blood normally becomes fully oxygenated within the first third of capillary transit time, ensures arterial blood remains well-oxygenated even when capillary transit time is reduced to less than half normal during intense exercise. Decreased surface area, thicker membrane, and decreased minute ventilation describe changes opposite to what occurs. Doubled hemoglobin is incorrect because hemoglobin concentration doesn't acutely double during exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: O2 diffusing capacity rises almost 3-fold in exercise, mainly from more participating capillaries (larger surface area) and a more ideal V/Q in the upper lungs. FA: with exercise apical capillaries dilate and V/Q approaches 1. COMLEX: Patients who have lost capillary bed (emphysema, ILD, pulmonary vascular disease) cannot recruit, so their limitation shows during exertion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 10 — O2 and CO2 diffusing capacities at rest and during exercise","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q08_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old marathon runner's blood is analyzed during peak exercise. Her tissue PO₂ in active leg muscles has dropped from the resting value of 40 mm Hg to 15 mm Hg. What is her utilization coefficient (the percentage of oxygen extracted from blood) during this intense exercise?","choices":{"A":"The utilization coefficient increases slightly to 35% during intense exercise","B":"The utilization coefficient remains at 25% regardless of exercise intensity","C":"The utilization coefficient decreases to 10% because of reduced capillary transit time","D":"The utilization coefficient increases to approximately 75-85% during intense exercise","E":"The utilization coefficient reaches 50% at maximum during intense exercise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During intense exercise when tissue PO₂ falls to 15 mm Hg, the utilization coefficient can increase to 75-85%, meaning that 75-85% of the oxygen bound to hemoglobin in arterial blood is extracted and delivered to tissues. This is a dramatic increase from the normal resting utilization coefficient of 25%. At a tissue PO₂ of 15 mm Hg, hemoglobin saturation drops to approximately 22%, leaving only about 4.4 mL of oxygen bound per 100 mL blood (compared to 19.4 mL in arterial blood). This means 19.4 - 4.4 = 15 mL of oxygen is delivered per 100 mL blood flow, representing a 3-fold increase over the normal 5 mL delivered at rest. In highly metabolically active tissues with very slow blood flow, utilization coefficients approaching 100% have been recorded. This demonstrates the remarkable extraction capacity of tissues during periods of high metabolic demand. The other options all underestimate the extraction capability during intense exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Utilization coefficient = fraction of arterial O2 given up in the tissues — about 25% at rest, 75–85% for the whole body in strenuous exercise, and near 100% in tissues with very slow flow or very high metabolism. COMLEX: FA — exercise widens the arteriovenous O2 difference through a right-shifted curve, so a low mixed venous saturation is expected at peak effort.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — how exercise raises O2 demand and extraction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q09_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"Arterial blood normally has a pH of about 7.41, and as it picks up CO₂ in resting tissues its pH falls only to about 7.37. During heavy exercise, or when blood flow through a tissue is sluggish, how large can the fall in pH of the tissue blood become, according to Guyton?","choices":{"A":"About 0.04 unit, the same as at rest","B":"About 0.10 unit, 2.5 times normal","C":"About 0.02 unit, half the resting value","D":"About 0.50 unit, about 12 times normal","E":"About 1.50 units, about 40 times normal"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton notes that buffers keep the normal arterial-to-venous pH change to about 0.04 unit (7.41 to 7.37), but during heavy exercise, other conditions of high metabolic activity, or sluggish tissue blood flow, the decrease in pH in the tissue blood and tissues can be as much as 0.50, about 12 times normal, causing significant tissue acidosis; the extra H⁺ also contributes to the rightward shift of the dissociation curve that unloads O₂ in active muscle. 0.04 unit is the resting change, and 0.02 unit is smaller still. 0.10 unit understates the exercise value. A fall of 1.50 units would drive blood pH to about 5.9, far below what Guyton describes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Buffers keep the arterial-to-venous pH change to about 0.04 unit (7.41 to 7.37); in heavy exercise or sluggish tissue flow the fall can reach 0.50, about 12 times normal, causing significant tissue acidosis. COMLEX: Local tissue acidosis is also a right-shift stimulus, so poorly perfused tissue unloads O2 more readily from what blood does reach it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — how blood flow changes alter tissue O2 and CO2 levels","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q10_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man's red blood cells are studied in vitro. When CO₂ is added to the blood sample, the enzyme carbonic anhydrase rapidly catalyzes a reaction. Approximately how much does carbonic anhydrase accelerate the reaction between CO₂ and water compared to the uncatalyzed reaction?","choices":{"A":"10-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","B":"5000-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","C":"100-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","D":"500-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction","E":"50-fold acceleration of the CO₂-water reaction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase, an enzyme found inside red blood cells, catalyzes the reaction between CO₂ and water and accelerates the reaction rate by approximately 5000-fold. This dramatic acceleration is essential for efficient CO₂ transport. Without carbonic anhydrase, the reaction CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ would require many seconds or minutes to reach equilibrium. With carbonic anhydrase, the reaction occurs so rapidly that it reaches almost complete equilibrium within a small fraction of a second, even before blood leaves the tissue capillaries. This rapid reaction allows tremendous amounts of CO₂ to combine with red blood cell water and be transported as bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻), which accounts for about 70% of CO₂ transport. When carbonic anhydrase is experimentally blocked with inhibitors like acetazolamide, CO₂ transport becomes so impaired that tissue PCO₂ can rise from the normal 45 mm Hg to 80 mm Hg or higher. The other options all underestimate the catalytic power of this essential enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Red-cell carbonic anhydrase accelerates CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 about 5000-fold, so a reaction that takes seconds to minutes in plasma occurs within a fraction of a second; H2CO3 then dissociates to H+ and HCO3−. COMLEX: FA — acetazolamide inhibits this enzyme; it augments renal HCO3− excretion at altitude and is used in idiopathic intracranial hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and their role in acute mountain sickness","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q11_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns about the chloride shift that occurs in red blood cells during CO₂ transport. After carbonic anhydrase catalyzes the formation of carbonic acid, which then dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, what happens to these ions?","choices":{"A":"Both H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ remain in red blood cells throughout circulation","B":"Both H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ diffuse into plasma, leaving red blood cells","C":"H⁺ binds to hemoglobin while HCO₃⁻ diffuses to plasma; Cl⁻ enters RBCs in exchange","D":"HCO₃⁻ binds to hemoglobin while H⁺ diffuses to plasma; Na⁺ enters RBCs in exchange","E":"H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ immediately recombine to form CO₂ again"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻ ions in red blood cells, these ions follow different paths: (1) Most H⁺ ions combine with hemoglobin because hemoglobin protein is a powerful acid-base buffer, preventing large pH changes; (2) Many HCO₃⁻ ions diffuse from red blood cells into plasma, while chloride ions (Cl⁻) diffuse into red blood cells to take their place and maintain electrical neutrality. This exchange is facilitated by a special bicarbonate-chloride carrier protein in the red blood cell membrane that shuttles these two ions in opposite directions at rapid velocities. This phenomenon is called the \"chloride shift\" or \"Hamburger shift.\" As a result, the chloride content of venous red blood cells is greater than that of arterial red blood cells. In the lungs, this process reverses—HCO₃⁻ moves back into RBCs, combines with H⁺ to form H₂CO₃, which dissociates into CO₂ and water, and the CO₂ diffuses into alveoli. The other options describe incorrect mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After carbonic anhydrase acts, most H+ binds hemoglobin (a powerful buffer), HCO3− leaves the red cell for plasma, and Cl− enters on a bicarbonate-chloride carrier — so venous red cells hold more chloride (the chloride shift). FA: about 90% of CO2 travels as HCO3− in plasma (Guyton: about 70%). COMLEX: The red cell's buffering keeps the venous pH change small despite the CO2 load.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport mechanisms and chemical combinations in blood","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q12_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"Carbon dioxide forms carbamino compounds by reacting directly with amine radicals of proteins. In tissue capillaries it can react this way with both hemoglobin and the plasma proteins. Why does Guyton consider the plasma-protein reaction much less significant for CO₂ transport?","choices":{"A":"Plasma proteins lack the amine radicals CO₂ binds","B":"The plasma reaction needs carbonic anhydrase","C":"Plasma proteins bind CO₂ too tightly to release it","D":"Plasma carbamino forms in the lungs, not the tissues","E":"Plasma proteins total about a quarter of hemoglobin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that a small amount of CO₂ reacts with plasma proteins in the tissue capillaries in the same way it reacts with hemoglobin, but this is much less significant because the quantity of these proteins in the blood is only one-fourth as great as the quantity of hemoglobin; carbamino transport by both together is about 30% of the total in theory, and probably no more than 20% in practice because the reaction is slower than the carbonic anhydrase reaction. Plasma proteins do have amine radicals, which is why the reaction occurs at all. Carbamino formation is a direct reaction that does not require carbonic anhydrase; that enzyme catalyzes CO₂ hydration inside red cells and is absent from plasma. The carbamino bond is loose and easily reversed, not too tight. Carbamino compounds form in the tissues, where PCO₂ is high, and release CO₂ in the lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 forms carbamino compounds with amine radicals on both hemoglobin and plasma proteins, but plasma proteins total only about one-fourth as much as hemoglobin, so their share is small. FA: carbamino compounds form at the N-terminus of globin in deoxygenated hemoglobin (about 5% of CO2). COMLEX: Deoxygenated hemoglobin binds CO2 more readily — the Haldane effect at the venous end.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — carbaminohemoglobin among the CO2 transport routes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q13_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man is exposed to carbon monoxide (CO) in his garage. CO competes with oxygen for binding sites on hemoglobin. Approximately how much more tightly does CO bind to hemoglobin compared to oxygen?","choices":{"A":"CO binds approximately 250 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin","B":"CO binds approximately 50 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin","C":"CO binds approximately 10 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin","D":"CO binds with the same affinity as oxygen to hemoglobin","E":"CO binds approximately 1000 times more tightly than oxygen to hemoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carbon monoxide binds to hemoglobin at the same sites as oxygen but with approximately 250 times as much tenacity (Guyton Ch 41; First Aid 2025 gives > 200 times the affinity of O₂). This is demonstrated by the CO-hemoglobin dissociation curve, which is almost identical to the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve except that the CO partial pressures are at a level 1/250 of those for oxygen. Therefore, a CO partial pressure of only 0.4 mm Hg in alveoli (1/250 of the normal alveolar oxygen pressure of 100 mm Hg) allows CO to compete equally with oxygen for hemoglobin binding, causing 50% of hemoglobin to become bound with CO instead of oxygen. A CO pressure of only 0.6 mm Hg (less than one part per 1000 in air) can be lethal. This extraordinarily high affinity makes CO poisoning particularly dangerous—even small amounts of CO can displace large amounts of oxygen from hemoglobin, severely impairing oxygen delivery to tissues. The other options underestimate or overestimate the binding differential.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO binds hemoglobin at the O2 sites with about 250 times O2's tenacity (Guyton; FA: more than 200 times), so an alveolar PCO of only 0.4 mm Hg competes equally with normal alveolar O2 and about 0.6 mm Hg can be lethal. COMLEX: FA — CO comes from furnaces, space heaters, motor exhaust and fires; multiple victims in one household suggests it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 11 — the CO method for diffusing capacity, which relies on this avid binding","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q14_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with suspected carbon monoxide poisoning from a faulty heater. Despite having 50% of her hemoglobin bound to CO (carboxyhemoglobin), her arterial PO₂ measurement is normal at 95 mm Hg. Why is the arterial PO₂ normal despite severe CO poisoning?","choices":{"A":"CO increases oxygen diffusion from alveoli, raising arterial PO₂ to compensate","B":"The body compensates by producing more hemoglobin immediately","C":"PO₂ reflects dissolved O₂ only; CO lowers O₂ content but not PO₂","D":"CO binding to hemoglobin doesn't affect oxygen content, only PO₂","E":"CO is rapidly converted to CO₂ in the lungs, restoring normal oxygenation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Even though oxygen content of blood is greatly reduced in CO poisoning (because hemoglobin binding sites are occupied by CO), the arterial PO₂ can remain normal. This occurs because PO₂ measures the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen in plasma, not the oxygen bound to hemoglobin. The alveolar-to-blood oxygen gradient remains normal, so dissolved oxygen equilibrates normally, maintaining normal arterial PO₂. This situation makes CO exposure especially dangerous because: (1) the blood appears bright red (not cyanotic) since carboxyhemoglobin is bright red like oxyhemoglobin; (2) there are no obvious visual signs of hypoxemia like bluish discoloration of lips or fingertips; (3) the PO₂ is not reduced, so the feedback mechanism that normally stimulates increased respiration in response to low oxygen (detected by peripheral chemoreceptors responding to PO₂) is absent. The person may become disoriented and unconscious before becoming aware of danger because the brain is one of the first organs affected by lack of oxygen delivery. The other options describe mechanisms that don't occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PaO2 measures dissolved O2, so in CO poisoning it stays normal while O2 content and %O2Hb fall; the blood is bright red and there is no cyanosis. FA: normal PaO2, ↑ carboxyhemoglobin on co-oximetry — standard pulse oximetry cannot distinguish it. COMLEX: Order co-oximetry for headache, confusion or vomiting after heater or exhaust exposure even when the SpO2 looks normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's role in O2 transport and O2-carrying capacity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q15_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man severely poisoned with carbon monoxide is brought to the hospital. What is the most effective treatment to rapidly remove CO from his blood and restore oxygen-carrying capacity?","choices":{"A":"Intravenous administration of methylene blue to convert carboxyhemoglobin","B":"Pure oxygen plus 5% CO₂ to stimulate ventilation and displace CO","C":"Blood transfusion to replace CO-bound red blood cells with fresh cells","D":"Hyperventilation with room air to increase CO₂ removal","E":"Administration of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors to enhance gas exchange"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A patient severely poisoned with CO can be effectively treated by administering pure oxygen (100% O₂) because oxygen at high alveolar pressure can displace CO from its combination with hemoglobin much more rapidly than breathing room air. Additionally, simultaneous administration of 5% CO₂ provides further benefit because CO₂ strongly stimulates the respiratory center, increasing alveolar ventilation and reducing alveolar CO concentration. With intensive oxygen and CO₂ therapy, CO can be removed from blood as much as 10 times more rapidly than without therapy. The high oxygen partial pressure (potentially 600-700 mm Hg when breathing 100% O₂) overwhelms CO's much higher binding affinity (about 250-fold in Guyton, > 200-fold in First Aid 2025) through mass action—the sheer abundance of oxygen molecules competing for hemoglobin binding sites. In clinical practice the mainstay is 100% O₂, with hyperbaric O₂ (pressures >1 atmosphere) for severe cases (First Aid); the added CO₂ is Guyton's physiological refinement. Methylene blue treats methemoglobinemia, not carboxyhemoglobin; transfusion and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors do not remove CO; and hyperventilating room air lacks the high alveolar PO₂ that displaces CO.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's treatment for severe CO poisoning — pure O2, because high alveolar PO2 displaces CO from hemoglobin rapidly, plus 5% CO2, which strongly stimulates the respiratory center, raising alveolar ventilation and lowering alveolar CO. COMLEX: FA — give 100% O2 at once and hyperbaric O2 if severe; consider concurrent cyanide in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric oxygen therapy, its rationale and mechanism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q17_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman hyperventilates during a panic attack, causing her arterial PCO₂ to drop from 40 mm Hg to 25 mm Hg and her pH to rise from 7.4 to 7.6. How does this respiratory alkalosis affect oxygen delivery to her tissues through the Bohr effect?","choices":{"A":"Decreased PCO₂ and increased pH shift the curve leftward, impairing tissue oxygen release","B":"Decreased PCO₂ and increased pH shift the curve rightward, enhancing oxygen delivery","C":"Decreased PCO₂ and increased pH have no effect on hemoglobin-oxygen binding","D":"Decreased PCO₂ increases hemoglobin concentration, improving oxygen capacity","E":"Decreased PCO₂ denatures hemoglobin, preventing all oxygen transport"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When PCO₂ decreases and pH increases (becomes more alkaline) during hyperventilation, the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the LEFT. This leftward shift increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, which impairs oxygen release to tissues. The Bohr effect normally works in favor of tissue oxygenation—when blood passes through metabolically active tissues where PCO₂ is high and pH is low (acidic), the curve shifts right to enhance oxygen release. In this hyperventilation scenario, the opposite occurs: the leftward shift means hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly, making it harder to release at normal tissue PO₂ levels. The paresthesias and carpopedal spasm of hyperventilation come mainly from alkalosis lowering ionized calcium (more Ca²⁺ bound to plasma protein), not from the left shift itself. The phenomenon demonstrates why respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation can paradoxically worsen tissue oxygenation. The other options are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperventilation lowers PCO2 and raises pH, shifting the curve left (Guyton: pH 7.4 → 7.6 shifts it about as much as 7.4 → 7.2 shifts it right), so hemoglobin holds O2 more tightly and tissue unloading falls. COMLEX: FA — at altitude, hypoxic hyperventilation produces the same respiratory alkalosis, and renal HCO3− excretion (augmentable with acetazolamide) compensates.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — respiratory and renal compensation during altitude acclimatization","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q18_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology researcher studies the CO₂ dissociation curve under different oxygen saturation conditions. At a PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg (normal venous), blood with PO₂ of 40 mm Hg (deoxygenated) carries 52 volume percent CO₂. When the same blood is oxygenated to PO₂ of 100 mm Hg while PCO₂ remains at 45 mm Hg, what happens to the CO₂ content?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ content decreases due to the Haldane effect as oxygen binding displaces CO₂","B":"CO₂ content increases because oxygen binding enhances CO₂ transport","C":"CO₂ content remains unchanged at 52 volume percent","D":"CO₂ content increases to 55 volume percent from enhanced carbonic anhydrase activity","E":"CO₂ content decreases to 40 volume percent from competitive inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Haldane effect describes how oxygen binding to hemoglobin decreases the blood's CO₂-carrying capacity at any given PCO₂. When oxygen binds to hemoglobin, it makes hemoglobin a stronger acid, which has two consequences: (1) the more acidic hemoglobin has less tendency to form carbaminohemoglobin, releasing CO₂ that was bound to hemoglobin; (2) the increased acidity causes hemoglobin to release H⁺ ions, which combine with HCO₃⁻ to form carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which dissociates into CO₂ and water. Therefore, when blood at PCO₂ 45 mm Hg is oxygenated, the CO₂ dissociation curve shifts downward, and the CO₂ content decreases even though PCO₂ hasn't changed. This is why the CO₂ dissociation curve shows different curves for oxygenated versus deoxygenated blood. In the lungs, this effect facilitates CO₂ release; in tissues, deoxygenation facilitates CO₂ uptake. An increase from enhanced transport and an increase to 55 volume percent incorrectly show increases. No change at 52 volume percent ignores the Haldane effect. A decrease to 40 volume percent overestimates the magnitude.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Haldane effect — at the same PCO2, oxygenated blood holds less CO2 than deoxygenated blood because acidified oxyhemoglobin forms less carbaminohemoglobin and releases H+ that converts HCO3− to CO2. COMLEX: FA's one-liner for the lungs: Hb oxygenation promotes H+ dissociation and CO2 release from red cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport mechanisms and the CO2 dissociation curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q19_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Bohr and Haldane effects in oxygen and carbon dioxide transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with chronic lung disease has blood pH of 7.25 (acidemia) due to CO₂ retention. His oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is analyzed. How would the PO₂ at which his hemoglobin is 50% saturated (P₅₀) change?","choices":{"A":"P₅₀ would decrease, reflecting a leftward shift","B":"P₅₀ would stay at its normal value despite the pH change","C":"P₅₀ would decrease from increased oxygen affinity","D":"P₅₀ would increase from CO₂ competing at the O₂-binding site","E":"P₅₀ would increase, reflecting a rightward (Bohr) shift"},"correct":"E","explanation":"P₅₀ is the PO₂ at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated; an increase in P₅₀ reflects decreased O₂ affinity and a decrease reflects increased affinity (Costanzo Ch 5). A fall in pH from 7.4 toward 7.2 shifts the curve, on average, about 15% to the right (Guyton Ch 41), so a higher PO₂ is needed to reach 50% saturation and P₅₀ rises. This Bohr shift helps release O₂ to the tissues despite his lung disease. CO₂ acts through H⁺ and carbamino formation, not by competing for the O₂-binding site. A lower P₅₀ would mean a leftward shift, which alkalosis rather than acidemia produces, and P₅₀ does not stay fixed when pH changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: P50 is the PO2 at 50% saturation; acidemia from CO2 retention shifts the curve right (about 15% for pH 7.4 → 7.2), so P50 rises. FA: right shift ↔ ↑ P50; 'left is lower'. COMLEX: In chronic CO2 retention the right shift helps tissues unload O2 even as arterial saturation falls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the pathophysiology of hypoxia and hypercapnia in chronic emphysema","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q20_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old medical student donates blood, and a unit of packed red blood cells is stored at 4°C for 28 days before transfusion. During storage, 2,3-BPG levels in the red blood cells decrease significantly. How does this affect the stored blood's ability to deliver oxygen to tissues when transfused?","choices":{"A":"Decreased 2,3-BPG has no effect on oxygen delivery from transfused blood","B":"Decreased 2,3-BPG shifts the curve leftward, impairing oxygen release to tissues","C":"Decreased 2,3-BPG shifts the curve rightward, enhancing oxygen delivery","D":"Decreased 2,3-BPG increases hemoglobin concentration, improving oxygen capacity","E":"Decreased 2,3-BPG causes immediate hemolysis, preventing oxygen transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During blood storage, 2,3-BPG (2,3-biphosphoglycerate) levels decrease progressively because metabolism continues slowly even at 4°C. Decreased 2,3-BPG shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT, increasing hemoglobin's oxygen affinity. This leftward shift means the transfused hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly and releases it less readily to tissues. Stored blood with depleted 2,3-BPG may have impaired oxygen-delivering capacity despite normal oxygen content, which can be clinically significant in patients receiving large transfusions. After transfusion the red cells gradually regenerate 2,3-BPG and the curve returns toward its normal position. Normal 2,3-BPG keeps the curve shifted slightly right; its absence removes this rightward shift. No effect ignores this important effect. A rightward shift shows the wrong direction. Increased hemoglobin concentration and immediate hemolysis describe incorrect mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and keeps the curve slightly right-shifted; less BPG shifts it left, raising affinity and holding O2 back from tissues. FA lists low 2,3-BPG with CO, methemoglobin and fetal hemoglobin as left-shift causes. COMLEX: A left-shifted curve means a normal saturation can overstate how much O2 actually reaches the tissues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — hemoglobin's role in carrying O2 from alveoli to tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q21_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman in her third trimester asks how her fetus obtains enough oxygen from her blood across the placenta. Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) binds 2,3-BPG less avidly than adult hemoglobin (HbA). How does this property affect oxygen transfer at the placenta?","choices":{"A":"HbF and HbA have identical curves, so transfer depends only on PO₂ gradients","B":"HbF's curve lies left of maternal HbA's, so O₂ moves from maternal to fetal Hb","C":"Weaker 2,3-BPG binding lowers HbF's O₂ affinity, slowing fetal O₂ uptake","D":"HbF's curve lies right of maternal HbA's, so fetal blood unloads O₂ to the mother","E":"Maternal blood begins producing HbF, raising its own oxygen affinity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"2,3-BPG binds deoxyhemoglobin and lowers its O₂ affinity. Because HbF (α2γ2) binds 2,3-BPG less avidly, it has a higher O₂ affinity than HbA, so its dissociation curve lies to the left of the maternal curve; at the placental PO₂, fetal hemoglobin can capture O₂ released by maternal hemoglobin (First Aid, hemoglobin development). Weaker 2,3-BPG binding raises, not lowers, affinity, and the curves are not identical or reversed. The mother does not begin making HbF in pregnancy; her own adaptations include a mild respiratory alkalosis from increased ventilation (First Aid, physiologic changes in pregnancy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HbF (α2γ2) binds 2,3-BPG with lower affinity than HbA (α2β2), so it has higher O2 affinity and a left-shifted curve, letting fetal blood capture O2 from maternal hemoglobin across the placenta. COMLEX: FA's switch mnemonic — 'Alpha always; gamma goes, becomes beta' — tracks the postnatal shift from fetal to adult hemoglobin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — factors that influence hemoglobin saturation and O2 transport","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q22_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Factors shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student plots O₂ saturation against PO₂ for adult hemoglobin and for myoglobin from the same subject. The myoglobin curve lies to the left of the hemoglobin curve and is hyperbolic rather than sigmoidal. Which feature of myoglobin best explains both differences?","choices":{"A":"It binds 2,3-BPG more avidly than hemoglobin","B":"It is a tetramer that resists the Bohr effect","C":"It is a monomer with no positive cooperativity","D":"It contains ferric iron that cannot release O₂","E":"It binds CO₂ in place of O₂ at low PO₂"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First Aid 2025 explains that the sigmoidal shape of hemoglobin's curve comes from positive cooperativity, since the tetramer has higher affinity for each successive O₂ bound, whereas myoglobin is a single polypeptide chain with one heme, shows no cooperativity, and has a hyperbolic curve; it also has a higher affinity for O₂ than hemoglobin, placing its curve to the left, and it is essential for full oxygenation of aerobically active muscle. Avid 2,3-BPG binding would lower affinity and shift a curve to the right. Myoglobin is monomeric, not a tetramer. Ferric (Fe³⁺) iron binds O₂ poorly and describes methemoglobin, not myoglobin. Myoglobin does not substitute CO₂ for O₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myoglobin is a single polypeptide chain with one heme — no positive cooperativity, so its curve is hyperbolic and left of hemoglobin's; hemoglobin's tetramer, with higher affinity for each successive O2, gives the sigmoidal curve. COMLEX: FA — myoglobin's higher O2 affinity is essential for full oxygenation of aerobically active muscle, which is why it is concentrated in working skeletal and cardiac muscle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 6 — intracellular PO2 and the pressure gradients that drive O2 into cells","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q23_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old sprinter's blood is analyzed immediately after a 400-meter race. Her venous blood from leg muscles shows PO₂ of 18 mm Hg, pH of 7.15, PCO₂ of 55 mm Hg, and temperature of 39.5°C. How do these local conditions in active muscle affect oxygen extraction compared to resting conditions?","choices":{"A":"These conditions shift the curve leftward, reducing oxygen extraction to 15%","B":"These conditions have no net effect because factors cancel out","C":"These conditions moderately increase oxygen extraction to 40%","D":"These conditions cause hemoglobin denaturation, preventing oxygen release","E":"These conditions shift the curve rightward, raising extraction to 75-85%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The extreme local conditions in intensely exercising muscle create a powerful rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve through multiple mechanisms: (1) very low pH (7.15, acidosis from lactic acid and CO₂), (2) very high PCO₂ (55 mm Hg from enhanced metabolism), (3) elevated temperature (39.5°C, 2.5°C above normal), and (4) increased 2,3-BPG from sustained exercise. All these factors shift the curve rightward (Bohr effect and other mechanisms), greatly facilitating oxygen release from hemoglobin even at relatively high tissue PO₂ levels. Combined with the very low tissue PO₂ (18 mm Hg), this allows oxygen extraction (utilization coefficient) to reach the 75–85% Guyton gives for strenuous exercise (Ch 41), or higher in local tissue. At 18 mm Hg, hemoglobin saturation might be only 20-25%, meaning 75-80% of oxygen has been extracted. This demonstrates the remarkable physiological adaptations that allow muscles to extract maximal oxygen during intense exercise. A leftward shift to 15%, no net effect, and A moderate rise to 40% underestimate this effect. Hemoglobin denaturation describes a mechanism that doesn't occur at physiological temperatures.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active muscle adds CO2, acids and a 2–3°C temperature rise, each shifting the curve right; together with low tissue PO2 this raises the whole-body utilization coefficient from about 25% to 75–85%. COMLEX: FA — the right shift in exercise widens the arteriovenous O2 difference while arterial PO2 stays normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — exercise effects on O2 demand and diffusion efficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q24_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old marathon runner trains at sea level (PO₂ = 150 mm Hg) and then competes at high altitude (10,000 feet, PO₂ = 110 mm Hg). After 3 weeks of altitude acclimatization, which adaptation helps maintain tissue oxygen delivery despite reduced atmospheric oxygen?","choices":{"A":"Increased 2,3-BPG causing rightward curve shift to enhance tissue oxygen release","B":"Decreased hemoglobin concentration to improve blood flow properties","C":"Rightward curve shift from respiratory alkalosis to enhance oxygen unloading","D":"Decreased cardiac output to conserve oxygen for critical tissues","E":"Conversion to fetal hemoglobin with higher oxygen affinity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At high altitude, the body makes multiple adaptations to maintain tissue oxygen delivery despite reduced atmospheric PO₂. One of the most important adaptations during acclimatization (taking days to weeks) is increased red blood cell 2,3-BPG levels, which shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT. This rightward shift decreases hemoglobin's oxygen affinity, allowing oxygen to be released to tissues at higher PO₂ levels (by as much as 10 mm Hg higher than normal). While this seems counterintuitive (since arterial PO₂ is already reduced), the benefit occurs at the tissue level—oxygen is more readily released from hemoglobin to meet metabolic demands. Other important adaptations include increased hemoglobin concentration (polycythemia, not decreased), increased capillary density, increased mitochondrial density, and hyperventilation. Alkalosis-driven rightward shift is wrong because the respiratory alkalosis of hyperventilation shifts the curve leftward, not rightward. Decreased hemoglobin, decreased cardiac output, and conversion to fetal hemoglobin describe changes that don't occur or would be detrimental.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoxia lasting more than a few hours raises red-cell BPG, shifting the curve right so O2 is released at up to 10 mm Hg higher tissue PO2. FA: altitude response = ↑ ventilation, ↑ erythropoietin → ↑ Hct, ↑ 2,3-BPG, ↑ mitochondria, renal HCO3− excretion. COMLEX: FA notes acetazolamide can augment the renal compensation for the respiratory alkalosis of ascent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — respiratory and renal compensation in high-altitude acclimatization","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q25_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Exercise adaptations enhancing oxygen transport","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old cyclist completes an incremental exercise test. At peak exercise, her pulmonary blood flow is 25 L/min and her pulmonary capillary transit time is reduced to 0.3 seconds. Despite this brief transit time, her arterial oxygen saturation remains at 96%. Which factor is MOST responsible for maintaining adequate oxygenation?","choices":{"A":"Decreased metabolic rate during exercise reduces oxygen demand","B":"Hemoglobin oxygen-binding affinity increases during exercise","C":"Alveolar PO₂ increases to 200 mm Hg during heavy breathing","D":"Diffusing capacity triples; blood saturates in the capillary's first third","E":"Blood velocity decreases during exercise, increasing capillary transit time"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Two critical factors maintain adequate oxygenation despite reduced capillary transit time during exercise: (1) at rest, blood becomes fully saturated with oxygen within the first THIRD of its transit through the pulmonary capillary, providing a 3-fold safety margin; (2) during exercise, the pulmonary diffusing capacity for oxygen increases almost 3-fold (from ~21 to ~65 mL/min/mm Hg) due to recruitment of additional capillary surface area and more ideal ventilation-perfusion ratios. Even though capillary transit time may be reduced from about 0.8 second to about 0.3 second (Guyton Ch 39) (less than half of resting), the combination of the resting safety margin and increased diffusing capacity ensures complete oxygenation. Decreased metabolic rate is incorrect—metabolic rate increases dramatically. Increased hemoglobin affinity is incorrect—affinity actually decreases (Bohr effect). Alveolar PO₂ of 200 mm Hg is incorrect—alveolar PO₂ cannot approach 200 mm Hg on room air, since humidified inspired air has a PO₂ of only about 149 mm Hg (Guyton Ch 40). Slower blood velocity is opposite to what occurs—blood velocity increases with increased cardiac output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two safety factors keep arterial blood saturated in exercise — blood normally saturates within the first third of the capillary (about three times the time needed), and O2 diffusing capacity rises almost 3-fold. COMLEX: Exertional desaturation therefore signals lost reserve — thickened membrane or lost capillary bed — and is a common early finding in ILD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 12 — normal capillary transit time and gas exchange efficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q26_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with glaucoma is prescribed acetazolamide, a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, to reduce intraocular pressure. As a side effect, the drug also inhibits carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells throughout her body. What compensatory mechanism partially maintains CO₂ transport despite carbonic anhydrase inhibition?","choices":{"A":"Increased ventilation rate to compensate for impaired CO₂ transport","B":"Conversion of all CO₂ transport to the carbaminohemoglobin pathway","C":"Enhanced CO₂ solubility in plasma from drug-induced pH changes","D":"Increased hemoglobin concentration to enhance CO₂-carrying capacity","E":"Direct CO₂ excretion through the kidneys bypassing pulmonary elimination"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Red-cell carbonic anhydrase normally makes the bicarbonate route carry about 70% of the CO₂ leaving the tissues; blocking it makes CO₂ transport so poor that tissue PCO₂ can rise toward 80 mm Hg instead of the normal 45. The rising PCO₂ stimulates the central and peripheral chemoreceptors, and the extra ventilation lowers alveolar and arterial PCO₂ — widening the gradient that carries CO₂ out of the tissues and the blood. An all-carbaminohemoglobin pathway is wrong because carbaminohemoglobin cannot take over all CO₂ transport (the slow uncatalyzed reaction and dissolved CO₂ still carry some); enhanced plasma solubility, increased hemoglobin, and renal CO₂ excretion describe effects that do not occur.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Without red-cell carbonic anhydrase, CO2 transport is so poor that tissue PCO2 may rise toward 80 mm Hg; the rising PCO2 and H+ stimulate the chemoreceptors and increase ventilation, partly offsetting the defect. COMLEX: FA — acetazolamide is a sulfa drug used for altitude, idiopathic intracranial hypertension and urinary alkalinization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 7 — how central and peripheral chemoreceptors drive respiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q27_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A physiology student learns that in venous blood, chloride concentration in red blood cells is higher than in arterial blood, while bicarbonate concentration in RBCs is lower in venous blood. What is the primary mechanism responsible for this \"chloride shift\"?","choices":{"A":"Active transport pumps that move Cl⁻ into RBCs in exchange for ATP","B":"Carbonic anhydrase directly transports Cl⁻ across the RBC membrane","C":"A bicarbonate-chloride exchanger that swaps the ions to keep electroneutrality","D":"Hemoglobin binding to chloride ions, pulling them into red blood cells","E":"Passive diffusion of Cl⁻ and HCO₃⁻ following their concentration gradients alone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The chloride shift (also called the Hamburger shift) occurs via a specific bicarbonate-chloride exchanger protein (also known as anion exchanger 1 or Band 3 protein) in the red blood cell membrane. This carrier protein facilitates the reciprocal exchange of HCO₃⁻ and Cl⁻ ions at rapid velocities to maintain electrical neutrality across the RBC membrane. In tissues, as CO₂ enters RBCs and is converted to HCO₃⁻ by carbonic anhydrase, the HCO₃⁻ concentration inside RBCs rises. Much of this HCO₃⁻ diffuses out into plasma to be transported to the lungs. To maintain electrical neutrality as negatively charged HCO₃⁻ leaves the cell, negatively charged Cl⁻ ions enter from the plasma. In the lungs, the process reverses—HCO₃⁻ moves back into RBCs, and Cl⁻ moves out. This explains why venous RBCs have higher Cl⁻ and lower HCO₃⁻ than arterial RBCs. An ATP-driven pump incorrectly invokes ATP. Carbonic anhydrase transport misattributes the transport function. Hemoglobin binding chloride describes incorrect hemoglobin binding. Passive diffusion ignores the specific carrier protein required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The chloride shift — a bicarbonate-chloride carrier protein in the red-cell membrane exchanges HCO3− out for Cl− in at rapid velocity, so venous red cells contain more chloride than arterial red cells. FA's CO2 transport diagram shows the same HCO3−/Cl− exchange. COMLEX: The exchange lets the red cell export most of its bicarbonate to plasma, where FA places about 90% of CO2 (Guyton: about 70%).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport mechanisms and the bicarbonate route","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q28_b1","objective":10,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 10 — CO₂ transport mechanisms including carbonic anhydrase, bicarbonate, and carbaminohemoglobin","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man's blood sample is analyzed in the laboratory. When the blood PCO₂ is 40 mm Hg and the sample is fully oxygenated (PO₂ = 100 mm Hg), the blood contains approximately 48 volume percent CO₂. What approximate CO₂ content would you expect if the same blood were deoxygenated (PO₂ = 40 mm Hg) while maintaining the same PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg?","choices":{"A":"45 volume percent (decreased from oxygenated state)","B":"48 volume percent (unchanged from oxygenated state)","C":"46 volume percent (slightly decreased from oxygenated state)","D":"52 volume percent (significantly increased from oxygenated state)","E":"50 volume percent (slightly increased from oxygenated state)"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the same PCO₂, deoxygenated blood carries more CO₂ than oxygenated blood due to the Haldane effect. When hemoglobin releases oxygen (becomes deoxygenated), it becomes a weaker acid, which has two consequences: (1) reduced hemoglobin can form carbaminohemoglobin more readily, binding more CO₂; (2) reduced hemoglobin takes up H⁺ ions more readily, driving the equilibrium HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃ → CO₂ + H₂O to the left, allowing more CO₂ to be carried as bicarbonate. The CO₂ dissociation curves for oxygenated and deoxygenated blood are shifted vertically by about 2 volume percent: at a PCO₂ of 40 mm Hg, oxygenated blood holds about 48 volume percent CO₂ and deoxygenated blood about 50. This difference is crucial for CO₂ transport—in the tissues, deoxygenation facilitates CO₂ uptake; in the lungs, oxygenation facilitates CO₂ release. 45 volume percent and 46 volume percent incorrectly show decreases. 48 volume percent incorrectly suggests no change. 52 volume percent is the venous value at a PCO₂ of 45 mm Hg — it adds the effect of the higher PCO₂ to the Haldane shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At the same PCO2, deoxygenated blood holds more CO2 than oxygenated blood — reduced hemoglobin is a weaker acid, forms carbaminohemoglobin more readily and binds more H+, favoring bicarbonate formation (Haldane effect). COMLEX: This is why venous blood takes up CO2 in tissues where O2 is unloaded — the two transports reinforce each other.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 2 — CO2 transport and the CO2 dissociation curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q29_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old man is found unconscious in his car with the engine running in a closed garage. His arterial blood shows 40% carboxyhemoglobin (CO-Hgb). Besides directly reducing oxygen-carrying capacity, how does carbon monoxide poisoning additionally impair oxygen delivery to tissues?","choices":{"A":"CO shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT, impairing oxygen release","B":"CO shifts the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the RIGHT, causing excessive release","C":"CO has no effect on the dissociation curve for hemoglobin still bound to oxygen","D":"CO increases 2,3-BPG levels, which partially compensates for reduced oxygen capacity","E":"CO increases oxygen solubility in plasma, which fully compensates for hemoglobin loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Carbon monoxide poisoning impairs oxygen delivery through two mechanisms: (1) directly reducing oxygen-carrying capacity by occupying hemoglobin binding sites, and (2) shifting the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the LEFT for the remaining oxygen-bound hemoglobin. This leftward shift occurs because when some hemoglobin subunits are bound to CO, the remaining subunits that are bound to oxygen have INCREASED affinity for oxygen due to cooperative binding effects. This means the oxygen that is still bound to hemoglobin is held more tightly and released less readily to tissues. This combination—reduced oxygen content AND impaired oxygen release—makes CO poisoning particularly dangerous. For example, with 50% CO-Hgb, the blood carries only half the normal oxygen, and what oxygen remains is released less readily. The leftward shift can lower tissue PO₂ even more than would be predicted from the reduced oxygen content alone, potentially causing tissue hypoxia even at oxygen saturations that might be tolerable in simple anemia. The other options are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO harms O2 delivery twice — it occupies hemoglobin's O2 sites (lower content and %O2Hb) and shifts the curve of the remaining oxyhemoglobin left, so tissues get less of what O2 there is. FA: left shift → ↑ affinity → ↓ unloading; CO is the leading worldwide cause of death by poisoning. COMLEX: FA — CO is classically associated with bilateral globus pallidus lesions on MRI.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 11 — classification of hypoxia, including abnormal hemoglobin transport of O2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q30_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman is exposed to carbon monoxide while camping with a faulty propane heater. Her blood is analyzed and shows 30% carboxyhemoglobin. Despite this significant CO poisoning, her peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) do NOT trigger increased ventilation. Why do peripheral chemoreceptors fail to respond appropriately to CO poisoning?","choices":{"A":"CO directly inhibits peripheral chemoreceptor function, preventing their activation","B":"CO poisoning causes immediate unconsciousness, eliminating all respiratory drive","C":"CO increases PCO₂ dramatically, which overrides the hypoxia signal completely","D":"Peripheral chemoreceptors respond to arterial PO₂, which remains normal in CO poisoning","E":"CO converts to CO₂ in peripheral chemoreceptors, fooling them into sensing normal oxygen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) primarily respond to arterial PO₂ (the partial pressure of dissolved oxygen in arterial blood), NOT to oxygen content or hemoglobin saturation. In CO poisoning, even though oxygen content is dramatically reduced (because hemoglobin sites are occupied by CO), the arterial PO₂ remains normal or even elevated because dissolved oxygen equilibrates normally with alveolar air. The alveolar-to-blood oxygen gradient is unaffected, so dissolved oxygen (measured as PO₂) is normal. Since peripheral chemoreceptors sense PO₂ rather than oxygen content, they fail to detect the tissue hypoxia occurring in CO poisoning. This lack of appropriate ventilatory response makes CO poisoning particularly insidious—victims may become disoriented, weak, and unconscious without experiencing the breathlessness (dyspnea) that would normally accompany severe hypoxia. Direct chemoreceptor inhibition is incorrect—CO doesn't directly inhibit chemoreceptors. Immediate unconsciousness is incorrect—consciousness may be maintained initially. A dramatic PCO₂ rise is incorrect—PCO₂ doesn't dramatically increase. Conversion of CO to CO₂ is incorrect—CO doesn't convert to CO₂.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In CO poisoning arterial PO2 is not reduced, so the feedback that normally raises respiration in hypoxemia is absent, and the blood stays bright red without cyanosis. COMLEX: FA — CO poisoning shows normal PaO2 with ↑ carboxyhemoglobin on co-oximetry, so neither SpO2 nor breathing pattern rules it out.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 6 — central versus peripheral chemoreceptors and what each senses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_ex1_q31_b1","objective":11,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 11 — Effects of carbon monoxide on hemoglobin function and oxygen-carrying capacity","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old firefighter is rescued from a burning building with suspected carbon monoxide poisoning. Blood analysis reveals 45% carboxyhemoglobin. He is treated with 100% oxygen at 3 atmospheres pressure (hyperbaric oxygen therapy). What is the rationale for using hyperbaric oxygen rather than just 100% oxygen at 1 atmosphere?","choices":{"A":"High pressure oxygen damages carboxyhemoglobin molecules, preventing CO rebinding","B":"Hyperbaric conditions cause hemoglobin to release CO more rapidly through pressure effects","C":"Raised O₂ pressure speeds CO displacement and adds dissolved oxygen","D":"High pressure oxygen stimulates new red blood cell production to replace CO-bound cells","E":"Hyperbaric therapy is no more effective than normobaric 100% oxygen for CO poisoning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Raising the inspired O₂ pressure speeds CO removal and adds dissolved O₂. CO binds hemoglobin about 250 times as tightly as O₂ (Guyton Ch 41; > 200 times in First Aid 2025), so a very high alveolar PO₂ is needed to displace it by mass action. The carboxyhemoglobin half-life is about 300–320 minutes on room air, about 80 minutes on 100% O₂ at 1 atm, and about 20 minutes with hyperbaric O₂ at 2–3 atm (First Aid 2025; Katzung Ch 56). Dissolved O₂ rises in proportion to PO₂ (about 0.29 mL/100 mL at a PO₂ of 95 mm Hg, Guyton Ch 41), so at several atmospheres plasma alone carries a useful amount of O₂ while hemoglobin is blocked. Katzung calls hyperbaric O₂ somewhat controversial but recommends considering it when carboxyhemoglobin exceeds 50% and particularly in pregnancy. High pressure does not damage carboxyhemoglobin, force CO off by a direct pressure effect, or act by stimulating red cell production; the faster CO clearance is why it is chosen over normobaric O₂ in severe cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High alveolar PO2 displaces CO from hemoglobin by mass action and adds dissolved O2. FA: CO-Hb half-life is about 300 min on room air, about 80 min on 100% O2 and about 20 min in a hyperbaric chamber. COMLEX: FA — give 100% O2 at once, hyperbaric O2 if severe, and suspect concurrent cyanide poisoning in fire victims.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — hyperbaric oxygen therapy and its mechanism of action","isHighYield":false}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with suspected respiratory failure has arterial blood gases drawn in the emergency department. The laboratory measures his arterial PO₂, PCO₂, and pH using specialized electrodes. Which combination of methods is used to measure these three fundamental parameters?","choices":{"A":"All three measurements use the same polarography electrode technique","B":"All three measurements use variations of the pH meter with glass electrodes","C":"pH: glass electrode; PCO₂: bicarbonate-pH electrode; PO₂: polarography","D":"pH uses colorimetry; PCO₂ uses infrared spectroscopy; PO₂ uses polarography","E":"All three measurements use infrared spectroscopy techniques"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Blood gas measurements use three distinct electrode systems: (1) pH is measured using a pH meter with a glass electrode and reference electrode that measures voltage differences; (2) PCO₂ is measured using a glass pH electrode surrounded by a sodium bicarbonate solution separated from blood by a plastic membrane—CO₂ diffuses into the bicarbonate solution and the resulting pH change is measured and converted to PCO₂ using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation; (3) PO₂ is measured using polarography with a negative platinum electrode separated from blood by a plastic membrane—oxygen diffuses to the electrode and the resulting current flow is directly proportional to PO₂. These three measurements can often be made within a minute using a single droplet of blood, allowing moment-by-moment monitoring at the bedside. The other options incorrectly describe the measurement techniques used for these fundamental parameters.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Blood gases are measured in minutes from a few drops of blood — pH with a glass electrode, PCO2 with a glass pH electrode bathed in bicarbonate behind a CO2-permeable membrane, and PO2 by polarography at a negative platinum electrode. COMLEX: FA's ABG ladder starts with pH (< 7.35 acidemia, > 7.45 alkalemia), then PCO2 and HCO3− to find the primary disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 5 — how O2, CO2 and H+ act as the controlled variables of respiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist needs to rapidly assess pulmonary performance in a patient with acute respiratory distress. Arterial blood gas analysis shows PO₂ of 55 mm Hg, PCO₂ of 60 mm Hg, and pH of 7.28. Why are these three measurements considered the most fundamental tests of pulmonary performance?","choices":{"A":"They directly assess lung volumes, dead space, and respiratory muscle strength simultaneously","B":"They report oxygenation, ventilation, and acid-base status quickly enough to guide therapy","C":"They measure pulmonary diffusing capacity more accurately than other available methods","D":"They are the only tests that can differentiate obstructive from restrictive lung disease","E":"They directly measure airway resistance and lung compliance in real-time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PO₂, PCO₂, and pH are considered the most fundamental tests of pulmonary performance because they provide immediate, crucial information that guides therapy: (1) PO₂ assesses oxygenation adequacy—low PO₂ (hypoxemia) indicates impaired gas exchange; (2) PCO₂ assesses ventilation adequacy—elevated PCO₂ (hypercapnia) indicates alveolar hypoventilation or severe V/Q mismatch; (3) pH assesses acid-base status—changes in pH reflect respiratory compensation (or failure) and metabolic disturbances. These measurements can be made rapidly (within minutes) using only drops of blood, allowing clinicians to determine appropriate therapy for acute respiratory distress or acid-base abnormalities. This patient shows hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and respiratory acidosis, indicating severe respiratory failure requiring immediate intervention. The other options describe capabilities these measurements don't possess—they assess gas exchange status, not mechanical properties or structural characteristics of lungs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton calls arterial PO2, PCO2 and pH among the most fundamental tests of pulmonary performance — often measured within minutes, they report oxygenation, ventilation and acid-base status at once. COMLEX: FA — PCO2 > 44 mm Hg with pH < 7.35 is respiratory acidosis, from hypoventilation, airway obstruction, lung disease, opioids or sedatives, or muscle weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — when hypoxia and hypercapnia coexist and when they occur independently","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with suspected pulmonary disease undergoes arterial blood gas analysis. Her results show PO₂ of 58 mm Hg (normal 80-100), PCO₂ of 48 mm Hg (normal 35-45), and pH of 7.34 (normal 7.35-7.45). What do these measurements reveal about her pulmonary performance?","choices":{"A":"Normal pulmonary function with no gas exchange abnormalities","B":"Isolated metabolic acidosis without respiratory involvement","C":"Normal oxygenation with isolated ventilatory failure","D":"Hyperventilation causing respiratory alkalosis","E":"Impaired oxygenation and ventilation with respiratory acidosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has impaired pulmonary performance on all three measurements: (1) hypoxemia (PO₂ 58 mm Hg, below the normal 80–100), indicating impaired oxygenation; (2) hypercapnia (PCO₂ 48 mm Hg, above 35–45), indicating inadequate alveolar ventilation or severe V/Q mismatch; and (3) pH 7.34, just below 7.35, an acidemia caused by the retained CO₂ — a respiratory acidosis. No bicarbonate is given, so whether the kidneys have begun to compensate cannot be judged from these values. The combination of low PO₂ and high PCO₂ points to significant lung disease such as COPD or severe pneumonia. The values are not normal, the high PCO₂ rules out a purely metabolic process or respiratory alkalosis, and the oxygenation is clearly abnormal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low PO2 means impaired oxygenation, PCO2 above normal means inadequate alveolar ventilation, and pH below 7.35 with a high PCO2 is a respiratory acidosis. FA: PCO2 > 44 mm Hg with acidemia → respiratory acidosis; renal HCO3− reabsorption compensates later. COMLEX: Acute versus chronic is judged by how far HCO3− has risen — compensation is delayed in respiratory disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 10 — the composite effects of PCO2, pH and PO2 on alveolar ventilation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old man performs a maximum expiratory flow maneuver. Despite exerting tremendous additional expiratory force, his expiratory airflow reaches a plateau at 350 L/min and cannot be increased further. What physiological mechanism limits maximum expiratory flow beyond this point?","choices":{"A":"The alveoli become completely emptied of air, preventing further expiration","B":"The expiratory muscles have reached their maximum force-generating capacity","C":"Oxygen depletion in expired air prevents further gas exchange","D":"The diaphragm contracts reflexively to prevent complete lung collapse","E":"Pleural pressure narrows airways as much as it raises driving pressure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Maximum expiratory flow is limited by dynamic airway compression. Once maximum flow is reached, extra expiratory effort raises pleural pressure, which raises alveolar (driving) pressure but compresses the bronchioles from outside by the same amount; because flow equals driving pressure divided by resistance, the two effects cancel and flow cannot rise further, even with greatly increased force (Guyton Ch 43). The flow plateau is effort-independent, so the respiratory muscles are not at their limit — they are generating more force with no gain in flow. The alveoli never empty completely (residual volume remains), the diaphragm relaxes rather than contracts during forced expiration, and O2 content of expired air has nothing to do with expiratory flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In a forced expiration the same pressure that squeezes the alveoli also squeezes the bronchioles; once they nearly collapse, more effort raises alveolar pressure and airway resistance by equal amounts, so flow cannot rise further — the effort-independent maximum expiratory flow. COMLEX: Robbins' emphysema patient ('pink puffer') shows prolonged expiration, sits hunched forward and breathes through pursed lips.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of expiration, including the pleural pressure that compresses the airways","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A physiologist studies maximum expiratory flow at different lung volumes. After a subject inspires maximally to total lung capacity (TLC) and then expires with maximum effort, the maximum expiratory flow rate exceeds 400 L/min. However, as lung volume decreases during the expiration, the maximum flow rate progressively declines. What explains this decrease in maximum expiratory flow as lung volume decreases?","choices":{"A":"Alveolar PO₂ decreases as lungs empty, reducing the driving force for expiration","B":"Respiratory muscles fatigue during prolonged expiratory effort","C":"Decreased oxygen content in remaining alveolar air reduces gas diffusion rates","D":"Airways collapse more easily at low volumes as elastic tethering is lost","E":"Increased airway resistance from mucus accumulation blocks smaller airways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Maximum expiratory flow decreases as lung volume decreases primarily because bronchi and bronchioles become more susceptible to dynamic compression at lower lung volumes. When lungs are fully inflated (at TLC), the bronchi and bronchioles are held open by elastic tethering—radial traction from lung structural elements pulling outward on airway walls. As lung volume decreases during expiration, these elastic structures become relaxed, reducing the outward tethering force that normally helps keep airways open. With less structural support, the bronchi and bronchioles collapse more easily when subjected to external pressure from forced expiration, progressively reducing maximum expiratory flow. This explains why the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shows highest flow rates at large lung volumes and progressively decreasing flow as volume decreases toward residual volume. The other options describe mechanisms that either don't occur or aren't the primary cause of flow limitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the expanded lung, bronchi and bronchioles are held open partly by elastic pull of lung structural elements on their outsides; as the lung empties these relax, the airways collapse more easily under chest pressure, and maximum expiratory flow falls progressively with volume. COMLEX: FA — emphysema raises compliance and lowers elastic recoil, removing this support, so flow collapses early in expiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the major obstructive pulmonary diseases and their mechanics","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old asthmatic patient performs a forced expiration test. Her physician notes that she reaches a maximum expiratory flow of only 200 L/min (normal >400 L/min). During which phase of respiration is airway obstruction typically more severe in asthma, and why?","choices":{"A":"Inspiration is more obstructed because negative pleural pressure collapses airways","B":"Both phases are equally obstructed due to bronchial smooth muscle constriction","C":"Expiration is more obstructed because positive pleural pressure further narrows already constricted airways","D":"Inspiration is more obstructed because airway dilation during expiration compensates for bronchoconstriction","E":"Neither phase is significantly obstructed because asthma primarily affects alveolar function"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In asthma and other obstructive diseases, expiration is typically much more difficult than inspiration. During forced expiration, positive pleural pressure (from chest compression) not only drives air out but also compresses airways from the outside. In asthmatic patients with already narrowed bronchioles from smooth muscle spasm, edema, and mucus, this external compression causes airways to collapse even more readily than normal, greatly increasing airway resistance during expiration. In contrast, during inspiration, negative pleural pressure actually pulls airways open (radial traction) while simultaneously expanding alveoli, making inspiration relatively easier. This explains why asthmatic patients often can inspire adequately but have great difficulty expiring—air becomes trapped in lungs, leading to increased functional residual capacity and residual volume. Inspiration being more obstructed shows the wrong direction—negative pressure opens airways. Equal obstruction of both phases is incorrect because phases are not equally affected. Inspiration from expiratory dilation and no significant obstruction are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With airway obstruction it is much harder to expire than to inspire — positive expiratory chest pressure adds to the closing tendency of narrowed airways, while negative inspiratory pleural pressure pulls them open; air enters easily and is trapped. COMLEX: FA — asthma is diagnosed by an obstructive spirometry pattern with bronchodilator response (it may be normal between attacks), +/− methacholine challenge.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the etiology and pathogenesis of asthma and its phenotypes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist reviews maximum expiratory flow-volume curves from two patients with different lung diseases. Patient A has pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive disease), while Patient B has emphysema (obstructive disease). How would their curves differ from normal in terms of lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Both patients show increased total lung capacity and increased residual volume","B":"Restrictive disease shows reduced TLC; obstructive shows normal TLC","C":"Both patients show normal lung volumes but reduced maximum expiratory flow rates","D":"Obstructive disease shows increased TLC; restrictive shows normal TLC and increased RV","E":"Restrictive disease shows reduced TLC and RV; obstructive shows increased TLC and RV"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Restrictive and obstructive diseases produce opposite patterns on flow-volume curves: (1) Restrictive diseases (pulmonary fibrosis, kyphosis, scoliosis) cause REDUCED total lung capacity (TLC) and REDUCED residual volume (RV) because the lungs cannot expand normally due to stiffness or chest cage limitation. The flow-volume curve is shifted downward and leftward, with reduced volumes at all points. (2) Obstructive diseases (emphysema, severe asthma) cause INCREASED TLC and INCREASED RV due to air trapping—airways close during expiration, trapping air in alveoli. Over time, this increases both TLC and RV. The obstructive curve shows reduced maximum expiratory flow rates and is shifted upward and rightward. A normal TLC with a raised RV does not describe restriction: Guyton (Ch 43) notes that constricted lungs have both reduced TLC and reduced RV. The other options don't correctly describe these opposite patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Constricted (restrictive) lungs have reduced TLC and RV; obstruction traps air so TLC and RV rise over months to years. FA: obstructive — ↑ RV, ↑ FRC, ↑ TLC, flow-volume loop shifted left; restrictive — ↓ volumes, loop shifted right. COMLEX: Per the pulmonary OMM lab, both patterns raise the work of breathing — from about 2–3% of energy expenditure at baseline to as much as 30–35% in significant disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 10 — the lung capacities IC, FRC, VC and TLC","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q10_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis undergoes pulmonary function testing. Her maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shows a total lung capacity of 3.5 L (normal 5.5 L) and residual volume of 0.8 L (normal 1.2 L). Why is her maximum expiratory flow rate also reduced despite having restrictive, not obstructive, disease?","choices":{"A":"She cannot reach the large lung volumes at which the highest flow rates occur","B":"Fibrosis directly obstructs airways similar to asthma, causing expiratory flow limitation","C":"Reduced lung compliance decreases the elastic recoil force available to drive expiration","D":"Fibrotic tissue produces excess mucus that blocks airways during forced expiration","E":"The diaphragm is weakened in restrictive disease, preventing forceful expiration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In restrictive lung disease like pulmonary fibrosis, maximum expiratory flow rate is reduced even though the primary problem is not airway obstruction. Maximum expiratory flow depends heavily on lung volume—flow rates are highest when lungs are fully inflated to total lung capacity because airways are maximally stretched open by elastic tethering from surrounding lung tissue. In this patient, her TLC is only 3.5 L (instead of normal 5.5 L) because fibrosis prevents normal lung expansion. Since she cannot reach normal lung volumes even with maximum inspiratory effort, she cannot achieve the high expiratory flow rates that would occur at those larger volumes. Her maximum expiratory flow rate is reduced because the starting point (TLC) is so much lower, not because of intrinsic airway obstruction. The curve shows reduced peak flow with a relatively normal shape, just compressed to smaller volumes. Fibrotic airway obstruction, excess mucus, and diaphragm weakness describe mechanisms that don't occur in restrictive disease. Reduced elastic recoil mentions elastic recoil but this isn't the primary reason for reduced flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Because the constricted lung cannot expand to a normal maximum volume, even maximal effort cannot raise expiratory flow to the normal curve — flow is highest at high lung volumes. COMLEX: FA — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis gives progressive dyspnea, dry cough, velcro crackles and clubbing, with a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern and honeycombing in advanced disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and the other major restrictive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q11_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with severe COPD/emphysema has a maximum expiratory flow-volume curve that shows increased total lung capacity (7.0 L, normal 5.5 L) and dramatically increased residual volume (3.5 L, normal 1.2 L). What pathophysiological mechanism primarily causes these increased lung volumes?","choices":{"A":"Enhanced respiratory muscle strength from chronic training increases lung expansion capacity","B":"Loss of alveolar walls decreases lung compliance, holding the lungs stiffly expanded","C":"Mucus hypersecretion expands airways, increasing total lung air volume","D":"Airway collapse during expiration traps air in alveoli, progressively increasing volumes over time","E":"Pulmonary hypertension forces more blood into lungs, displacing air into larger volumes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obstructive diseases like emphysema, airways tend to collapse during expiration because: (1) positive pleural pressure during expiration compresses airways; (2) loss of elastic tethering from destroyed alveolar walls means airways aren't held open normally; (3) airways may be intrinsically narrowed from inflammation and mucus. During each expiration, airways close prematurely, trapping air distal to the obstruction. This trapped air cannot be expired, leading to progressive air accumulation over time. Over months to years, this causes both TLC and RV to increase significantly—this patient's RV of 3.5 L is nearly three times normal. The increased volumes represent air that cannot be expelled due to airway closure, not increased lung capacity from other mechanisms. Stronger respiratory muscles is incorrect—muscles don't cause this. Decreased compliance is wrong because loss of alveolar walls increases, not decreases, compliance. Mucus hypersecretion and pulmonary hypertension are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstruction makes expiration harder than inspiration, so air is trapped and, over months or years, both TLC and RV rise. FA: emphysema CXR shows a barrel chest, increased AP diameter, flattened diaphragm and increased lucency. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab ties chronic COPD hyperinflation to a barrel chest with shortened respiratory muscles and increased resting tone — targets for rib and diaphragm OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four main types of emphysema and their morphology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with suspected COPD performs forced vital capacity (FVC) maneuvers. His results show FVC of 4.5 L (normal 5.0 L) and FEV₁ of 2.1 L (normal 4.0 L). What is his FEV₁/FVC ratio, and what does this indicate?","choices":{"A":"FEV₁/FVC = 90%; indicates normal airway function","B":"FEV₁/FVC = 47%; indicates significant airway obstruction","C":"FEV₁/FVC = 53%; indicates mild restrictive disease","D":"FEV₁/FVC = 80%; indicates normal pulmonary function","E":"FEV₁/FVC = 35%; indicates severe restrictive disease"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient's FEV₁/FVC ratio is (2.1 L / 4.5 L) × 100% = 47%, which indicates significant airway obstruction. In healthy individuals, the FEV₁/FVC ratio is normally about 80%—meaning 80% of the total forced vital capacity can be expired in the first second. As airway obstruction worsens, this ratio decreases progressively because airways narrow or collapse during expiration, greatly slowing expiratory airflow. This patient can only expire 47% of his FVC in the first second, indicating substantial obstruction consistent with moderate-to-severe COPD. The relatively preserved FVC (4.5 L vs. normal 5.0 L) indicates this is primarily an obstructive process rather than restrictive disease. In severe airway obstruction such as acute asthma, the FEV₁/FVC ratio can decrease to less than 20%. 90% and 80% show normal ratios. 53% with restriction and 35% with restriction incorrectly suggest restrictive disease, which would show reduced FVC with preserved or increased FEV₁/FVC ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FEV1/FVC is about 80% normally (Guyton) and falls to 47% in his obstruction example, below 20% in serious acute asthma; FA calls > 70% normal, with FEV1 reduced more than FVC in obstruction. COMLEX: A low FEV1/FVC is the spirometric definition of obstruction in COPD and asthma, so it is the first number to read on a PFT report.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the major obstructive lung diseases and their clinical features","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q13_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist explains to a medical student that the FEV₁/FVC ratio is one of the most important measurements for distinguishing obstructive from restrictive lung disease. In a patient with pure restrictive disease (pulmonary fibrosis), what would be the expected pattern of FEV₁, FVC, and their ratio?","choices":{"A":"Reduced FEV₁, reduced FVC, normal or increased ratio (≥80%)","B":"Reduced FEV₁, normal FVC, reduced ratio (<70%)","C":"Normal FEV₁, normal FVC, normal ratio (~80%)","D":"Normal FEV₁, reduced FVC, increased ratio (>90%)","E":"Reduced FEV₁, reduced FVC, markedly reduced ratio (<50%)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In pure restrictive disease such as pulmonary fibrosis the lungs cannot expand normally, so FVC is reduced, and FEV₁ falls roughly in proportion; because the stiff lungs empty quickly, the FEV₁/FVC ratio stays normal or even rises (Guyton Ch 43). A markedly reduced ratio (<50%) or a ratio below 70% with normal FVC is the obstructive pattern, in which airway narrowing slows emptying so FEV₁ falls more than FVC. All-normal values exclude disease, and a normal FEV₁ with a reduced FVC is not the expected pattern — in restriction FEV₁ falls along with FVC.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In restriction FVC and FEV1 fall roughly together, so FEV1/FVC is normal or increased; FA: restrictive disease lowers FVC and TLC with a normal or ↑ ratio. COMLEX: FA splits restriction by DLCO and A-a gradient — normal in neuromuscular or chest-wall disease, reduced DLCO with ↑ A-a gradient in interstitial disease such as pneumoconioses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — the major pneumoconioses, classic interstitial causes of restriction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q14_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with asthma exacerbation performs spirometry. Before bronchodilator treatment, her FEV₁ is 1.8 L and FVC is 3.6 L (FEV₁/FVC = 50%). After inhaled albuterol, her FEV₁ increases to 2.7 L while FVC increases to 4.0 L (FEV₁/FVC = 67.5%). What does this bronchodilator response indicate?","choices":{"A":"The obstruction is fixed and irreversible, indicating emphysema rather than asthma","B":"Bronchodilator therapy is ineffective and should be discontinued immediately","C":"Normal pulmonary function has been completely restored by bronchodilator therapy","D":"The patient has restrictive lung disease that paradoxically responds to bronchodilators","E":"Significant reversible airway obstruction is present, consistent with asthma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient demonstrates significant bronchodilator reversibility, which is characteristic of asthma. Her FEV₁ improved by 0.9 L (50% increase from baseline), and her FEV₁/FVC ratio improved from 50% to 67.5%. This substantial improvement indicates that much of her airway obstruction is due to reversible bronchospasm rather than fixed structural changes. In asthma, bronchodilators relieve smooth muscle constriction, improving airflow. The improvement in both FEV₁ (absolute expired volume) and FEV₁/FVC ratio (relative expiratory rate) confirms reduced airway obstruction. While she hasn't returned completely to normal (normal FEV₁/FVC ≥80%), the significant response supports asthma diagnosis and indicates bronchodilator therapy should be continued. In contrast, emphysema shows little bronchodilator response because obstruction is from destroyed alveolar walls and loss of elastic recoil, not reversible bronchospasm. The other options are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asthma is reversible obstruction — FEV1/FVC is low during an attack and FEV1 rises after a bronchodilator. FA: spirometry shows an obstructive pattern with bronchodilator response but may be normal between exacerbations; methacholine challenge can support the diagnosis. COMLEX: FA's mechanism — type I hypersensitivity, hyperresponsive bronchi and reversible bronchoconstriction with mucus plugging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes, such as β2 agonists, used in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q16_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with 40-pack-year smoking history has chronic pulmonary emphysema. Pathological examination of his lung tissue would show which major structural changes?","choices":{"A":"Alveoli filled with inflammatory cells and fluid with intact alveolar walls","B":"Widespread bronchial dilation with hypertrophied smooth muscle and intact alveoli","C":"Thickened alveolar walls with increased collagen deposition and fibrosis throughout","D":"Destruction of 50-80% of alveolar walls with enlarged, coalesced air spaces","E":"Normal alveolar architecture with isolated areas of mucus plug obstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chronic pulmonary emphysema is characterized by marked destruction of alveolar walls, with loss of 50-80% of the walls in severe cases. This destruction results from chronic infection and inflammation (from smoking or pollution), which causes: (1) airway obstruction leading to air trapping and alveolar overstretching; (2) release of proteolytic enzymes from inflammatory cells that digest elastin and other structural proteins; (3) combination of mechanical stress and enzymatic degradation destroying alveolar septa. The result is enlarged air spaces where multiple alveoli have coalesced into larger, irregular spaces (sometimes called \"bullae\"). This dramatically reduces the surface area available for gas exchange and destroys pulmonary capillaries embedded in alveolar walls. Alveoli filled with inflammatory cells and fluid describes pneumonia. Bronchial dilation with hypertrophied muscle describes bronchiectasis or asthma. Thickened, fibrotic alveolar walls describes pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive disease, opposite of emphysema). Normal architecture with mucus plugs doesn't describe emphysema's widespread destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic infection, mucus and bronchiolar edema obstruct small airways, trapping and overstretching alveoli; with infection this destroys as much as 50–80% of alveolar walls, leaving large coalesced air spaces. COMLEX: FA — centriacinar emphysema (upper lobes) goes with smoking, panacinar (lower lobes) with α1-antitrypsin deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four main types of emphysema and their morphology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q18_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman with severe emphysema has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 55 mm Hg (hypoxemia) and PCO₂ of 58 mm Hg (hypercapnia). What pathophysiological mechanisms in emphysema cause BOTH hypoxia and hypercapnia to occur?","choices":{"A":"Increased metabolic rate raises O₂ consumption and CO₂ production beyond pulmonary capacity","B":"Alveolar wall destruction reduces diffusing capacity; V/Q mismatch creates shunt and dead space","C":"Pulmonary hypertension reduces blood flow through lungs, limiting gas exchange time","D":"Mucus hypersecretion blocks all airways equally, preventing both O₂ uptake and CO₂ elimination","E":"Loss of chemoreceptor sensitivity prevents appropriate ventilatory responses to hypoxia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Emphysema causes both hypoxemia and hypercapnia through multiple mechanisms: (1) Marked loss of alveolar walls (50-80% destruction) greatly decreases diffusing capacity, reducing the lung's ability to oxygenate blood and remove CO₂; (2) Extremely abnormal ventilation-perfusion ratios occur because obstruction is worse in some lung regions than others—some areas have very low V̇A/Q̇ (physiological shunt, poorly oxygenated blood) while other areas have very high V̇A/Q̇ (physiological dead space, wasted ventilation). This combination reduces both oxygenation and CO₂ elimination; (3) Bronchiolar obstruction and increased work of breathing may lead to overall hypoventilation of functional alveoli. The result is progressive, devastating hypoxia and hypercapnia that can last for years. Increased metabolic rate is incorrect—metabolism is usually normal or reduced. Pulmonary hypertension contributes but isn't primary. Uniform mucus obstruction and loss of chemoreceptor sensitivity are incorrect or incomplete.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's emphysema effects — bronchiolar obstruction raises work of breathing; loss of alveolar walls lowers diffusing capacity; very low V/Q (shunt) and very high V/Q (dead space) coexist; capillary loss raises pulmonary vascular resistance. Hypoxia and hypercapnia follow from hypoventilation of many alveoli plus wall loss. COMLEX: FA — emphysema lowers DLCO, a measurable marker of the lost membrane.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt and dead space in COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q19_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from two patients: one with emphysema and one with bacterial pneumonia. What is the fundamental difference in alveolar pathology between these two conditions?","choices":{"A":"Emphysema shows alveolar wall thickening; pneumonia shows alveolar wall thinning","B":"Both conditions show identical alveolar pathology with different degrees of inflammation","C":"Emphysema shows fluid-filled alveoli; pneumonia shows air-filled but collapsed alveoli","D":"Emphysema shows intact alveoli with bronchial obstruction; pneumonia shows destroyed alveoli","E":"Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and enlarges air spaces; pneumonia fills intact alveoli"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Emphysema and pneumonia have fundamentally different alveolar pathologies: EMPHYSEMA is characterized by (1) destruction of alveolar walls with loss of 50-80% of walls in severe cases; (2) enlarged, coalesced air spaces where multiple alveoli have merged; (3) loss of pulmonary capillaries; (4) reduced surface area for gas exchange. The alveoli are overstretched and destroyed but still contain air. PNEUMONIA is characterized by (1) intact but inflamed alveolar walls; (2) alveoli filled with fluid, white blood cells, red blood cells, and bacteria; (3) progressive \"consolidation\" of lung tissue as infection spreads; (4) alveolar walls remain structurally present but are highly porous, leaking fluid. The key difference: emphysema destroys walls, pneumonia fills intact spaces. The other options incorrectly describe these pathologies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and enlarges air spaces; pneumonia fills intact alveoli with fluid and blood cells, consolidating whole lobes or a lung. COMLEX: FA — loss of alveolar walls raises compliance in emphysema, while pneumonia lowers compliance; both impair gas exchange by different routes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the four histologic stages of lobar pneumonia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q20_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student studies lung histology slides from emphysema and pneumonia patients. In emphysema, what happens to the pulmonary capillaries that are normally embedded in alveolar walls?","choices":{"A":"Capillaries proliferate and increase in number to compensate for reduced gas exchange","B":"Capillaries remain intact but become dilated from pulmonary hypertension","C":"Capillaries are destroyed along with the alveolar walls, reducing pulmonary vascular beds","D":"Capillaries migrate into bronchial walls to maintain some gas exchange capacity","E":"Capillaries remain completely normal despite extensive alveolar destruction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When alveolar walls are destroyed in emphysema, the pulmonary capillaries embedded in those walls are also destroyed. Loss of large portions of alveolar walls decreases the number of pulmonary capillaries through which blood can pass. This has several important consequences: (1) reduced surface area for gas exchange; (2) increased pulmonary vascular resistance because there are fewer capillary channels for blood flow; (3) pulmonary hypertension develops from increased resistance; (4) right-sided heart strain and eventual right heart failure (cor pulmonale). The destruction of 50-80% of alveolar walls means loss of a similar proportion of pulmonary capillaries, dramatically reducing the lung's capacity for gas exchange and blood flow. The other options incorrectly describe capillary changes—they are destroyed, not proliferated, dilated, migrated, or preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of alveolar walls also removes the pulmonary capillaries within them, so pulmonary vascular resistance rises markedly, causing pulmonary hypertension that overloads the right heart and often causes right-sided failure. COMLEX: FA — chronic hypoxic vasoconstriction adds to this, leading to pulmonary hypertension +/− cor pulmonale.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, its causes, mechanisms and classification","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q21_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A radiologist describes a chest X-ray showing bullous changes with large irregular air spaces in a patient with end-stage emphysema. What underlying structural change creates this appearance?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-filled bronchi creating air-fluid levels throughout the lungs","B":"Multiple abscesses with air-fluid interfaces scattered through lung tissue","C":"Coalescence of destroyed alveoli into large, irregular air spaces (bullae)","D":"Fluid-filled alveoli interspersed with normal air-filled alveoli","E":"Thickened interstitial septa creating a reticular pattern on imaging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The bullous appearance of emphysematous lungs results from coalescence of multiple destroyed alveoli into large, irregular air spaces called bullae. As alveolar walls are progressively destroyed (50-80% loss in severe cases), adjacent alveoli merge into larger and larger spaces. Instead of normal tiny alveoli (about 0.2 mm in diameter, Guyton Ch 40), the lung contains enlarged, irregular air spaces (several millimeters to centimeters in diameter) with markedly reduced surface area for gas exchange. This creates the characteristic appearance on gross pathology and imaging. The destruction is irreversible—once alveolar walls are destroyed, they cannot regenerate. This explains why emphysema progressively worsens and why smoking cessation can only slow progression, not reverse existing damage. Honeycombing is the end-stage fibrotic pattern of interstitial lung disease (Robbins Ch 15), not emphysema. The other options describe different pathologies, not emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: As alveolar walls are destroyed, neighboring air spaces coalesce; Robbins notes apical blebs or bullae in advanced disease, and FA describes increased lucency with a flattened diaphragm and barrel chest. COMLEX: Robbins — subpleural blebs may rupture in emphysematous lungs, causing a fatal pneumothorax, so sudden dyspnea in emphysema demands a chest film.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — other forms of emphysema, including bullous and focal emphysematous changes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q22_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with right lower lobe pneumonia has arterial oxygen saturation of 78% (normal 95-100%). Blood from his right pulmonary vein (draining the pneumonic lung) is 60% saturated, while blood from his left pulmonary vein (draining the healthy lung) is 97% saturated. What pulmonary abnormality primarily explains his reduced arterial oxygen saturation?","choices":{"A":"Pneumonic lung is perfused but poorly ventilated, creating low V/Q and venous admixture","B":"Increased metabolic rate from infection consumes oxygen faster than it can be delivered","C":"Reduced total lung volume from consolidation prevents adequate oxygen intake","D":"Inflammation destroys pulmonary capillaries, preventing blood from reaching functional alveoli","E":"Bacterial toxins directly poison hemoglobin, preventing normal oxygen binding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pneumonic lobe's alveoli are filled with exudate, so its ventilation falls while its blood flow continues: a low V/Q ratio. Blood leaving that lobe is poorly oxygenated (60% saturated here) and mixes with fully saturated blood from the healthy lung — venous admixture — lowering systemic arterial saturation (Guyton Ch 43). Pneumonia fills the alveoli but does not destroy the capillaries; if perfusion of the lobe stopped, its blood could not lower arterial saturation. Lung volume per se, a faster metabolic rate, and a toxin acting on hemoglobin do not explain a pulmonary-vein saturation of 60% on one side and 97% on the other.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In one-lung pneumonia the consolidated side keeps its blood flow but loses ventilation (low V/Q); Guyton's example — aerated lung blood 97% saturated, unaerated about 60%, so aortic saturation averages only about 78%. COMLEX: FA — V/Q mismatch and shunt raise the A-a gradient, whereas hypoventilation and low inspired O2 do not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — the ventilation-perfusion ratio and its effect on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q23_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with bilateral pneumonia involving both lower lobes has arterial oxygen saturation of 72%. Besides the decreased V/Q ratio, what other pulmonary abnormality contributes to her severe hypoxemia?","choices":{"A":"Increased diffusing capacity from inflammatory mediator effects on capillaries","B":"Decreased total available surface area of the respiratory membrane from consolidation","C":"Enhanced oxygen extraction by metabolically active inflammatory cells in alveoli","D":"Increased pulmonary blood flow compensating for reduced ventilation","E":"Hyperventilation of healthy lung regions completely compensating for diseased regions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Besides decreased V/Q ratio, pneumonia also reduces total available surface area of the respiratory membrane. As alveoli progressively fill with fluid, cells, and debris, those alveoli are no longer available for gas exchange—they are \"consolidated\" and non-functional. In bilateral lower lobe pneumonia, large areas of both lungs may be consolidated, dramatically reducing the total membrane surface area where oxygen can diffuse from air into blood. Pneumonia thus causes two major abnormalities: (1) reduced V/Q ratio in partially affected areas (ventilation reduced but perfusion continues); (2) reduced total respiratory membrane surface area in fully consolidated areas. Both effects contribute to hypoxemia. When significant portions of both lungs are involved (bilateral pneumonia), hypoxemia becomes severe, as in this patient with 72% saturation. The other options either don't occur or don't contribute to hypoxemia. Healthy regions cannot make up the difference, because blood through aerated lung is already about 97% saturated and cannot carry more O2, so the mixed aortic blood stays well below normal (about 78% in Guyton's example).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton names two pneumonia abnormalities — reduced total surface area of the respiratory membrane and a decreased ventilation-perfusion ratio; both cause hypoxemia and hypercapnia. COMLEX: Bilateral consolidation multiplies both effects, which is why multilobar pneumonia carries a higher risk of respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — disease states that reduce gas diffusion efficiency","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q24_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with lobar pneumonia is treated with appropriate antibiotics and supportive care. As the infection resolves and inflammatory fluid is reabsorbed from the alveoli, what happens to the arterial oxygen saturation?","choices":{"A":"Saturation remains permanently reduced because pneumonia causes irreversible alveolar wall destruction","B":"Saturation decreases further as healing fibrosis reduces lung compliance","C":"Saturation fluctuates unpredictably depending on bacterial toxin levels in blood","D":"Saturation shows no change because capillary damage from inflammation is permanent","E":"Saturation gradually improves as alveolar ventilation is restored and V/Q ratio normalizes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pneumonia the alveolar walls remain intact; the alveoli are filled with exudate rather than destroyed. As the infection resolves and the fluid is reabsorbed, ventilation returns to the affected alveoli, the low V/Q regions and venous admixture disappear, and arterial saturation gradually improves (Guyton Ch 43). Permanent wall or capillary destruction is the picture of emphysema, not lobar pneumonia, and healing pneumonia does not usually leave fibrosis that worsens saturation. Saturation follows the gas-exchange abnormality, not circulating bacterial toxin levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pneumonia fills alveoli with fluid and cells but does not destroy their walls (Guyton Fig 43.5 contrasts it with emphysema), so as the exudate clears, ventilation, V/Q and saturation recover. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab lists O2 when indicated, antibiotics and OMT (rib raising, lymphatic pump, diaphragm release) as adjuncts while the lung recovers; MOPSE found shorter hospital stay with OMT per protocol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the four classic stages of lobar pneumonia, ending in resolution","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q25_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains to a patient with pneumonia why supplemental oxygen therapy may help improve oxygen saturation. Which statement best explains why oxygen therapy can be beneficial in pneumonia?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen therapy directly kills bacteria, accelerating resolution of infection","B":"High inspired oxygen increases alveolar PO₂, improving oxygen gradient even in poorly ventilated regions","C":"Oxygen therapy reverses alveolar consolidation by drying inflammatory fluid","D":"Supplemental oxygen has no benefit in pneumonia because the problem is fluid-filled alveoli, not low inspired oxygen","E":"Oxygen directly restores normal V/Q ratios by dilating airways and removing fluid"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Oxygen therapy benefits pneumonia patients by increasing the alveolar PO₂ in regions that still have some ventilation, improving the diffusion gradient for oxygen into blood. Even in poorly ventilated alveoli (those partially filled with fluid), increasing inspired oxygen concentration can raise alveolar PO₂ enough to improve oxygenation of blood perfusing those regions. By breathing higher oxygen concentrations (30-100% O₂ instead of room air's 21%), the patient can partially compensate for reduced V/Q ratios. However, oxygen therapy doesn't address the underlying problem (fluid-filled alveoli) and won't help regions with complete consolidation (zero ventilation, V/Q = 0). It's a supportive measure while antibiotics treat the infection. Antibiotics address the cause; oxygen provides symptomatic support. Bacterial killing is incorrect—oxygen doesn't kill bacteria. Drying the consolidation, no benefit, and direct restoration of V/Q are incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Supplemental O2 raises alveolar PO2 wherever some ventilation remains; in diffusion-type hypoxia it can raise alveolar PO2 from about 100 toward 600 mm Hg, increasing the diffusion gradient by more than 800% (Guyton's pulmonary-edema example shows O2 uptake 3–4 times faster). COMLEX: FA — true shunt (V/Q = 0) does not correct with 100% O2, so persistent hypoxemia on high-flow O2 signals shunt physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 12 — the value of oxygen therapy in different types of hypoxia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q26_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with COPD has arterial blood gases measured using a blood gas analyzer. The PCO₂ measurement relies on a pH electrode surrounded by sodium bicarbonate solution separated from the blood sample by a thin plastic membrane. What principle allows this system to measure PCO₂?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen molecules displace CO₂ in the bicarbonate solution, creating measurable current flow","B":"CO₂ molecules are too large to cross the membrane, creating a concentration gradient","C":"CO₂ diffuses into the bicarbonate solution, and the pH change is converted to PCO₂","D":"The plastic membrane generates voltage proportional to CO₂ concentration","E":"CO₂ binds to hemoglobin in the bicarbonate solution, changing its optical density"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The PCO₂ electrode uses a clever indirect measurement technique: CO₂ from the blood sample diffuses through a thin plastic membrane (which is permeable to CO₂) into a sodium bicarbonate solution that surrounds a standard glass pH electrode. When CO₂ enters the bicarbonate solution, it forms carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), which dissociates into H⁺ and HCO₃⁻, changing the pH of the bicarbonate solution. The pH electrode measures this pH change, and the analyzer uses the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation to mathematically convert the pH change to the corresponding PCO₂ of the blood sample. This is why it's called a \"Severinghaus electrode\" or \"bicarbonate-pH electrode system.\" Oxygen displacing CO₂ is incorrect because oxygen doesn't displace CO₂ in this measurement. CO₂ too large to cross is wrong because CO₂ must cross the membrane for measurement. Membrane-generated voltage incorrectly describes how the membrane works. Hemoglobin binding is incorrect because there's no hemoglobin in the bicarbonate solution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The PCO2 electrode is a glass pH electrode bathed in weak sodium bicarbonate behind a CO2-permeable membrane; CO2 diffusing in changes the pH, and PCO2 is calculated from the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation, pH = 6.1 + log(HCO3−/0.03 × PCO2). COMLEX: The same equation (FA) links ABG values — knowing any two of pH, PCO2 and HCO3− defines the third.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 10 — CO2 transport through carbonic anhydrase and bicarbonate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q27_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A critical care physician receives blood gas results showing PO₂ of 48 mm Hg, PCO₂ of 72 mm Hg, and pH of 7.18 on a mechanically ventilated patient. The laboratory reports these values were obtained within 90 seconds from a single small blood sample. What makes rapid simultaneous measurement of these three parameters possible at the bedside?","choices":{"A":"All three electrodes use the same basic glass electrode technology with different membranes","B":"Modern analyzers use optical spectroscopy that measures all parameters instantaneously","C":"The pH, CO₂ (bicarbonate-pH) and O₂ (platinum) electrodes can be miniaturized together","D":"Capillary action separates the blood into three chambers for sequential measurement","E":"The measurements are actually performed on expired air rather than blood"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid simultaneous measurement of PO₂, PCO₂, and pH is possible because the three different electrode systems can be miniaturized and positioned adjacent to each other in a single analyzer unit that requires only drops of blood. The pH electrode (glass electrode), PCO₂ electrode (bicarbonate-pH system), and PO₂ electrode (platinum polarography) are separate technologies but can be housed together, allowing simultaneous measurements within a minute or so (Guyton Ch 43) from a single small sample. This rapid turnaround time makes blood gas analysis practical for moment-by-moment monitoring at bedside, crucial for managing critically ill patients. This patient shows severe respiratory failure with marked hypoxemia, severe hypercapnia, and uncompensated respiratory acidosis, requiring immediate ventilator adjustment. Identical glass electrodes is incorrect because they use different technologies. Optical spectroscopy is wrong—these analyzers use electrodes, not optical spectroscopy. Capillary separation incorrectly describes the sampling process. Measurement on expired air is wrong—arterial blood must be measured directly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton notes the pH, CO2 and O2 electrodes can be miniaturized and combined, so blood gases are measured within minutes using no more than a few drops of blood. COMLEX: FA — in a ventilated patient with pH < 7.35 and PCO2 > 44, the respiratory acidosis points to inadequate ventilation, guiding ventilator changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 14 — hypoxia and hypercapnia, together or independently","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q28_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — PO₂, PCO₂, and pH as fundamental measures of pulmonary performance","batch":1,"stem":"A respiratory therapist draws arterial blood for analysis on a patient with acute pulmonary embolism. The PO₂ measurement uses a platinum electrode with a negative voltage separated from blood by an oxygen-permeable membrane. What physical process allows this electrode to measure oxygen tension?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen molecules absorb light at specific wavelengths, creating measurable optical changes","B":"Oxygen is reduced at the platinum electrode, producing a current proportional to PO₂","C":"Oxygen binds to chemicals on the membrane surface, generating voltage","D":"The membrane expands when exposed to oxygen, changing electrical capacitance","E":"Oxygen molecules carry positive charge that attracts electrons from the platinum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The PO₂ electrode uses polarography (Clark electrode): a platinum cathode with negative voltage (about -0.6 volts) is covered by an oxygen-permeable plastic membrane. Oxygen from the blood diffuses through the membrane to reach the platinum electrode surface, where it undergoes reduction by accepting electrons from the negatively charged platinum (O₂ + 4e⁻ → 2O²⁻). This electron acceptance creates a current flow that is directly proportional to the PO₂—more oxygen in the blood means more O₂ molecules reach the electrode, more electrons are accepted, and greater current flows. An ammeter measures this current and converts it to mm Hg of oxygen pressure. This direct electrochemical measurement makes polarography highly accurate and responsive. Light absorption describes optical methods not used in standard blood gas analyzers. Membrane chemical binding and membrane expansion describe mechanisms that don't occur. Positively charged oxygen incorrectly describes oxygen's electrical properties—oxygen molecules are electrically neutral.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Polarography — when a platinum electrode is held more than −0.6 volt from the solution, O2 deposits on it, and the current is directly proportional to O2 concentration and therefore PO2. COMLEX: FA lists pulmonary embolism among causes of hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, so PE often gives a low PaO2 with a low PaCO2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — the pathophysiology of pulmonary embolism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q29_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A healthy 25-year-old man inhales to total lung capacity and then expires with maximal effort into a flow-volume recorder. According to Guyton, approximately what maximum expiratory airflow does a healthy person reach quickly in this maneuver, a value that greater effort cannot raise?","choices":{"A":"Near 40 L/min","B":"Near 100 L/min","C":"Over 400 L/min","D":"Near 2,000 L/min","E":"Near 6 L/min"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton's maximum expiratory flow-volume curve shows a healthy person quickly reaching a maximum expiratory airflow of more than 400 L/min after a maximal inspiration; regardless of additional expiratory effort, this remains the maximum because the same pressure that compresses the alveoli also compresses and narrows the bronchioles, so resistance rises as much as driving pressure. The flow then declines as lung volume falls. A flow near 6 L/min is the resting minute ventilation, not an instantaneous expiratory flow. Flows near 40 and 100 L/min fall well below the normal peak and are values seen in obstructed or constricted lungs, not in health. A flow near 2,000 L/min is several times higher than Guyton's figure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: After a maximal inspiration a healthy person quickly reaches a maximum expiratory airflow of more than 400 L/min; regardless of additional effort, flow at each lung volume cannot exceed this maximum — the principle of effort-independent expiratory flow. COMLEX: FA — obstruction lowers FEV1 more than FVC, so the early, effort-limited part of the forced expiration is what falls first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of inspiration and expiration and the pressures that drive airflow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q30_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist analyzes flow-volume curves from a healthy 30-year-old man who performed maximum forced expiration from TLC. The curve shows peak expiratory flow of 500 L/min at high lung volumes, but this decreases to 200 L/min when only 20% of vital capacity remains. The patient claims he maintained maximum effort throughout the maneuver. Why does maximum expiratory flow decrease progressively as lung volume falls?","choices":{"A":"CO₂ accumulation in remaining alveolar air reduces the diffusion gradient for expiration","B":"Respiratory muscle force decreases as the chest wall reaches its minimum volume configuration","C":"Mucus progressively accumulates in smaller airways, increasing resistance","D":"Decreased alveolar surface area at low volumes reduces gas exchange efficiency","E":"Loss of elastic radial traction on airways at low volumes makes them collapse more easily"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Maximum expiratory flow decreases as lung volume decreases primarily because of changes in elastic radial traction on airways. At high lung volumes (near TLC), the lung parenchyma is stretched, creating outward radial forces that pull on the bronchial and bronchiolar walls, helping keep them open (elastic tethering). This structural support resists dynamic compression during forced expiration. As lung volume decreases during expiration, the lung parenchyma relaxes, reducing these radial traction forces. With diminished elastic support, the airways collapse more easily when subjected to external compression from positive pleural pressure, progressively limiting maximum flow. By the time lung volume approaches residual volume, radial traction is minimal and airways are highly susceptible to collapse, explaining the marked reduction in maximum flow. This volume-dependence of flow creates the characteristic shape of the maximum expiratory flow-volume curve. The other options either don't occur or aren't the primary mechanism for progressive flow reduction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At high lung volumes the airways are held open by elastic pull of surrounding lung tissue; as volume falls this support relaxes, the airways collapse more easily, and maximum expiratory flow declines even with maximal effort. COMLEX: FA — emphysema raises compliance and lowers elastic recoil, so the same support is lost at every volume and the loop shifts left.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of expiration and the pressures that set airway caliber","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q31_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Principle of maximum expiratory flow","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with emphysema performs maximum forced expiration. His peak expiratory flow reaches only 180 L/min compared to predicted 450 L/min, and his flow rate decreases very rapidly as lung volume falls. His physician explains that his airways collapse more readily than normal during expiration. What pathophysiological change in emphysema increases susceptibility to dynamic airway compression?","choices":{"A":"Destruction of alveolar attachments reduces elastic radial traction supporting airways","B":"Bronchial smooth muscle hypertrophy increases baseline airway resistance","C":"Mucus hypersecretion directly obstructs the airways, reducing their cross-sectional area","D":"Inflammation of airway walls increases their thickness, making them more rigid","E":"Decreased surfactant production increases surface tension in small airways"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls eliminates the normal elastic attachments between alveoli and airways. These attachments normally provide radial traction—outward pulling forces that help keep airways open, particularly during expiration. Loss of these structural connections reduces the mechanical support for airways, making them much more susceptible to dynamic compression during forced expiration. Even at relatively high lung volumes where normal individuals maintain good airway patency, emphysema patients experience premature airway collapse because of inadequate radial traction. This explains why emphysema patients have markedly reduced maximum expiratory flow rates and why their flow-volume curves show very rapid decline in flow as volume decreases. Additionally, the loss of elastic recoil means lower driving pressures for expiration while airways are simultaneously more prone to collapse—a double problem. Smooth muscle hypertrophy and mucus hypersecretion describe features of chronic bronchitis, not emphysema's primary mechanism. Wall inflammation and rigidity incorrectly suggests increased rigidity (emphysema airways are actually more collapsible). Decreased surfactant incorrectly describes surfactant's role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys the alveolar walls that tether small airways, removing their elastic pull, so bronchioles collapse readily under expiratory chest pressure. FA: elastase–antiprotease imbalance destroys elastic fibers; loss of recoil and ↑ compliance follow. COMLEX: FA — α1-antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema of the lower lobes in younger patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the types of emphysema and their pathogenesis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q32_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"Guyton describes a constricted maximum expiratory flow-volume curve, with reduced total lung capacity, reduced residual volume, and a peak flow that cannot reach normal. Some patients have this pattern from lung fibrosis, but it also arises from disease outside the lung. Which patient would most likely show this pattern?","choices":{"A":"A man with chronic asthma and air trapping","B":"A woman with panacinar emphysema from α1-AT loss","C":"A smoker with chronic bronchitis and mucus plugs","D":"A woman with severe thoracic kyphoscoliosis","E":"A man with bronchiectasis and copious sputum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton lists the constricted-lung diseases as fibrotic lung diseases such as tuberculosis and silicosis and diseases that constrict the chest cage, such as kyphosis, scoliosis, and fibrotic pleurisy; because the lung cannot expand to a normal maximum volume, TLC and RV are reduced and maximal expiratory flow cannot rise to normal. Severe kyphoscoliosis restricts the chest cage and produces this pattern even with normal lung tissue. Chronic asthma, panacinar emphysema, chronic bronchitis with mucus plugging, and bronchiectasis are airway-obstruction diseases, in which air enters easily but is trapped, so TLC and RV rise and maximum expiratory flow is greatly reduced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Guyton's constricted lung diseases — fibrotic lung disease (tuberculosis, silicosis) and chest-cage constriction (kyphosis, scoliosis, fibrotic pleurisy) — reduce TLC and RV and keep peak flow below normal. COMLEX: FA classes chest-wall restriction (scoliosis, severe obesity) as extrapulmonary with normal DLCO and A-a gradient; the pulmonary OMM lab aims OMT at thoracic cage and diaphragm mechanics to lower the work of breathing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the chest-wall structures and mechanics that expand the lungs in inspiration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q33_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"Over several years, a 50-year-old woman with poorly controlled asthma shows a progressive rise in both total lung capacity and residual volume on serial testing. Guyton explains this in terms of how airway caliber changes between the two phases of breathing. Which explanation best fits?","choices":{"A":"Inspiration pulls airways open, but expiration closes them and traps air","B":"Both phases narrow the airways equally and trap air throughout","C":"Fibrosis stiffens the lungs and holds them at a higher resting volume","D":"Alveolar walls thicken and add tissue volume to the measured TLC","E":"Weak inspiratory muscles leave more air in the lungs after each breath"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton explains that with airway obstruction it is much more difficult to expire than to inspire: the extra positive chest pressure required for expiration greatly increases the closing tendency of the airways, whereas the extra negative pleural pressure of inspiration pulls the airways open while it expands the alveoli. Air therefore enters easily but becomes trapped, and over months or years this increases both TLC and RV; in asthma the chest may become permanently enlarged (barrel chest). Equal narrowing in both phases would not explain why air enters easily but cannot leave. Fibrosis lowers TLC and RV, producing a constricted curve. Thickened alveolar walls reduce diffusion, not gas volume. Weak inspiratory muscles would limit how much air enters, lowering TLC rather than raising it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In airway obstruction expiration is much harder than inspiration — expiratory positive pressure adds to airway closure, while inspiratory negative pleural pressure pulls airways open — so air enters easily, becomes trapped, and over months or years TLC and RV rise. COMLEX: Robbins notes some patients with otherwise typical asthma develop an irreversible component, which is why long-standing asthma can come to resemble COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinical differentiation of the obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q34_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Obstructive vs restrictive disease on Maximum Expiratory Flow-Volume Curve","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients perform maximum forced expiration maneuvers. Patient A (with emphysema) shows TLC of 8.5 L, RV of 5.0 L, and VC of 3.5 L. Patient B (with pulmonary fibrosis) shows TLC of 3.8 L, RV of 0.9 L, and VC of 2.9 L. Although Patient A has 5.0 L of air remaining at RV while Patient B has only 0.9 L remaining, why can't Patient A expire this \"extra\" 4.1 L of air?","choices":{"A":"The extra air is located in non-ventilated bullae that don't communicate with airways","B":"Patient A's respiratory muscles are too weak to generate additional expiratory force","C":"The extra air represents increased blood volume in pulmonary capillaries, not actual gas","D":"Patient A's airways collapse during expiration, trapping air distally before reaching RV","E":"Patient A's alveoli are completely rigid and cannot compress further"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obstructive disease like emphysema, the markedly elevated RV (5.0 vs 0.9 L in the other patient, 1.5 L normal) represents air trapping from premature airway closure during expiration. As Patient A expires, two pathological processes cause airways to close before the lungs can empty completely: (1) loss of elastic radial traction from destroyed alveolar attachments makes airways collapse more easily; (2) loss of elastic recoil reduces driving pressure for expiration. Airways collapse while significant air remains trapped distally in alveoli. This trapped air is ventilated and participates in gas exchange during the breathing cycle, but cannot be fully expelled even with maximum expiratory effort. This is why obstructive disease increases RV—it's not that the lungs have larger capacity, but rather that airways close prematurely, preventing complete emptying. The \"extra\" 4.1 L is trapped behind closed airways. Non-communicating bullae might describe some air in bullae but doesn't explain most of the RV increase. Weak respiratory muscles is incorrect—muscle strength isn't the limiting factor. Increased capillary blood volume incorrectly identifies the trapped air. Rigid alveoli is wrong—emphysematous alveoli are actually more compliant (less elastic), not rigid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In obstruction, airways close during expiration before the alveoli empty, trapping air so RV and TLC rise. FA: RV and any capacity that contains it (FRC, TLC) cannot be measured by spirometry. COMLEX: Guyton measures FRC indirectly by helium dilution, from which RV and TLC are then calculated — the way air trapping is quantified.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 11 — spirometry's limits and the helium dilution method for FRC","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q35_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with chronic dyspnea performs spirometry. Her results show FEV1 of 1.8 L (predicted 3.0 L), FVC of 3.0 L (predicted 3.8 L), giving an FEV1/FVC ratio of 60% (predicted 80%). What pathophysiological process best explains the reduced FEV1/FVC ratio?","choices":{"A":"Reduced lung compliance prevents full inspiration, limiting both FEV1 and FVC equally","B":"Weakness of inspiratory muscles reduces the driving force for expiration","C":"Narrowed or collapsing airways limit expiratory flow more than volume","D":"Loss of diffusing capacity reduces oxygen uptake, limiting expiratory effort","E":"Increased elastic recoil causes rapid lung emptying, reducing measurable time"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An FEV1/FVC ratio of 60% (normal ~80%, abnormal <70%) indicates obstructive lung disease. This pattern occurs because airway obstruction or premature airway collapse disproportionately affects the rate of expiration (FEV1) more than the total volume that can eventually be expired (FVC). In the first second of forced expiration, flow is limited by narrowed or collapsed airways, so only 1.8 L can be expelled (reduced FEV1). Given more time and continued effort, the patient can eventually expire a total of 3.0 L (FVC), but this takes much longer than normal. The key pathophysiology: obstructive diseases create a bottleneck that slows expiratory flow. This might result from: (1) structural airway narrowing (chronic bronchitis, asthma), (2) loss of elastic radial traction allowing airway collapse (emphysema), or (3) mucus plugging. All cause disproportionate reduction in FEV1 compared to FVC, lowering the ratio. Reduced compliance describes restrictive disease which maintains normal or increased ratio. Inspiratory muscle weakness doesn't specifically affect the ratio. Loss of diffusing capacity and increased elastic recoil don't explain the pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstruction slows the rate of emptying more than the total volume emptied, so FEV1 falls more than FVC and the ratio drops (Guyton: normal about 80%; FA: normal > 70%). COMLEX: FA lists COPD (chronic bronchitis, emphysema), asthma and bronchiectasis as the obstructive diseases to sort with this ratio.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q36_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with chronic asthma performs spirometry before and after bronchodilator administration. Pre-treatment: FEV1 2.0 L, FVC 4.0 L, ratio 50%. Post-treatment: FEV1 2.8 L, FVC 4.2 L, ratio 67%. What explains the greater improvement in FEV1 than FVC after bronchodilator?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilators increase inspiratory capacity more than expiratory capacity","B":"Bronchodilators lower airway resistance, most improving early expiratory flow","C":"Bronchodilators primarily affect alveolar compliance rather than airway caliber","D":"The medication corrects respiratory muscle weakness, enhancing force generation","E":"Bronchodilators increase elastic recoil, proportionally improving all lung volumes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient shows classic reversible airway obstruction (asthma). The bronchodilator causes greater improvement in FEV1 (from 2.0 to 2.8 L, +0.8 L, +40%) than in FVC (from 4.0 to 4.2 L, +0.2 L, +5%), resulting in improved FEV1/FVC ratio (from 50% to 67%). This differential response occurs because bronchodilators work by relaxing airway smooth muscle, increasing airway diameter, and reducing airway resistance. The reduction in airway resistance particularly improves the rate of air movement during the early, rapid phase of forced expiration—when flow is most limited by airway caliber. With wider airways, more air can escape in the first second (increased FEV1). The FVC also improves slightly because reduced air trapping allows more complete lung emptying, but this effect is smaller than the flow rate improvement. The pattern confirms reversible airway obstruction, distinguishing asthma from fixed obstruction like emphysema. The other options incorrectly describe bronchodilator mechanisms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A bronchodilator relaxes bronchiolar smooth muscle, lowering airway resistance and raising early expiratory flow (FEV1) more than total exhaled volume (FVC), so the ratio improves. FA: asthma spirometry shows an obstructive pattern with bronchodilator response. COMLEX: Robbins separates asthma from chronic bronchitis and emphysema by reversible bronchospasm, while noting overlap — some COPD has a reversible component.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for each pharmacologic class in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q37_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — FEV1, FVC, and FEV1/FVC ratio with airway obstruction","batch":1,"stem":"A pulmonologist evaluates spirometry from three patients. Patient A: FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 3.0 L, ratio 83%. Patient B: FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 4.5 L, ratio 56%. Patient C: FEV1 2.0 L, FVC 2.3 L, ratio 87%. All three have reduced FEV1 compared to predicted (3.5 L). Which patient(s) have obstructive disease?","choices":{"A":"Only Patient A","B":"Only Patient B","C":"Only Patient C","D":"Patients A and C","E":"Patients B and C"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The FEV1/FVC ratio is the key discriminator between obstructive and restrictive patterns. Normal ratio is approximately 80% (Guyton Ch 43), and a ratio below 0.7 generally indicates obstruction (Robbins Ch 15). Patient A has FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 3.0 L, ratio 83%—this is a restrictive pattern (both FEV1 and FVC reduced proportionally, ratio normal/high). Patient B has FEV1 2.5 L, FVC 4.5 L, ratio 56%—this is obstructive disease (FEV1 disproportionately reduced compared to FVC, ratio <70%). Patient C has FEV1 2.0 L, FVC 2.3 L, ratio 87%—this is also restrictive (both reduced proportionally, ratio elevated). The crucial concept: in obstruction, airways limit flow rate more than volume, so FEV1 falls more than FVC, decreasing the ratio. In restriction, lung expansion is limited, reducing all volumes proportionally, so the ratio remains normal or even increases (because elastic recoil may be increased, causing faster emptying). Therefore, only Patient B has obstruction. Note that reduced FEV1 alone doesn't diagnose obstruction—the ratio is essential. The other options incorrectly identify the obstructive pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The FEV1/FVC ratio, not FEV1 alone, separates the patterns — a low ratio means obstruction; a normal or high ratio with low volumes means restriction. Guyton gives about 80% as normal; FA calls > 70% normal. COMLEX: FA's table — obstruction: FEV1 ↓↓, FVC ↓, ratio ↓; restriction: FEV1 ↓, FVC ↓, ratio normal or ↑.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the obstructive pulmonary diseases and their features","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q38_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with severe emphysema has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 52 mm Hg and PCO₂ of 58 mm Hg. Chest CT demonstrates destruction of 60% of alveolar walls with formation of large emphysematous spaces. Which pathophysiological change from alveolar wall destruction most directly causes his hypoxemia?","choices":{"A":"Complete obstruction of pulmonary arterioles prevents blood flow to remaining functional alveoli","B":"Reduced alveolar surface area decreases the respiratory membrane available for O₂ diffusion","C":"Destruction of pulmonary capillaries eliminates the pathway for oxygen delivery to tissues","D":"Increased dead space ventilation prevents any oxygen from reaching alveolar spaces","E":"Loss of type II pneumocytes eliminates surfactant production, causing alveolar collapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alveolar wall destruction in emphysema (typically 50-80% of walls in severe disease) directly reduces the respiratory membrane surface area available for gas exchange. Normal lungs have approximately 70 square meters of alveolar-capillary surface area; in emphysema it is often decreased as much as 5-fold (Guyton Ch 40). Even though remaining alveoli may be adequately ventilated and perfused, the total oxygen diffusion capacity is limited by reduced surface area. Additionally, the walls contain the pulmonary capillaries, so their destruction also reduces the capillary bed for gas exchange. This creates ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch: some areas have adequate ventilation but reduced perfusion (high V/Q, dead space), while other areas have adequate perfusion but reduced ventilation (low V/Q, physiological shunt). The combination of reduced membrane surface area and V/Q mismatch causes hypoxemia. Complete arteriolar obstruction is incorrect—arterioles aren't completely obstructed. Capillary destruction eliminating delivery overstates the problem. Dead space preventing any O₂ entry is wrong—oxygen does reach alveoli. Loss of type II pneumocytes incorrectly describes emphysema pathology (surfactant isn't the primary issue).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of alveolar walls greatly decreases the lung's diffusing capacity, reducing its ability to oxygenate blood and remove CO2. FA: emphysema lowers DLCO (Fick: area ↓). COMLEX: Robbins' emphysema-dominant 'pink puffer' has low diffusion capacity yet relatively normal resting blood gases, with severe overdistention and prolonged expiration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity, its definition and calculation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q39_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with emphysema develops worsening hypercapnia with PCO₂ rising from 48 to 62 mm Hg over three months. Her respiratory rate is 16 breaths/min (normal 12-20), and tidal volume is normal. Despite apparently adequate minute ventilation, why is her CO₂ elimination impaired?","choices":{"A":"Her alveoli are completely collapsed and not participating in ventilation","B":"CO₂ diffuses too slowly across thickened alveolar walls to reach expired air","C":"Increased dead space ventilation and V/Q mismatch reduce alveolar ventilation effectiveness","D":"Bronchospasm prevents CO₂-rich air from exiting the lungs during expiration","E":"Her respiratory rate is too low to eliminate the increased CO₂ production from infection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypercapnia in emphysema results from increased physiological dead space and ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch, not simply from reduced minute ventilation. Alveolar wall destruction creates several problems for CO₂ elimination: (1) Some areas develop high V/Q ratios (ventilated but poorly perfused) functioning as dead space—ventilation to these regions doesn't eliminate CO₂ effectively because little blood delivers CO₂ there. (2) Destruction reduces capillary surface area for CO₂ exchange. (3) Some regions develop low V/Q ratios where perfusion exceeds ventilation, allowing CO₂-rich blood to pass without adequate CO₂ removal. Although her minute ventilation appears adequate, much of it is \"wasted\" ventilating dead space, so alveolar ventilation (the portion that actually exchanges with capillary blood) is inadequate. As emphysema progresses, dead space fraction increases, progressively impairing CO₂ elimination and causing hypercapnia. Collapsed alveoli is wrong—alveoli aren't collapsed, they're enlarged. Slow diffusion across thickened walls incorrectly describes the pathology (walls are destroyed, not thickened). Bronchospasm describes asthma, not emphysema's primary problem. A low respiratory rate assumes inadequate respiratory rate without evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema creates very high V/Q regions (physiological dead space, wasted ventilation) alongside very low V/Q regions (shunt), so normal minute ventilation can still deliver too little effective alveolar ventilation. FA: alveolar ventilation = (VT − VD) × RR. COMLEX: Rising dead space raises the work of breathing needed just to hold PaCO2 steady.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiological shunt versus physiological dead space","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q40_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Emphysema pathophysiology - hypoxia and hypercapnia","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student asks why patients with emphysema develop both hypoxemia and hypercapnia, while some other pulmonary diseases cause only hypoxemia. What property of oxygen and carbon dioxide transport explains why CO₂ retention indicates more severe disease?","choices":{"A":"Oxygen diffuses more rapidly than CO₂ across alveolar membranes, so CO₂ is affected first","B":"CO₂ is about 20 times more diffusible than O₂, so retention needs more severe impairment","C":"Oxygen transport depends on hemoglobin while CO₂ dissolves freely in plasma","D":"Carbon dioxide has higher molecular weight, making it harder to eliminate","E":"Oxygen and CO₂ are equally affected; patients just perceive hypoxemia symptoms sooner"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbon dioxide is approximately 20 times more diffusible across biological membranes than oxygen due to its much higher solubility in tissue fluids and membranes. This means that even when respiratory membrane surface area is reduced or V/Q mismatching occurs, CO₂ can still diffuse adequately and be eliminated, while O₂ transfer becomes impaired. Therefore, hypoxemia typically appears before hypercapnia in progressive pulmonary disease. When hypercapnia develops, it indicates that pulmonary impairment has become so severe that even the highly diffusible CO₂ cannot be adequately eliminated. In emphysema, patients develop hypoxemia relatively early as alveolar surface area decreases and V/Q mismatch develops. Hypercapnia appears later when: (1) surface area is so reduced that even CO₂'s high diffusibility cannot compensate, (2) dead space ventilation becomes so extensive that alveolar ventilation is grossly inadequate, or (3) respiratory muscle fatigue develops. The presence of hypercapnia therefore indicates advanced disease. Faster O₂ diffusion reverses the relative diffusibility. Hemoglobin-dependent O₂ transport and higher CO₂ molecular weight don't explain the clinical pattern. Equal effects is incorrect—timing does differ.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CO2 diffuses about 20 times as rapidly as O2, so diffusion defects cause hypoxemia first; hypercapnia appears only when alveolar ventilation itself falls. FA: diffusion-limiting disease is more likely to cause early hypoxia than hypercapnia. COMLEX: New CO2 retention in a COPD patient therefore marks advanced disease or acute ventilatory failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 8 — diffusion rates of O2 versus CO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q41_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from two deceased patients. Patient A (emphysema) shows large airspaces measuring 5-10 mm with virtually absent intervening walls. Patient B (lobar pneumonia) shows alveolar spaces filled with inflammatory cells and fluid, but walls remain intact. What accounts for this fundamental difference in alveolar wall integrity?","choices":{"A":"Emphysema involves enzymatic destruction of alveolar walls; pneumonia involves exudation without structural destruction","B":"Emphysema results from viral infection; pneumonia results from bacterial infection with different pathophysiology","C":"Emphysema causes wall thickening mistaken for absence; pneumonia causes true wall destruction","D":"Both diseases destroy alveolar walls, but pneumonia walls regenerate whereas emphysema walls cannot","E":"The difference is artifact from tissue preparation; actual pathology is identical"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The fundamental pathological distinction between emphysema and pneumonia lies in alveolar wall integrity. Emphysema involves permanent destruction of alveolar walls through enzymatic degradation (primarily by elastase when antiprotease defense is inadequate), creating enlarged airspaces from confluence of adjacent alveoli. The walls are physically absent—destroyed. This destruction is irreversible; once walls are eliminated, they cannot regenerate. In contrast, pneumonia involves filling of alveolar spaces with inflammatory exudate (neutrophils, bacteria, fluid, fibrin) but the alveolar walls themselves remain intact. The walls are structurally preserved even though the spaces are filled. This distinction has crucial implications: emphysema causes permanent structural damage and irreversible loss of gas exchange surface area, while pneumonia is potentially reversible—once infection resolves and exudate clears, the intact walls allow alveoli to return to normal function and gas exchange capacity. The other options incorrectly describe or equate these fundamentally different pathological processes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls — FA: imbalance of proteases and antiproteases (↑ elastase activity) causes loss of elastic fibers; pneumonia fills intact alveoli with exudate. COMLEX: FA — α1-antitrypsin, the antiprotease that inhibits neutrophil elastase, is deficient in panacinar emphysema.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the etiology and morphology of the emphysema types","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q42_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A radiologist compares chest CT scans from two patients with dyspnea. The emphysema patient shows areas of decreased attenuation (darker) representing large emphysematous spaces. The pneumonia patient shows areas of increased attenuation (whiter) representing consolidated lung. What histological difference between these diseases explains the opposite radiographic appearances?","choices":{"A":"Emphysema walls are calcified (hyperdense); pneumonia walls are destroyed (hypodense)","B":"The radiographic difference is technical artifact, not reflecting actual pathology","C":"Emphysema increases pulmonary blood flow; pneumonia decreases blood flow","D":"Emphysema thickens alveolar walls with fibrosis; pneumonia causes wall thinning","E":"Emphysema enlarges air-filled spaces; pneumonia fills them with fluid and cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The opposite radiographic appearances directly reflect the underlying pathology. In emphysema, alveolar wall destruction creates enlarged air-filled spaces (bullae, blebs)—essentially more air with less tissue per unit volume. Since air has very low radiographic density, these regions appear darker (decreased attenuation, radiolucent) on CT. The enlarged spaces can measure 5-10+ mm compared to normal alveoli of about 0.2 mm (Guyton Ch 40). In pneumonia, alveolar spaces fill with exudate (inflammatory cells, bacteria, protein-rich fluid), replacing air with higher-density material. Since cells and fluid have much higher radiographic density than air, these consolidated regions appear white (increased attenuation, opacification) on CT. The walls themselves remain intact but cannot be visualized individually; the consolidated airspace is what's visible. This pathological difference (destroyed walls creating large air spaces vs. intact walls with filled spaces) produces characteristic and opposite radiographic patterns that aid diagnosis. The other options incorrectly describe the pathological changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema means more air and less tissue per volume (FA: increased lung field lucency); pneumonia consolidates lobes with fluid and cellular debris, raising density. COMLEX: Guyton — consolidation can involve whole lobes or a whole lung, and the lost ventilation of that tissue is what drives the hypoxemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 4 — lobar pneumonia versus bronchopneumonia, gross distribution and histology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q43_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Alveolar changes in emphysema vs pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A physician explains to a medical student that emphysema causes permanent disability while pneumonia, despite acute severity, is potentially reversible. The student asks what structural difference allows complete recovery from pneumonia but not emphysema. What is the correct explanation?","choices":{"A":"Pneumonia destroys bronchi which can regenerate; emphysema destroys alveoli which cannot regenerate","B":"Pneumonia leaves alveolar walls intact so they recover; emphysema destroys them permanently","C":"Pneumonia affects only one lung; emphysema affects both lungs simultaneously","D":"Pneumonia lasts only days whereas emphysema lasts weeks, allowing adaptation","E":"Pneumonia stimulates surfactant production; emphysema inhibits surfactant permanently"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The reversibility distinction is fundamental: pneumonia preserves alveolar wall architecture while filling spaces with exudate that can be cleared, whereas emphysema permanently destroys the alveolar walls themselves. In pneumonia, once the infection is controlled (antibiotics kill bacteria), the inflammatory response resolves and the exudate (cells, fluid, fibrin) is cleared by macrophages and lymphatic drainage. Since the underlying structural framework (alveolar walls with their capillary networks, elastic tissue, and epithelium) remains intact, the alveoli can return to normal architecture and function. Gas exchange surface area is restored. In emphysema, proteolytic enzymes destroy alveolar walls—the walls are physically absent, degraded. These walls contain specialized cells (type I and II pneumocytes), capillary networks, and elastic fibers that cannot regenerate once destroyed. The body cannot rebuild alveolar walls, so emphysema causes permanent loss of gas exchange surface area and irreversible functional impairment. This pathological distinction explains why pneumonia has good recovery potential while emphysema causes progressive, irreversible disability. The other options don't accurately explain the fundamental difference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pneumonia's alveolar walls remain intact while the spaces fill with exudate, so clearing the exudate restores function; emphysema permanently destroys the alveolar walls themselves. COMLEX: Guyton — emphysema progresses slowly over many years toward devastating dyspnea, so smoking cessation is the key intervention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the stages of lobar pneumonia, ending in resolution","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q44_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with lobar pneumonia affecting the right lower lobe has arterial blood gases showing PO₂ of 58 mm Hg (oxygen saturation 88%) despite adequate ventilation. The affected lobe's alveoli are filled with inflammatory exudate. What mechanism most directly causes her hypoxemia?","choices":{"A":"Complete absence of ventilation to the affected lobe creates absolute anatomical shunt","B":"Low ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio in the affected lobe creates physiological shunt","C":"Increased V/Q ratio in the affected lobe increases dead space ventilation","D":"Destruction of alveolar walls reduces total respiratory membrane surface area","E":"Bronchospasm prevents oxygen from reaching any alveoli in the affected lobe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pneumonia causes hypoxemia primarily through creating low ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio regions—physiological shunt. In the affected lobe, alveoli are partially or completely filled with inflammatory exudate (cells, bacteria, fluid), markedly reducing ventilation to those alveoli. However, pulmonary blood flow (perfusion) to the affected region initially continues relatively normally. This creates low V/Q regions: blood flows past poorly ventilated alveoli without adequate oxygenation, then this poorly oxygenated blood mixes with well-oxygenated blood from healthy lung regions (venous admixture), reducing overall arterial oxygen content. While some alveoli may be completely unventilated (absolute shunt), most pneumonic alveoli retain some ventilation (partial obstruction), making this physiological shunt rather than anatomical shunt. The body attempts compensation through hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (reducing blood flow to poorly ventilated regions), but this is incomplete. Absolute anatomical shunt overstates the ventilation loss. Increased V/Q reverses the V/Q change. Alveolar wall destruction describes emphysema. Bronchospasm isn't the primary mechanism in pneumonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exudate-filled alveoli are perfused but poorly ventilated, giving a low VA/Q that acts as physiological shunt and lowers arterial O2. FA: V/Q = 0 is shunt, does not correct with 100% O2, and raises the A-a gradient. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab ties pneumonia to T2–T6 viscerosomatic findings — Still emphasized ribs 6–8 — and to rib raising among adjunctive OMT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — VA/Q and its effect on alveolar gas pressures","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q45_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student observes that a patient with right lower lobe pneumonia has hypoxemia (PO₂ 55 mm Hg) despite normal ventilation to the left lung and right upper/middle lobes. The student calculates that 75% of lung tissue is functioning normally. Why doesn't the healthy lung tissue compensate fully for the abnormal region?","choices":{"A":"The healthy lung tissue becomes inflamed and ceases gas exchange","B":"Healthy alveoli reflexly hypoventilate to match the diseased lobe's ventilation","C":"Poorly oxygenated blood from low V/Q regions mixes with well-oxygenated blood","D":"The heart preferentially shunts blood to the diseased lung lobe","E":"Normal lung tissue reduces its oxygen extraction to maintain homeostasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypoxemia in regional lung disease results from venous admixture—the mixing of poorly oxygenated blood (from diseased regions) with well-oxygenated blood (from healthy regions). In this patient, healthy lung regions (75%) ventilate and perfuse normally, fully oxygenating blood passing through them (PO₂ ~100 mm Hg). However, the diseased region (25%) has low V/Q ratio: blood flows past fluid-filled alveoli with inadequate oxygen uptake, emerging with a low PO₂, close to that of venous blood. When these two blood streams mix in the left atrium (venous admixture), the overall arterial PO₂ represents the weighted average of the two streams. Even though 75% of blood is well-oxygenated, the remaining 25% with low oxygen content significantly reduces the mixed arterial PO₂. The healthy lung tissue cannot compensate by \"extra\" oxygenation—blood leaving healthy alveoli is already ~98% saturated; increasing PO₂ further adds negligible oxygen due to hemoglobin saturation plateau. Thus regional disease causes systemic hypoxemia through venous admixture that healthy tissue cannot overcome. Inflamed healthy tissue, preferential shunting to the diseased lobe, and reduced O₂ extraction describe mechanisms that don't occur. Reflex hypoventilation is wrong because the healthy regions do not reflexly hypoventilate; they are fully ventilated and their blood is already nearly saturated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Well-ventilated alveoli cannot raise their blood above about 97% saturation, so they cannot offset poorly oxygenated blood from consolidated lung; the mixture lowers aortic saturation (Guyton's example averages about 78%). COMLEX: This venous admixture is the same mechanism, on a larger scale, as the normal bronchial shunt that lowers PO2 from 104 to 95 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 1 — O2 transport and the shunt flow that lowers arterial PO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl15_ex1_q46_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Pulmonary abnormalities causing decreased O₂ saturation in pneumonia","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with bacterial pneumonia affecting 30% of lung parenchyma develops progressive hypoxemia (PO₂ declining from 70 to 52 mm Hg over 24 hours) despite stable chest X-ray. His physician considers that reduced respiratory membrane surface area may be contributing. How do fluid-filled alveoli impair O₂ uptake in regions that still contain some air?","choices":{"A":"The fluid physically blocks oxygen molecules from contacting the alveolar membrane","B":"Inflammatory cells in the fluid consume oxygen before it can diffuse to blood","C":"Fluid increases the diffusion distance between inspired air and capillary blood","D":"The alveolar walls dissolve in the inflammatory fluid, eliminating the membrane","E":"Fluid prevents hemoglobin from binding oxygen even when it reaches the capillaries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fluid-filled alveoli reduce gas exchange by increasing diffusion distance and effectively reducing functional respiratory membrane surface area. Normally, inspired air directly contacts the thin alveolar membrane (about 0.6 μm thick), with only a short diffusion distance to pulmonary capillary blood. In pneumonia, inflammatory exudate (fluid, cells, proteins, fibrin) fills alveolar spaces, creating a liquid barrier between inspired air and the alveolar membrane. Oxygen must now dissolve in and diffuse through this fluid layer before reaching the alveolar-capillary membrane, substantially increasing diffusion distance. Additionally, fluid-filled alveoli are functionally \"removed\" from gas exchange—they contribute little to oxygenation, effectively reducing available surface area from ~70 m² to ~50 m² (in 30% involvement). Some alveoli may be completely filled (no air contact at all), creating absolute shunt. Fick's law states gas diffusion is inversely proportional to distance and directly proportional to surface area, so increased distance and reduced functional area both impair oxygen uptake, causing progressive hypoxemia. Physical blocking of O₂ oversimplifies. O₂ consumption by inflammatory cells is incorrect—cells don't consume significant O₂. Dissolved alveolar walls is wrong—walls remain intact. Blocked hemoglobin binding incorrectly describes hemoglobin function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fluid in or around alveoli lengthens the diffusion path; Guyton lists edema fluid in the interstitial space and alveoli among the causes of reduced diffusion through the respiratory membrane, since rate falls as distance rises. COMLEX: FA — the Fick equation places thickness (Δx) in the denominator, the term that rises in edema and fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the factors, including membrane thickness, that set the diffusion rate","isHighYield":false}]};
